hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1","In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. May Allah send blessings upon our master Muhammad and his family and grant them peace. Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah ibn Salamah al-Tahawi al-Azdi, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Now to our topic: Allah, mighty and exalted, sent His Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as the seal of the prophets He had sent before him — the blessings of Allah be upon him and upon them, and His peace, mercy and benedictions — and He sent down upon him a Book that is the seal of the books He had sent down before it, watching over them and confirming them. In it He commanded those who believe in him not to raise their voices above his voice, and not to advance ahead of his command, and He informed them that He has taken charge of what he utters, by His saying, mighty and majestic: Nor does he speak from desire; it is nothing but revelation revealed. He commanded them to take what he brings them and to refrain from what he forbids them, by His saying, mighty and majestic: Whatever the Messenger gives you, take it, and whatever he forbids you, refrain from it. He forbade them to be with him as they are with one another, by His saying, exalted: And do not raise your voices to him as you raise your voices to one another. He warned them that if they did that, their deeds would be nullified while they perceived it not, and along with that He warned whoever opposes his command, by His saying, mighty and majestic: Let those who oppose his command beware, lest a trial befall them or a painful punishment befall them. Abu Ja'far said: I examined the reports narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with acceptable chains of transmission, conveyed by people of precision, trustworthiness and excellent delivery, and I found among them matters whose knowledge escapes most people, as does knowledge of their contents. So my heart inclined to reflect upon them, to clarify what I could of their difficulties, to extract the rulings contained in them, and to dispel impossibilities attributed to them, and to arrange that into chapters, mentioning in each chapter what Allah, mighty and majestic, grants me thereof, until I cover what I can of them in that manner, seeking thereby the reward of Allah, mighty and majestic. I ask Allah for success in that and help with it, for He is Generous, Noble, and He is sufficient for me, an excellent Guardian. I began it with what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded to begin a need with, as narrated from him with chains I shall mention after this, Allah willing, namely: All praise belongs to Allah; we praise Him, seek His help and seek His forgiveness. We seek refuge with Allah from the evils of our souls and the misdeeds of our works. Whomever Allah guides, none can misguide, and whomever He misguides, none can guide. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, alone, without partner, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. O you who believe, fear Allah as He deserves to be feared, and do not die except as Muslims. And fear Allah, by whom you ask one another, and the wombs; indeed Allah is ever watchful over you. And fear Allah and speak upright words; He will amend your deeds for you and forgive your sins, and whoever obeys Allah and His Messenger has attained a great triumph. The chains by which what we mentioned of the sermon of need was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are: 1. What al-Husayn ibn Nasr ibn al-Mu'arik al-Baghdadi Abu Ali narrated to us: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us: al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us the sermon of need, and he mentioned these very words."
"2","2. And what al-Husayn also narrated to us: Shababah ibn Sawwar narrated to us: al-Mas'udi informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught us, then he mentioned the like of it."
"3","3. And what Yazid ibn Sinan ibn Yazid al-Basri Abu Khalid narrated to us: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani and Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us, both saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abd Allah, who said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) used to teach us the sermon of need, then he mentioned these very words. Bishr added — Shu'bah said: Abu Ishaq informed us, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abu Ubaydah, from Abd Allah, with this hadith, and that this is the hadith of Abu Ubaydah. Abu Ja'far said: This, then, is what we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on this topic, from the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud. And there has also been narrated from Ibn Abbas something that falls under this topic:"
"4","4. What Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud and Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us, both saying: Muhammad ibn al-Salt al-Kufi narrated to us: Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Amr ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, who said: A man spoke to the Prophet (peace be upon him) about a need, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered him: All praise belongs to Allah; we praise Him and seek His help. Whomever Allah guides, none can misguide, and whomever He misguides, none can guide. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, alone, without partner, and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. Now to our topic. And there has been narrated from Nubayt ibn Sharit something that falls under this topic also:"
"5","5. What Fahd narrated to us: Abu Ghassan al-Nahdi narrated to us: Musa ibn Muhammad al-Ansari narrated to us: Abu Malik al-Ashja'i narrated to us, from Nubayt ibn Sharit, who said: I was riding behind my father on the rear of the mount while the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was delivering a sermon at Jamrat al-Aqabah, saying: All praise belongs to Allah; we seek His help and seek His forgiveness. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and that I am His servant and Messenger. Then he said: I counsel you to fear Allah. Then he (Abu Ja'far) said: So among the chapters after whose pattern I shall compose this book of mine, Allah willing, is what I shall now mention."
"6","Chapter 1: What has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) about the people who will suffer the severest punishment on the Day of Resurrection. 6. It is what Fahd narrated to us: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Asim, from Abu Wa'il, from Abd Allah, from the Prophet of Allah (peace be upon him), who said: The people who will suffer the severest punishment on the Day of Resurrection are a man who killed a prophet or whom a prophet killed, a leader of misguidance, and a mutilator among those who mutilate. Abu Ja'far said: By this we established who suffer the severest punishment on the Day of Resurrection — that they are people of these three categories — and in it is what rules out anyone among the punished besides them having a like thereof on that day. Yet we found in another hadith something that must be examined."
"7","7. It is what Yunus narrated to us, from Bishr ibn Bakr, from al-Awza'i, from Ibn Shihab: al-Qasim ibn Muhammad informed me, from A'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came in to me while I was screened by a curtain bearing images, so he tore it down, then said: The people who will suffer the severest punishment on the Day of Resurrection are those who imitate the creation of Allah, mighty and majestic. This hadith, then, states that the class mentioned in it suffers the severest punishment. If this is established, it contradicts the first; far be it from Allah that anything of that sort should run upon the tongue of His Messenger. So we examined it through other than this narration."
"8","8. We found that Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from al-Qasim, from A'ishah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Among the severest of people in punishment — and he mentioned it. By that we established that what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) in this hadith does not contradict what is in the first hadith, since one who imitates the creation of Allah is one who fashions likenesses of Allah's creation, and the class mentioned in this hadith is among the three classes mentioned in the first. Yet we found another hadith besides those two."
"9","9. It is what Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us: Shayban the grammarian informed us, from al-A'mash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from A'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: The severest of people in punishment on the Day of Resurrection is a man who lampooned a man, thereby lampooning the whole tribe. If what is in this stands as it is, it contradicts the first; far be it that the speech of the Messenger should differ about this or anything else. Yet it is possible that what is in this stems from the shortcoming of one of its narrators in retaining what came from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that. So we sought it in other than this narration."
"10","10. We found that Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Baghdadi narrated to us: Ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us (in one chain), and Ibrahim ibn Ahmad al-Wasiti narrated to us: Ibn Abi Saminah narrated to us — both saying: Jarir narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from A'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: The greatest of people before Allah in falsehood on the Day of Resurrection is a man who lampoons a whole tribe, or a man who disowns his father. By that we established that what the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) intended in this hadith was to mention lampooning as a grave falsehood before Allah, not to describe Allah's punishment of him for it as the severest punishment or otherwise among the kinds of punishment. Thus it contains nothing contradicting anything in the first. And among that:"
"11","Chapter 2: Clarifying what was difficult for us among what has been narrated from him (peace be upon him) about the last ten verses of Surah Al Imran which he recited on a night upon waking from sleep, and what was narrated from him about that. 11. Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him ... And what Isma'il al-Muzani narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Idris al-Shafi'i narrated to us, saying: Malik informed us, from Makhramah ibn Sulayman, from Kurayb, that Ibn Abbas informed him: that he spent the night at Maymunah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who was his maternal aunt. He said: I lay across the width of the pillow, while the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his wife lay along its length. He slept until, when midnight came — or a little before it, or a little after it — the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke and began wiping sleep from his face, then recited the last ten verses of Surah Al Imran. Then he went to a hanging waterskin, performed ablution from it and performed it well, then stood to pray. Ibn Abbas said: So I stood, did as he had done, then went and stood at his side. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed his right hand on my head and took my ear, twisting it. He prayed two rakahs, then two, then two, then two, then two, then two, then performed witr, then lay down until the caller to prayer came to him. He prayed two light rakahs, then went out and prayed the dawn prayer. By this hadith we could not establish the beginning of the ten verses the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited, so we needed to establish their exact identity, since the reciters of Madinah and of Kufah hold that the first of them is His saying: Those who remember Allah standing and sitting, while the reciters of Sham count them from: Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth. So we sought the truth of that."
"12","12. We found that Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, saying: Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Asadi narrated to us; and we found Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, saying: Shabababah ibn Sawwar narrated to us; and we found Fahd narrated to us, saying: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us — all saying: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Ali ibn Abd Allah ibn Abbas, from his father, who said: Al-Abbas ordered me to spend the night with the household of the Messenger of Allah that night, and instructed me not to sleep until I had memorized for him the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). He said: So I prayed the evening prayer with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and when he finished his prayer and the people left, no one remained in the mosque but me. The Prophet said: Who is this? He said: Is it Abd Allah? I said: Yes. He said: What brings you? I said: Al-Abbas ordered me to spend the night with you tonight. He said: Then join us. He said: So I entered with the Prophet (peace be upon him), and he said: Spread bedding for Abd Allah. I was brought a cushion of sackcloth stuffed with palm fiber. He slept until I heard his snoring or heavy breathing, then he sat upright on his bed, raised his head to the sky and said: Glory be to Allah, the Most Holy, three times, and recited this verse from the end of Surah Al Imran: Indeed, in the creation, until he sealed the surah."
"13","13. We found that Ahmad ibn Dawud al-Basri narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Husayn, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Muhammad ibn Ali: my father informed me, from Ibn Abbas, who said: I spent the night at the Prophet (peace be upon him). He stood, took his toothstick, then performed ablution, then raised his head to the sky saying: Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth — the verse. Then he prayed two rakahs, lengthening the standing, bowing and prostration in them. Then he slept until he snored. Then he stood, took the toothstick and used it, then raised his head to the sky and said: Indeed, in the creation of the heavens and the earth, to the end of the verse. He did that three times, then stood and performed witr with three rakahs."
"14","14. We found that Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us, saying: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us: Hushaym narrated to us: Husayn informed us, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain. By this hadith we established that the first of the ten verses from the end of Surah Al Imran is as in the counting of the Syrians, with Ibn Abbas agreeing with them on that. Then we found in the hadith of Kurayb, from the narration of Ibn Ishaq, agreement with what is in the hadith of Ali ibn Abd Allah. [Note: the source text of this row ends abruptly here.]"
"15","15. As Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Baghdadi, known as Ibn al-Imam, narrated to us: Ubayd Allah ibn Sa'd al-Zuhri narrated to us: my uncle Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl and Muhammad ibn al-Walid ibn Nuwayfi', freedman of the family of al-Zubayr — both narrated to me from Kurayb, from Ibn Abbas, who said: My father al-Abbas sent me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to memorize his prayer for him. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) woke in the night and gazed with his eyes toward the sky, then recited these verses from Surah Al Imran: Indeed, in the creation of the heavens, until he reached ten of them. Then he returned to his bed and slept. Then he woke and did as he had done the first time. Then he mentioned the rest of the hadith. Thus what Kurayb narrated from Ibn Abbas, in what we mentioned, returns to agreement with what Ali ibn Abd Allah narrated from Ibn Abbas from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as we described. We ask Allah for success. And among that:"
"16","Chapter 3: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from him about what is said in the evening, such that the sting of a venomous creature does not harm its sayer until morning. Abu Ja'far said: Among what was narrated about that is the hadith of Abu Salih al-Samman, which his son Suhayl narrated from him, about which there was disagreement over whom he mentioned in its chain after his father — some narrated it from him as Abu Hurayrah. 16. As Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Malik informed us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah: that a man from Aslam said: I did not sleep tonight. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: From what? He said: A scorpion stung me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had you said in the evening: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, it would not have harmed you, Allah willing."
"17","17. Among that is what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and said: I was stung last night and did not sleep until morning. He said to him: Had you said in the evening: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, the sting of a scorpion would not have harmed you until morning."
"18","18. Among that is what Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah — the like of the hadith of Yunus which we narrated in this chapter, except that he did not say in it: Allah willing. And the like of the hadith of Malik."
"19","19. Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb Abu Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, saying: I read to Luwayn, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah: that a man among the companions of the Prophet (peace be upon him) was stung, and suffered from it as Allah willed. That reached the Prophet, and he said: Had he said: I seek refuge in the complete word of Allah from the evil of what He created, three times, it would not have harmed him."
"20","20. Among that is what Ahmad also narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak informed us: Yazid informed us: Hisham informed us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever says in the evening three times: I seek refuge in the complete word of Allah from the evil of what He created, no sting shall harm him that night."
"21","21. Among that is what Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet — the like of it — and he said in it: three times."
"22","22. Among that is what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Uthman al-Uqayli informed us: Abd al-A'la — meaning al-Sami — narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah: that a man among the companions of the Prophet was absent from him one night, and he asked about him. When morning came he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: What kept you? He said: O Messenger of Allah, a scorpion stung me. He said: Had you said in the evening: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, three times, it would not have harmed you."
"23","23. Among that is what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Yusuf al-Kufi informed us: al-Ashja'i narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: A scorpion stung a man, and he came to the Prophet and informed him. He said to him: Had you said in the evening: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, nothing would have befallen you. Except that al-Ashja'i was contradicted regarding Sufyan in part of the chain of this hadith: he was told, in place of Abu Hurayrah: from a man from Aslam — and we shall mention it in the remainder of this chapter. And among that is one who narrated this hadith from Suhayl, from a man from Aslam."
"24","24. As Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Suhayl — he heard his father informing him — from a man from Aslam, who said: I was with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man from the Ansar came to him and said: I was stung last night and did not sleep until morning. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had you said in the evening: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, it would not have harmed you, Allah willing."
"25","25. As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from a man from Aslam, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), that he said: Whoever says in the evening: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, three times, no venom shall harm him that night."
"26","26. As Fahd narrated to us: Abu Ghassan narrated to us: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from a man from Aslam, who said: I was sitting with the Prophet (peace be upon him) when a man among his companions came and said: I was stung last night. Then he mentioned the like of it."
"27","27. As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from a man from Aslam, from the Prophet ... then he mentioned the like of it. Asad ibn Musa narrated this hadith from Shu'bah, from Suhayl and his brother, from their father, from a man from Aslam."
"28","28. As al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us: Asad narrated to us. And Yunus narrated to us: Asad narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Suhayl and his brother, from their father, from a man from Aslam: that he was stung and came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) ... then he mentioned the like of it. Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated this hadith from Suhayl, differing with all of them in its chain."
"29","29. As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Mansur informed us: Habban informed us: Wuhayb narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from a man from Aslam ... then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: Since they differed before us in the chain of this hadith from Suhayl, as we have narrated their disagreement over him in this chapter, we sought it apart from the narration of Suhayl, from the hadith of one who narrated it from Abu Salih other than him and other than his brother, so as to establish its truth: is it from Abu Hurayrah, or from a man from Aslam?"
"30","30. We found that Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from his father and Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ya'qub ibn Abd Allah — meaning Ibn al-Ashajj — from al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim, from Dhakwan Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, that he said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, what I suffered from a scorpion that stung me last night! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Had you said in the evening: I seek refuge in the words of Allah from the evil of what He created, it would not have harmed you."
"31","31. We found that Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb narrated to us the like of it."
"32","32. We found that al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, saying: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us: al-Layth informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Ja'far, from Ya'qub, that he mentioned to him that Abu Salih, freedman of Ghatafan, informed that he heard Abu Hurayrah saying: A man said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): A scorpion stung me. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Had you said in the evening: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, it would not have harmed you. In this hadith he attributed Abu Salih in his clientage to Ghatafan, though he was contradicted in that. Muhammad ibn Sa'd, the secretary of al-Waqidi, mentioned in his book on the classes, saying: Abu Salih al-Samman was the freedman of Juwayriyah, a woman from Qays. He (Abu Ja'far) said: We had mentioned in this chapter that al-Ashja'i was contradicted regarding Sufyan in his chaining the hadith of Suhayl from his father from Abu Hurayrah, which we narrated above; the one who contradicted him regarding Sufyan is Muhammad ibn Yusuf."
"33","33. As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Mansur informed us: Muhammad ibn Yusuf informed us, from Sufyan, from Suhayl, from his father, from a man from Aslam — then he mentioned the like of the hadith of al-Ashja'i. This hadith was also narrated from Abu Hurayrah by other than Abu Salih al-Samman, namely Tariq ibn Mukhashin."
"34","34. As Fahd narrated to us: Zayd ibn Abd Allah narrated to us: Baqiyyah narrated to us: al-Zubaydi narrated to me, from al-Zuhri, from Tariq ibn Mukhashin, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace be upon him): that a stung person stung by a scorpion was brought to him, and he said: Had he said: I seek refuge in the complete word of Allah from the evil of what He created, he would not have been stung, or it would not have harmed him. Since we found it from the narration of al-Qa'qa', from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah — not from a man from Aslam — it became strong in our hearts that the origin of this hadith is from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, not from a man from Aslam. Once these narrations in it were authenticated, what is in this hadith returns to this: that the sayer of these guarded words is protected by saying them until that night in which he said them ends, with no addition thereto. Yet we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) what extends beyond that, in the time its sayer is protected by them, what is in that hadith."
"35","35. It is what Yunus and Bahr narrated to us, both saying: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib and al-Harith ibn Ya'qub, from Ya'qub ibn Abd Allah al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, from Khawlah bint Hakim al-Sulamiyyah, that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) saying: When one of you alights at a dwelling, let him say: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created; then nothing shall harm him until he departs from it."
"36","36. And what Yunus narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Dimashqi narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us; and as al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us: Shu'ayb narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from al-Harith ibn Ya'qub: that Ya'qub ibn Abd Allah narrated to him: that he heard Busr ibn Sa'id saying: I heard Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas saying: I heard Khawlah bint Hakim al-Sulamiyyah saying: that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) saying: Whoever alights at a dwelling and says: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, nothing shall harm him until he departs from that dwelling of his."
"37","37. And what Nasr ibn Marzuq narrated to us: al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us: Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us: Ibn Ajlan narrated to us, from Ya'qub ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Sa'd ibn Malik, from Khawlah bint Hakim, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If one of you, upon alighting at a dwelling, said: I seek refuge in the perfect words of Allah from the evil of what He created, nothing in that dwelling would harm him until he departs from it. Thus Muhammad ibn Ajlan differed with al-Harith ibn Ya'qub and Yazid ibn Abi Habib over who comes after Ya'qub in the chain of this hadith: he said: from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, in place of al-Harith's saying in it: from Busr ibn Sa'id. There was nothing in these two narrations we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) specifying the time during which the sayer of these words is protected by them — far be it from Allah that there be any contradiction in them. Rather, their reconciliation is that what is in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah concerns one who is resident in his dwelling, not traveling, while what is in the hadith of Khawlah concerns one who is traveling. The traveler is lightened for on account of travel: a portion of his prayer is lifted from him, he is lightened in his obligatory fasting — permitted to delay it until he leaves his journey and returns to his homeland — while the resident is not so. These words we mentioned avail the traveler in warding off for a wider time than the time in which they ward off for the resident, given the lightening granted the traveler in his journey, which the resident has no like of in his residence. We ask Allah for success."
"38","Chapter 4: Clarifying what was difficult for us among what has been narrated from him (peace be upon him) of his forbidding taking riding animals as sitting places, and his forbidding taking them as chairs. 38. As Muhammad ibn Sinan ibn Sarh al-Shayzari Abu Ja'far narrated to us: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah narrated to us: Baqiyyah and Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us: al-Awza'i narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Amr al-Saybani narrated to me, from Abu Maryam, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), who said: Beware of taking the backs of your riding animals as pulpits, for Allah only subjected them to you to convey you to a land you would not reach except with great hardship, and He made the earth for you — so fulfill your needs upon it."
"39","39. As Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Sa'igh narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, and he mentioned it with its chain. [Note: the source text of this row is abbreviated here, referring to the previous hadith with its chain.]"
"40","40. As al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: I heard al-Layth saying: Sahl ibn Mu'adh al-Juhani narrated to me, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: Ride these animals sound, or leave them sound, and do not take them as chairs. Abu Ja'far said: From these two we established his forbidding what he forbade therein, along with our knowledge of his sitting on the back of his mount to deliver a sermon upon it on the day of Arafah at Arafah, and on the day of sacrifice."
"41","41. As al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from his father, from Jabir, in his hadith about the pilgrimage of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him): that when the sun declined on the day of Arafah in his pilgrimage, he ordered al-Qaswa', which was saddled for him. He rode until he reached the bottom of the valley, then addressed the people, saying: Your blood and your wealth are inviolable to you, like the inviolability of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours. Verily, everything of the affair of pre-Islamic ignorance is placed beneath my two feet; the blood of pre-Islamic ignorance is abolished. The first blood I abolish of our blood is the blood of the son of Rabi'ah ibn al-Harith — he was being nursed among Banu Sa'd, and Hudhayl killed him. The usury of pre-Islamic ignorance is abolished, and the first usury I abolish is the usury of al-Abbas — all of it is abolished. Fear Allah concerning women, for you took them in trust from Allah, and made their private parts lawful by the word of Allah. You have over them that they not allow anyone you dislike to tread your beds; if they do that, strike them with a striking that is not severe. I have left among you what you shall never go astray after: the Book of Allah. You shall be asked about me — what will you say? They said: We bear witness that you conveyed, fulfilled, and counseled. He pointed with his forefinger, raising it to the sky, gesturing it toward the people: O Allah, bear witness; O Allah, bear witness; O Allah, bear witness. Then Bilal called the prayer."
"42","42. As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir and Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, both saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Murrah ibn Sharahil, who said: A man among the companions of the Prophet (peace be upon him) narrated to me — and I think he said: in this Arafah of mine — saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) stood among us upon a red she-camel with a slit ear. Ya'qub said in his hadith: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us upon a red she-camel, saying: Do you know what day this is? They said: Yes, the day of sacrifice. He said: You are right — the day of the greater pilgrimage. He said: Do you know what month this is? They said: Yes, Dhu al-Hijjah. He said: You are right — the deaf month of Allah. Then he said: Do you know what land this is? They said: Yes, the sacred place. He said: You are right. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your blood and your wealth — and I think he said: and your honor — are inviolable to you, like the inviolability of this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours — or he said: like the inviolability of this day of yours, this month of yours, and this land of yours. Verily I precede you at the Basin, awaiting you, and I shall vie in numbers with the nations — or the people — through you, so do not blacken my face. Verily I shall rescue men, and others shall be rescued away from me, and I shall say: My companions! And it shall be said: You do not know what they innovated after you. You have seen me, you have heard from me, and you shall be asked about me; whoever lies about me, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"43","43. Among that is what Ali ibn Ma'bad ibn Nuh narrated to us: Hawdhah ibn Khalifah narrated to us: Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Ibn Sirin, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakrah, from his father, who said: When that day came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rode his she-camel, then halted and said: Do you know what day this is? We fell silent until we thought he would call it by other than its name. Then he said: Is it not the day of sacrifice? We said: Yes. Then he said: Do you know what month this is? We fell silent until we thought he would call it by other than its name. Then he said: Is it not Dhu al-Hijjah? They said: Yes. He said: Do you know what land this is? We fell silent until we thought he would call it by other than its name. Then he said: Is it not the sacred land? We said: Yes. He said: Your wealth, your honor and your blood are inviolable among you, as in this day of yours, in this month of yours, in this land of yours. Let the witness convey to the absent, for many a one conveyed to retains better than the conveyor. Then he inclined on his she-camel toward some small sheep, and began distributing them: one sheep between two men, and one sheep between three. He mentioned the hadith of Abu Bakrah, in which: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) rode his she-camel. Abu Ja'far said: His delivering a sermon upon his mount was sitting upon it in that act. Far be it from Allah that there was in his deed anything contradicting what was in his speech which we mentioned from him in the two hadiths whose mention we advanced. Rather, what he mentioned in those two hadiths was his forbidding sitting on the backs of animals for idle talk upon them, which the sitter has no need of, and in which sitting upon them has no advantage over sitting on the ground; and if his sitting on its back for that has an advantage that no necessity called him to, in that is tiring it for no necessity that called him to it. His sitting to address the people upon it, and to make them hear his command and prohibition — which could not be achieved likewise sitting on the ground, since sitting on the ground his command and prohibition would not be heard as they are heard while he is on his mount — was a sermon upon its back called for by necessity. What is in the first two hadiths of his forbidding what he forbade therein is only a forbidding of sitting on its back that no necessity called for. Each of what is in the two hadiths and what is in his sermon on his mount thus falls under a meaning other than the meaning under which its counterpart falls, ruling out any contradiction in that. Abu Ja'far said: A questioner asked about Mu'adh mentioned in one of the two hadiths — Mu'adh ibn Anas al-Juhani — saying: Is companionship established for him with you, making it obligatory to admit his hadith which you narrated from him in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), as you admitted therein the hadith of Abu Hurayrah which you narrated from him therein, due to companionship of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)? It was said to him: Yes; we have established his companionship of him and his narration from him."
"44","44. It is what Fahd narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Hiql narrated to me, from al-Awza'i, from Asid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Farwah ibn Mujahid, from Sahl ibn Mu'adh, from his father, who said: We went on campaign with the Prophet (peace be upon him), and the people constricted the halting places and cut off the roads. That reached the Prophet (peace be upon him), and he said: Whoever cuts off a road or constricts a halting place has no striving in the path of Allah."
"45","45. And what Muhammad ibn Sinan narrated to us: Isa ibn Sulayman narrated to us: Ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asid ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Farwah ibn Mujahid, from Sahl ibn Mu'adh al-Juhani, who said: I campaigned with my father in the summer campaign in the days of Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, and the people constricted the halting places and cut off the roads. My father stood among the people and said: O people, I campaigned with the Prophet (peace be upon him) in such-and-such campaign, and the people constricted the halting places and cut off the roads, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a caller proclaiming: Whoever constricts a halting place or cuts off a road has no striving in the path of Allah. By that we established that Mu'adh al-Juhani had companionship of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), campaigning with him, and narration from him, as we mentioned in this hadith. I heard Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud saying: Most of the hadith of this Mu'adh in people's hands is what the Egyptians narrated from him, and nothing of that indicates his companionship of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him); what we found indicating that is what the Syrians narrated from him, despite the paucity of their narration from him. Abu Ja'far said: Abu Ubayd said, in what Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz licensed to us from him: al-mukhadramah means: with slit ear — and others denied that of him, among them Abbas al-Riyashi, in what Ibrahim ibn Humayd narrated to me from him, who said: It is impossible with us that the Prophet (peace be upon him) addressed upon a she-camel of that description, for it is docked; rather, it is a she-camel born between Arabian and Yemeni stock, so called mukhadramah for that, as one born in pre-Islamic ignorance who reached Islam is called mukhadram — i.e., for attaining both ends together. I heard Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi saying: I heard Yahya ibn Ma'in saying to Ahmad ibn Hanbal at the door of Affan: O Abu Abd Allah, if it pleases you to write from a man about whom there is nothing in your heart, then write from Abu Ghassan Malik ibn Isma'il."
"46","Chapter 5: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from the Prophet (peace be upon him) of his forbidding Abu Dharr to undertake judging between two men and to take custody of a trust. 46. Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that Darraj Abu al-Samh narrated to him, from Abu al-Muthanna, from Abu Dharr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to me for six days: Take heed, O Abu Dharr, to what I say to you. Then on the seventh day he said: I counsel you to fear Allah in your private and public affairs; if you do evil, do good after it; do not ask anyone for anything, even if your whip falls; do not take custody of a trust, do not take custody of an orphan, and do not judge between two men. This hadith contains his forbidding Abu Dharr what he forbade him — while he (peace be upon him) had employed Ali ibn Abi Talib as judge. By that we understood that he did not employ him in a disliked work, nor admit him into a matter diminishing his rank from what it is; rather, he admitted him only into a matter increasing his rank, and a matter bringing him nearer to his Lord, exalted. There was narrated, of what passed from him to Ali in that when he sent him upon what he appointed him over, among it:"
"47","47. What Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us: Shayban the grammarian narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Habashi, from Ali, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Yemen, and I said: O Messenger of Allah, you send me to a people of elders of age, and I fear I may not get it right. He said: Allah shall make your tongue firm and guide your heart."
"48","48. And what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us: Sharik, Za'idah and Sulayman ibn Mu'adh narrated to us — all from Simak ibn Harb, from Hanash — he is Ibn al-Mu'tamir — from Ali, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to me: When two litigants come to you, do not judge for the first until you hear what the other says; for when you have heard that, you will know how to judge. Ali said: I never ceased being a judge thereafter. Sulayman added that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said to Ali in this hadith: Allah shall make your tongue firm and guide your heart."
"49","49. And what Fahd narrated to us: Abu Ghassan al-Nahdi narrated to us: Isra'il ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Harithah ibn Mudarrib, from Ali, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Yemen, and I said: You sent me to a people older than me — how shall I judge? He said: Go, for Allah shall guide your heart and make your tongue firm."
"50","50. And what Fahd narrated to us: Ibn al-Asbahani Muhammad ibn Sa'id narrated to us: Sharik narrated to us, from Simak, from Hanash, who said: Ali said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to Yemen while I was young in age, and I said: You sent me while I am young, with no knowledge of judging. He said: Allah shall guide your heart and your tongue; when the two disputants sit before you, do not judge for the first until you hear the speech of the other. He said: I never doubted in a judgment thereafter. Abu Ja'far said: It is impossible with us — and Allah knows best — that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) admitted Ali into anything but what increased his rank, the eminence of his station, and what brings him nearer to his Lord, exalted. Among what confirms what we mentioned:"
"51","51. What Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari and Bakr ibn Idris al-Azdi narrated to us, both saying: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us: Haywah ibn Shurayh narrated to us: Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn Usamah ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from Abu Qays, freedman of Amr ibn al-As, from Amr: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: When the judge judges, striving, then gets it right, he has two rewards; and when he judges, striving, then errs, he has one reward. He said: So I narrated this hadith to Abu Bakr ibn Hazm, who said: Thus Abu Salamah narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah."
"52","52. And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah and Fahd narrated to us, both saying: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me: Abu al-Zinad narrated to me, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain."
"53","53. And what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb informed us: Ishaq ibn Mansur — meaning al-Kawsaj — informed us: Abd al-Razzaq informed us: Ma'mar informed us, from Sufyan, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Abu Bakr ibn Amr ibn Hazm, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace be upon him) the like of it."
"54","54. And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Asbagh ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated to us: Sharik narrated to me, from al-A'mash, from Sa'd ibn Ubaydah, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), who said: Judges are three: two judges in the Fire, and one judge in Paradise: a judge who abandoned the truth knowingly, and a judge who judged contrary to the truth unknowingly, destroying people's rights — these two are in the Fire; and a judge who judged by the truth — he is in Paradise."
"55","55. And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us; and what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us — both saying: Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us: Abu Hashim narrated to us, saying: Were it not for the hadith of Ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), that he said: Judges are three: two in the Fire and one in Paradise: a man who knew the truth and judged by it — he is in Paradise; a man who knew the truth but did not judge by it and acted unjustly in judgment — he is in the Fire; and a man who did not know the truth and judged for people in ignorance — he is in the Fire. [We would have said: the judge who strives bears nothing.] Abu Ja'far said: Do you not see what is in judging of what may be a cause of Paradise? That is proof of its eminent worth, and that the Prophet (peace be upon him) did not withhold Abu Dharr from it on account of judging itself, but for another reason. So we sought what that reason is."
"56","56. We found that Yazid ibn Sinan, Ali ibn Shaybah, Ibrahim ibn Munqidh al-Usfuri and Musa ibn al-Nu'man al-Makki narrated to us, from al-Muqri, from Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub — he narrated to me — from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ja'far al-Qurashi, from Salim ibn Abi Salim al-Jayshani, from his father, from Abu Dharr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abu Dharr, I love for you what I love for myself; I see you as weak — so do not take command over two men, nor take charge of an orphan's wealth. By this hadith we established the reason for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade Abu Dharr what he forbade him in the first hadith: a quality in him by which he fell short of the rank of judging — the opposite of what was in Ali ibn Abi Talib, by which he deserved appointment to judging."
"57","57. We found that Yusuf ibn Yazid ibn Kamil al-Qurashi narrated to us, saying: Abu Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Bakr ibn Amr, from al-Harith ibn Yazid al-Hadrami, from Ibn Hujayrah al-Akbar, from Abu Dharr, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, will you not employ me? He said: He struck my shoulder with his hand, then said: O Abu Dharr, you are weak; it is a trust, and on the Day of Resurrection it is disgrace and regret, except for one who took it by its right and discharged what was due upon him therein. By this too we established the reason for which the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) disliked for Abu Dharr what he disliked for him in the first hadith. By the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) we mentioned from him in this hadith — Except for one who took it by its right and discharged what was due upon him therein — we established that whoever is so is not subject to any forbidding in that, nor any dislike; the dislike thereof attaches only to those who expose themselves to it, seeking its appointment. Among what was narrated confirming this meaning:"
"58","58. What Abu Bakrah narrated to us: Husayn ibn Hafs al-Asbahani narrated to us: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us, from Isma'il — he is Ibn Abi Khalid — from his brother, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, who said: Two men of the Ash'aris came to the Prophet (peace be upon him); they spoke, then sought office. He said: The most treacherous of you with me is one who seeks it. Upon you both be the fear of Allah, exalted."
"59","59. And what Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Qasim al-Kufi Abu al-Husayn narrated to us: Isma'il Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Samurah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to me: O Abd al-Rahman, do not ask for leadership; if you are given it upon asking, you are left to it, and if you are given it without asking, you are aided in it. Abu Ja'far said: In what I mentioned is what clarifies all we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) in this chapter: the first hadith we narrated from him, containing his forbidding Abu Dharr what he forbade him; and the hadiths we narrated after it, containing the lifting of that forbidding from others possessed of strength for what they undertake thereof. By what we mentioned it is clear there is no contradiction in anything we mentioned in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that its meanings stand clear, coherent, evident to their beholder, as we mentioned therein. We ask Allah for success."
"60","Chapter 6: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated about the occasion in which was revealed: And it is He who withheld their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of Makkah, after He gave you victory over them — which some people thought the narrations from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) contradicted one another about, though in truth it is otherwise. 60. Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah informed us; and Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Thabit, from Anas: that eighty men of the people of Makkah descended upon the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and his companions at al-Tan'im at the dawn prayer, to kill them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seized them peacefully and set them free, whereupon Allah, exalted, sent down: And it is He who withheld their hands from you — the verse. Abu Salamah said: I narrated this hadith to al-Kalbi, who said: Such was the hadith."
"61","61. Muhammad ibn Ja'far ibn A'yan narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us: Abd al-Razzaq informed us; and Ubayd ibn Muhammad ibn Musa al-Bazzaz Abu al-Qasim narrated to us — this Muhammad being known as Rijal — : Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us. Then they concurred, each one of them saying in turn: Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, saying: Urwah informed me, from al-Miswar and Marwan ibn al-Hakam, each confirming the other — saying, in the hadith of the truce: Suhayl, among what he stipulated in the peace between him and him in the year of al-Hudaybiyyah, was: no man of ours shall come to you, even if upon your religion, but you shall return him to us. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned to Madinah, and Abu Basir, a man of Quraysh, came — a Muslim. They sent two men seeking him, saying: The covenant you made for us. So he handed him to the two men. They set out with him, and when they reached Dhu al-Hulayfah they alighted, eating of their dates. Abu Basir said to one of the two men: By Allah, I see your sword, O so-and-so, is fine. The other drew it, saying: Yes, by Allah, it is fine. Abu Basir said: Show me, let me look at it. He struck him with it until he grew cold, and the other fled until he reached Madinah and entered the mosque. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said when he saw him: This one has seen terror. When he reached him he said: By Allah my companion was killed, and I am to be killed. Then Abu Basir came and said: O Prophet of Allah, by Allah Allah has discharged your liability — you returned me to them, then Allah saved me from them. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Woe to his mother — a kindler of war, had he supporters! When he heard that from him, he knew he would return him to them, so he left until he reached the seashore — meaning the sea. Abu Jandal escaped from them and joined Abu Basir, and no man of Quraysh who had embraced Islam set out but joined Abu Basir, until a band gathered of them. He said: By Allah, they heard of no camel caravan of Quraysh setting out for Sham but intercepted it, killing them and taking their wealth. Quraysh sent to the Prophet (peace be upon him), beseeching him by Allah and kinship to send to them: whoever comes to him is safe. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to them, and Allah sent down: And it is He who withheld their hands from you and your hands from them, until He reached: the fervor, the fervor of pre-Islamic ignorance. Their fervor was that they did not affirm that he is the Prophet of Allah, nor affirm: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, and they barred him from the House. Abu Ja'far said: What is in the hadith of Anas — that eighty men of Makkah descended upon the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and his companions from al-Tan'im at the dawn prayer to kill them — and that the occasion of this verse's revelation was in that; while what is in that hadith is attributed to Anas, not as a report from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he told him that. What is in the hadith of al-Miswar and Marwan is that its revelation was in what happened with Abu Basir, Abu Jandal, and those Muslims of Quraysh who joined them at the seashore, in their cutting off the Quraysh caravans and other provisions passing by them, until Quraysh in Makkah asked the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), beseeching him by Allah and kinship to send to them: whoever comes to him is safe — and that Allah's sending down this verse we recited was in that. Each aspect in these two hadiths is attributed to its narrators, not to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). By that it is clear there is no contradiction in anything in these two hadiths from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him); the contradiction therein about the occasion of this verse's revelation is from those below him (peace be upon him). There has also been narrated from Salamah ibn al-Akwa' about its revelation something indicating what Anas said — that its revelation was therein."
"62","62. As Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi informed us: Ikrimah ibn Ammar al-Ijli narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah, from his father Salamah, who said: My uncle came with a man of the Abalat and his horse, in armor, among seventy of the polytheists, until he halted with them before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: Let them be, that the upper hand and the glory may be ours. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pardoned them, and Allah sent down: And it is He who withheld their hands from you and your hands from them — the verse. Abu Ja'far said: Then we reflected afterwards upon what they said about that, and found in the verse we recited what indicates what Anas said about the occasion in which it was revealed — not what Marwan and al-Miswar said about that — for in it is: And it is He who withheld their hands from you and your hands from them in the valley of Makkah, after He gave you victory over them — the verse. Al-Tan'im is part of Makkah, while the seashore is not part of the valley of Makkah; and what occurred in that, in the hadith of Anas, was victory over the people who attempted what they attempted against the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and his companions — while there is no victory in the hadith of al-Miswar and Marwan. And among that:"
"63","Chapter 7: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), then from Ibn Abbas — with encompassing knowledge that he said it only by taking it from him, since the like of it is found only from him, nor is it attained by opinion, derivation or extraction — about the nine signs given to Musa (peace be upon him). 63. Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Musaddad ibn Musarhad narrated to us: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Shu'bah: Amr ibn Murrah narrated to me, from Abd Allah ibn Salamah, from Safwan ibn Assal, who said: A man among the Jews said to another: Take us to this Prophet. The other said to him: Do not say: this Prophet — for if he hears it, he shall have four eyes. They went to him and asked him about nine clear signs. He said: Worship Allah, associating nothing with Him; do not kill the soul Allah has forbidden except by right; do not commit adultery; do not steal; do not flee from battle; do not practice sorcery; do not consume usury; do not walk with an innocent man to a ruler; and upon you, O Jews: do not transgress the Sabbath. They said: We bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah. Abu Ja'far said: This phrase — We bear witness that you are the Messenger of Allah — none among Shu'bah's companions said it in this hadith except Yahya ibn Sa'id. This hadith thus states that the nine signs Allah gave Musa are the nine mentioned in this hadith, and that they are acts of worship — not warnings, frightenings or threatenings. We know of none among those who narrated this hadith from Shu'bah who precisely fixed the nine mentioned in it except Yahya. Some thought that Abd Allah ibn Idris al-Awdi also fixed them from Shu'bah, with Yahya's fixing from him. He mentioned about that:"
"64","64. What Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Abu Kurayb informed us, from Ibn Idris: Shu'bah informed us, from Amr, from Abd Allah, from Safwan, who said: A Jew said to his companion: Take us to this Prophet. His companion said: Do not say: Prophet — were he to hear it, he would have four eyes. They came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him about nine clear signs. He said to them: Do not associate anything with Allah; do not steal; do not commit adultery; do not kill the soul Allah has forbidden except by right; do not walk with an innocent man to a ruler; do not practice sorcery; do not consume usury; do not slander the chaste woman; do not turn away on the day of battle; and upon you especially, O Jews: do not transgress the Sabbath. They kissed his hands and his feet and said: We bear witness that you are a Prophet. He said: What prevents you from following me? They said: Dawud prayed that there should ever remain a Prophet among his offspring, and we fear that if we follow you, the Jews will kill us. Thus Ibn Shu'ayb narrated it to us — without doubt from him in it, nor from any who narrated it from him, nor from any above him among its narrators. What this supposer supposed was contrary to what he supposed; had it been as he supposed, Ibn Idris would have added over Yahya ibn Sa'id therein another sign, making what is in it ten signs — while what Allah informed that He gave Musa thereof is nine signs, not ten. The truth of this addition in it from Abd Allah over Yahya is only that Shu'bah had later doubted in it, not knowing: is among the signs in it turning away on the day of battle, or slandering the chaste woman? He narrated it thus until he died, while Yahya's hearing it from him without doubt was before that. The proof of what we mentioned:"
"65","65. That Abd al-Aziz ibn Mu'awiyah ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Attabi informed us — Khalid, Ibrahim ibn Marzuq, Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abd Allah ibn Salamah, from Safwan ibn Assal: that a Jew said to his companion: Come, let us ask this Prophet. The other said: Do not call him the Prophet — for if he hears it, he gets four eyes. They came to him and asked him about this verse: And We gave Musa nine clear signs. He said: Do not associate anything with Allah; do not kill the soul Allah has forbidden except by right; do not steal; do not commit adultery; do not practice sorcery; do not consume usury; do not walk with an innocent man to a ruler to have him killed; do not slander the chaste woman, or flee from battle; and upon you especially, O Jews: do not transgress the Sabbath. He said: They kissed his hand and said: We bear witness that you are a Prophet. He said: What prevents you from following me? They said: Dawud prayed that there should ever remain a Prophet in his offspring, and we fear that if we follow you, the Jews will kill us."
"66","Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Abu Dawud, author of the Tayalisi collection, narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us — then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Abu al-Walid, with the doubt contained in it."
"67","Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, saying: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us — adding that the doubt was Shu'bah's. By that we understood Yahya ibn Sa'id's singularity in this hadith from Shu'bah, free of doubt therein — apart from Ibn Idris and apart from any besides him among those who narrated it from Shu'bah whom we mentioned in this chapter. This is what we found about these verses from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). The place in which doubt occurred thereof is a place whose benefit must be established: it indicated that Allah's ruling, exalted, forbidding flight from battle, was among what He bound His Prophet Musa (peace be upon him) to, and what He never abrogated thereafter, until it became part of the law of our Prophet — and in that is a rebuttal of one who said that Allah's saying, exalted: Whoever turns his back to them on that day — the verse He mentioned in Surah al-Anfal — was only about the day of Badr in particular, and that its ruling does not extend beyond it. As for what we mentioned as narrated from Ibn Abbas in its interpretation and in the nine signs mentioned therein:"
"68","Yahya ibn Uthman narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud al-Harrani Abu Salih narrated to us: Attab ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, about His saying: nine clear signs — saying: the hand, the staff, the flood, the locusts, the lice, the frogs, the blood, the years of drought, and deficiency of fruits. Yet we examined what was narrated from Ibn Abbas about that apart from the path of Ikrimah his freedman, and found from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from him, about that, what he mentioned in his hadith on the trials."
"69","66. As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: al-Asbagh ibn Zayd informed us: al-Qasim ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Jubayr narrated to me, saying: I asked Ibn Abbas about His saying, exalted, to Musa: And We tried you with trials. I asked him: what are the trials? He said: Begin the day anew, O Ibn Jubayr, for it has a long account. In the morning I went early to him to claim what he had promised me, and he mentioned from him what he mentioned in his hadith, until he mentioned Musa's saying to Pharaoh: I want you to believe in Allah, exalted, and send the Children of Israel with me; and that Pharaoh refused him that, saying: Bring a sign if you are truthful. So he cast his staff, and behold, a huge serpent, mouth agape, heading swiftly toward Pharaoh. When Pharaoh saw it heading toward him he feared it, leapt from his throne, and implored Musa to withhold it from him — and he did. Then he drew his hand from his bosom, and it was white without harm, without leprosy; then he returned it and it reverted to its first color. Then he carried the account until he reached Musa's enduring Pharaoh's lying promises: each time he brought him a sign, he promised thereby to send the Children of Israel with him, and when it passed he broke his promise, saying: Can your Lord do aught but this? So Allah sent upon him and his people the flood, the locusts, the lice, the frogs and the blood — distinct signs. In each case he complained to Musa, asking him to withhold it from him, agreeing to send the Children of Israel with him; and when it was withheld from him he broke his covenant and reneged, until Musa (peace be upon him) was commanded to leave with his people. He left with them by night, and in the morning Pharaoh saw they had gone, so he sent musterers into the cities, and a huge, numerous host followed them. Allah revealed to the sea: when My servant Musa strikes you with his staff, split into twelve parts, that Musa and those with him may cross; then swallow whoever remains of Pharaoh and his followers. Then he mentioned what Allah, exalted, did in drowning Pharaoh and his people, until he reached what Allah, exalted, did with the people of Musa (peace be upon him): that He raised the mountain over them as a canopy, and it drew near them until they feared it would fall upon them. Then he mentioned what came after that in his hadith which we mentioned, until he reached the place of Allah's forbidding, exalted, upon those of the people He forbade — whom Musa had earlier called transgressors — then His trying them with wandering in the land, in which He tried them with wandering forty years, wandering in the earth, each morning setting out with no resting place. Then He shaded them with clouds in the wandering, sent down upon them manna and quails, made for them garments that neither wore out nor soiled, placed among them a square stone, and commanded Musa, exalted, who struck it with his staff, whereupon twelve springs gushed from it, three springs on each side; each tribe knew the spring from which to drink; and they never departed from a halting place but found that stone with them in the place where it had been with them the day before. Ibn Abbas raised this hadith to the Prophet (peace be upon him). Abu Ja'far said: What is in this hadith of the nine signs comprises seven signs that were from Allah, exalted, before His drowning Pharaoh and his people in the sea — namely: Musa's staff, his hand, and His sending upon Pharaoh and his people the flood, the locusts, the lice, the frogs and the blood — and among them after His drowning Pharaoh and his people is what we mentioned in this hadith: His raising the mountain over those He raised it, the wandering with which He tried those He tried, His shading them with clouds in the wandering, His sending down manna and quails upon them, His making for them garments that neither wear out nor soil, His placing among them the stone described in this hadith, Musa's striking it with his staff until twelve springs gushed from it, three springs from each one, his informing each tribe of the spring from which to drink, and their never departing from a halting place but finding that stone with them in the place where they had been the day before — and Allah knows best what the two remaining signs are after the seven before the drowning of Pharaoh and his people, of these things. This hadith thus stands raised to the Prophet (peace be upon him). Then we compared what is narrated from whomever we could of those from whom anything about that was narrated: is it in agreement with what we narrated from Ibn Abbas from the Prophet (peace be upon him) and from Safwan about that? [Note: the source text continues beyond what is excerpted; translated literally as it stands.]"
"70","We found that Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Salim narrated to us: Hushaym informed us: Mansur informed us, from al-Hasan — and Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi — about His saying, exalted: nine clear signs — saying: the flood, the locusts, the lice, the frogs, the blood, his hand, his staff, the years of drought, and deficiency of fruits."
"71","We found that Ahmad narrated to us, saying: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Isma'il, from Abu Salih and Ikrimah — the like of it."
"72","We found that Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, saying: al-Firyabi narrated to us: Isra'il narrated to us, from Abu Yahya, from Mujahid — the like of it. The signs mentioned in the hadith of Ibn Abbas, and in the hadiths of those Successors we mentioned with him, were warnings, frightenings and threatenings; and signs are tokens. Allah, exalted, said: And We made the son of Mary and his mother a sign; and He said: And We made the night and the day two signs. Those signs were proofs against creation, for they know they come only from Allah, exalted, and that creatures are powerless over them — so they understand thereby that if they do not return to His command, in what came to them on its account, Allah shall punish and chastise them. Signs may also be acts of worship. Among that is what Allah, exalted, mentioned of His servant and Prophet Zakariyya (peace be upon him), in his saying: My Lord, appoint for me a sign — and Allah's saying to him, exalted: Your sign is that you shall not speak to people for three days except by gesture — in one of the two places in which He mentioned that in His Book; and in the other of them, He said: Your sign is that you shall not speak to people for three nights, being sound. The reconciliation of what is in the hadith of Ibn Abbas and what is in the hadith of Safwan about that is: what is in the hadith of Safwan concerns the signs by which they were bound in worship, while what is in the hadith of Ibn Abbas concerns the signs with which they were threatened, frightened and warned, if they did not perform what they were bound to — what He had made clear to them upon the tongue of His Messenger (peace be upon him). Thus what is in both hadiths stands sound, and we understood from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) that his intent in the one differs from his intent in the other. We ask Allah for success. A questioner asked, saying: In what you narrated from Ibn Abbas and from Safwan, we establish that Allah, exalted, had given His Prophet Musa (peace be upon him) eighteen signs — in each of the two hadiths you narrated thereof nine signs — while in the verse on whose account you mentioned these two hadiths, His giving him is nine signs, namely His saying: And We gave Musa nine signs — mentioning therein no more signs than that. Our need thereafter to establish what the nine signs mentioned therein are stands. Our answer in that, by Allah's success and aid, is that in the verse he recited is His saying, exalted: So ask the Children of Israel, when he came to them, and Pharaoh said to him: I think you, O Musa, bewitched. By that we understood that Musa came to the Children of Israel only with what Allah, exalted, had then bound them to in worship — not with... [Note: source row ends abruptly here; translated literally.]"
"73","Chapter 8: Clarifying what was difficult in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the occasion in which was revealed His saying, exalted: O you who believe, be not like those who harmed Musa — the verse — and what was narrated from Ali about that, with encompassing knowledge that Ali said it not by opinion or derivation, since the like of it is not said by opinion or derivation, but only by instruction from the Prophet (peace be upon him). 67. Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us: Awf the Bedouin narrated to us, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, about this verse: Be not like those who harmed Musa — the verse. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Musa (peace be upon him) was a modest, covering man; hardly any of his skin was seen, out of modesty. Those among the Children of Israel who harmed him did so, saying: He covers himself thus only for a defect in his skin — either leprosy, or a scrotal hernia — thus Ibrahim said to us in his hadith; the philologists differ with him in that, saying it is udrah, for it is adar in the sense of adam, whence by attribution to it: udrah — or a blemish. Allah, exalted, willed to clear him of what they said. One day Musa was alone; he placed his garment on a stone, then bathed. When he finished his bath he turned to his garment to take it, but the stone fled with his garment. Musa took his staff and pursued the stone, saying: My garment, O stone! My garment, O stone! — until he reached an assembly of the Children of Israel, who saw him naked, as the finest of men in form. Allah cleared him of what they said. The stone halted; he took his garment and wore it, then set to beating the stone. He said: By Allah, upon the stone are marks from his beating it — three, four or five. This is what was narrated on this topic from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). As for what was narrated from Ali about that — of which we have encompassing knowledge that he said it only by taking it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), since in it is his informing that Allah, exalted, meant what he mentioned therein — and that is testimony from him about Allah thereby, which he could not venture except by taking it from whence we mentioned."
"74","As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Abbad ibn al-Awwam, from Sufyan ibn Husayn, from al-Hakam, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, from Ali, about: Be not like those who harmed Musa — saying: Musa and Harun ascended the mountain, and Harun died. The Children of Israel said: You killed him — he was gentler to us than you, and more modest. They harmed him in that. Allah, exalted, commanded the angels, who carried him and spoke of his death, until the Children of Israel knew he had died; they buried him, and none knew the place of his grave except the vultures — for Allah made them mute and deaf. Abu Ja'far said: One without knowledge, among those who came upon these two hadiths, would deem them contradictory — far be it from Allah that they be so — for the Children of Israel may have harmed Musa with each of the two harms mentioned in the two hadiths, until Allah cleared him thereof by what He cleared him with, as mentioned in these two hadiths."
"75","Chapter 9: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) of what he did with Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul, chief of the hypocrites, after his death — his praying over him — and what indicates the contrary thereof from him therein. 68. Yazid ibn Sinan and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud both narrated to us, saying: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me: Uqayl ibn Khalid ibn Shihab narrated to me: Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utbah informed me, from Ibn Abbas, from Umar, that he said: When Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul died, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was called to pray over him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) stood, I leapt to him and said: O Messenger of Allah, do you pray over Ibn Ubayy, though he said on such-and-such day such-and-such — recounting his sayings against him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled and said: Step back from me, O Umar. When I pressed him he said: I was given a choice and I chose; had I known that were I to add to seventy, he would be forgiven, I would add thereto. He said: So he prayed over him. Thus Yazid and Ibn Abi Dawud narrated it to us — in his hadith in particular — then he left, and only a little time passed until the two verses of Bara'ah came down: And never pray over any of them who dies, nor stand by his grave — to His saying, exalted: while they are transgressors."
"76","69. Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa both narrated to us, saying: Musaddad narrated to us: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us: Ubayd Allah ibn Umar narrated to me, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar: that when Abd Allah ibn Ubayy died, his son came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, give me your shirt to shroud him in, pray over him and seek forgiveness for him. He gave him his shirt, then said: Inform me of him, that I may pray over him. He informed him, and when he intended to pray over him, Umar pulled him, saying: Has not Allah forbidden you to pray over the hypocrites? He said: I am between two choices: Ask forgiveness for them or do not ask forgiveness for them; if you ask forgiveness for them seventy times, Allah will never forgive them. Then came down: And never pray over any of them who dies, nor stand by his grave. So he abandoned praying over them."
"77","70. Fahd narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us: Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: When Abd Allah ibn Ubayy died, his son Abd Allah came to the Messenger of Allah and asked him to give him his shirt to shroud his father in; he gave him. Then he asked him to pray over him. The Messenger of Allah stood to pray over him, but Umar stood and seized the garment of the Messenger of Allah, saying: O Messenger of Allah, do you pray over him, though Allah has forbidden you to pray over him? The Messenger of Allah said: Allah only gave me a choice, saying: Ask forgiveness for them or do not ask forgiveness for them; if you ask forgiveness for them seventy times — and I shall add to seventy. He said: He is a hypocrite. But the Messenger of Allah prayed over him, and Allah sent down: And never pray over any of them who dies, nor stand by his grave. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith of Ibn Umar is Umar's saying to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him): Do you pray over him, though Allah has forbidden you to pray over the hypocrites — in the hadith of Yahya ibn Sa'id — and in the hadith of Abu Usamah: though Allah has forbidden you to pray over him. That is not in the hadith of Ibn Abbas we narrated before it; in its place in the hadith of Ibn Abbas is: Do you pray over him, though he said on such-and-such day such-and-such. What is in the hadith of Ibn Abbas thereof is preferable with us to what is in the hadith of Ibn Umar; for it is impossible that Allah, exalted, should forbid His Prophet something, then he should do that thing. We deem this only an error of one of this hadith's narrators, and Allah knows best."
"78","71. Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yahya narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Jabir, who said: The chief of the hypocrites enjoined that the Prophet (peace be upon him) pray over him and shroud him in his shirt. When he died, he shrouded him in his shirt, prayed over him and stood by his grave, whereupon Allah sent down: And never pray over any of them who dies, nor stand by his grave. I say: Umar thought that in His saying: Ask forgiveness for them — the verse — is a forbidding of praying over them, so the Prophet (peace be upon him) informed him that is no forbidding; His saying, exalted: And never pray over any of them, had not yet come down — and this is clear in the report. Among what confirms this, and that the matter is contrary to what Abu Ja'far supposed: 72. What Ya'qub ibn Shaybah narrated, from Sunayd ibn Dawud, from Hammad ibn Zayd, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Ali ibn al-Husayn, who said: When Abd Allah ibn Ubayy died, his son al-Hubab — among his righteous companions — came and said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu al-Hubab has died; give him your shirt next to your skin to shroud him in, and pray over him. Umar said: Do you pray over him, though Allah has forbidden him? He said: Where is the forbidding, O Ibn al-Khattab? He recited to him: Ask forgiveness for them or do not ask forgiveness for them, to His saying: Allah for them. He said: Where is the forbidding — do you see any forbidding? So he gave him his shirt and prayed over him. Abu Ja'far said: In these reports we narrated is the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) over Ibn Ubayy; yet there has been narrated from him what indicates he had not prayed over him."
"79","73. As Abd al-Ghani ibn Rifa'ah ibn Abi Aqil Abu Ja'far al-Lakhmi narrated to us: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar — he heard Jabir saying: The Prophet (peace be upon him) came to Ibn Ubayy after he had been placed in his pit, ordered him brought out, placed him upon his knees, breathed upon him of his saliva, and clothed him in his shirt (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — and Allah knows best."
"80","74. As al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us: Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us: Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, who said: When Abd Allah ibn Ubayy died, his son came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, if you do not attend him, we shall ever be reproached for him. He came to him after he had been placed in his pit and said: Why not before you placed him in? He said: He was taken out of his pit; he spat upon him from his head to his foot, and clothed him in his shirt."
"81","75. As Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn Qasim al-Kufi narrated to us: Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Abd al-Malik narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir — the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: In this is what indicates he had not prayed over him, nor attended him, nor come to him before that. This is more like his acts with others among people: his praying over whom he prayed over was only for what Allah does for one over whom it is prayed."
"82","76. As Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi narrated to us: Hushaym narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Hakim al-Ansari, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from Yazid ibn Thabit: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Let me know of none among the believers who dies but you inform me, that I may pray over him; for my prayer over them is mercy."
"83","77. What Fahd narrated to us: Yahya al-Himmani narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Thabit, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace be upon him): that he entered the graveyard and prayed over a man after he was buried, saying: This graveyard was filled with light, after it had been dark over them. Abu Ja'far said: Since his prayer for whom he prayed was only for what is mentioned in these two hadiths, and Ibn Ubayy was not among those entering into that, it is impossible that he prayed over him. He (peace be upon him) left praying over one who pilfered from the spoils — though among those who had campaigned with him to fight his enemies, with no blame known to attach to him from any deed of his besides that — while permitting others with him to pray over him."
"84","78. As al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, saying: Sufyan informed us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from Abu Amrah, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, who said: We were with the Prophet (peace be upon him) at Khaybar when a man of Ashja' died. The Prophet (peace be upon him) did not pray over him, saying: Pray over your companion. They looked into his baggage and found in it beads of Jewish beads not worth two dirhams."
"85","79. As al-Muzani also narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Abd al-Wahhab al-Thaqafi informed us, saying: I heard Yahya ibn Sa'id saying: I heard Muhammad ibn Yahya narrating from Abu Amrah, from Zayd ibn Khalid: that a man among the companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of Ashja', died on the day of Khaybar; they mentioned him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who, he claimed, said to them: Pray over your companion. People's faces changed at that, whereupon, he claimed, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Your companion pilfered in the path of Allah. He said: We searched his baggage and found beads of Jewish beads — by Allah, not worth two dirhams. Abu Ja'far said: If it was his practice not to pray over a pilferer among the believers — for by his pilfering he deserves neither praise in his praying over him, nor his asking his Lord for him what he asks for him in his praying over him, from one free of the like thereof — then his praying over the hypocrites, of whose disbelief Allah had informed him, is farther off, and leaving it over them more deserved. Likewise what was narrated from him of leaving prayer over one who killed himself, among those professing Islam."
"86","80. As Ibn Ma'bad narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us: Isra'il, Sharik and Zuhayr narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Jabir ibn Samurah: that a man slaughtered himself with an arrowhead, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) did not pray over him. Since he did not pray over this man — though of the people of Islam — for his killing himself, he is more deserving not to pray over one whom He, (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the believers and he himself forbade — above that — and leaving him more deserved. It was his practice, with one of his community who died and he was called to pray over him, to consider his circumstances."
"87","81. What Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Ibn Abi Dhi'b and Yunus ibn Yazid informed me; and what Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Yunus informed me — without mentioning Ibn Abi Dhi'b — then both concurred, saying: from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be brought a dead man in debt, and would ask what he left to settle his debt. If told he left enough he prayed over him; otherwise he said: Pray over your companion. When Allah granted him conquests, he said: I am nearer to the believers than their own selves; whoever dies owing debt, its settlement is upon me, and whoever leaves wealth, it is for his heirs. Abu Ja'far said: Since he did not pray over indebted dead among the believers — for they are held back from Paradise by the debts upon them, as narrated from him about that."
"88","82. Among what al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Malik informed us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah al-Ansari, from his father, who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, if I am killed in the path of Allah, patient, seeking reward, advancing, not retreating — will Allah expiate my sins? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. When the man turned, he called him — or ordered him called — and said: How did you say? He repeated the saying to him, and he said: Yes, except debt. Thus Jibril (peace be upon him) said to me."
"89","83. Among what al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Ajlan, from Muhammad ibn Qays, from Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father: that a man came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, if I strike with this sword of mine in the path of Allah, patient, seeking reward, advancing, not retreating — will my sins be expiated? He said: Yes. When he turned he said: Come — this is Jibril saying: except if you owe debt. Abu Ja'far said: The meaning of his saying: will my sins be expiated — i.e., shall I enter Paradise — so he answered him with what he answered him in that. He is more deserving not to pray over one held back from Paradise by what is graver than debt."
"90","Chapter 10: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the numbers of time — which, were one passing before a praying man to stand them, would be better for him than passing before him — what they are, and whether of years, months or days. 84. Yunus narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu al-Nadr, from Busr ibn Sa'id. Abu Juhaym, nephew of Ubayy ibn Ka'b, sent him to Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, asking him: What did you hear from the Prophet (peace be upon him) about one passing before a praying man? He narrated to him from the Prophet (peace be upon him): That one of you should stand forty is better for him than passing before him — not knowing: forty years, months, or days."
"91","85. Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us, from Malik, from Abu al-Nadr, from Busr — as narrated to us from Ibn Uyaynah, from Abu al-Nadr — except that he said: Zayd sent him to Abu al-Juhaym. Abu Ja'far said: Since Malik and Sufyan differed over to whom the narration of what is in this hadith from the Prophet (peace be upon him) is referred — whether of Zayd ibn Khalid or Abu al-Juhaym al-Ansari — we needed to seek it from the narration of other imams who narrated it from Abu al-Nadr, that what we might find therein should judge between Malik and Ibn Uyaynah therein."
"92","86. We found that Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us: Sufyan — meaning al-Thawri — narrated to us, from Salim Abu al-Nadr, from Busr ibn Sa'id, from Abu al-Juhaym al-Ansari, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace be upon him) saying: That one of you should stand forty is better for him than passing before him. He said: I do not know: forty days, forty months, or forty years. In that is that its narrator from the Prophet (peace be upon him) is Abu al-Juhaym al-Ansari, not Zayd ibn Khalid. Judgment in what Malik and Sufyan ibn Uyaynah differed over thus falls for Malik against Ibn Uyaynah; for when Malik and al-Thawri concurred on something therein, they are more entitled to its retention than Ibn Uyaynah in what he differed with them in. Then we returned to seeking the numbers mentioned therein: are they of years, months or days?"
"93","87. We found that Abu Umayyah narrated to us, saying: Ali ibn Qadim narrated to us: Ubayd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us. Abu Ja'far said — meaning Ibn Mawhib — from his uncle, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Had one passing before his brother, cutting across, while he converses with his Lord, known, his standing in his place a hundred years would be better for him than the step he took. Abu Ja'far said: That indicates those forty are of years, not of months or days. We ask Allah for success. This hadith of Abu Hurayrah is with us — and Allah knows best — later than the hadith of Abu al-Juhaym we narrated at the opening of this chapter; for in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah is an increase in threatening the passer before the praying man, while in the hadith of Abu al-Juhaym is lightening. What suits us to think of Allah, exalted, is increase in threatening the disobedient passer before the praying man — not lightening thereof for him in his passing before the praying man."
"94","Chapter 11: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) in his saying: When the commander seeks suspicion among people, he corrupts them. 88. Abu Umayyah and Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud both narrated to us, saying: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Damdam ibn Zur'ah, from Shurayh ibn Ubayd, from Abu Umamah, al-Miqdam ibn Ma'dikarib, Kathir ibn Murrah and Amr ibn al-Aswad: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: When the commander seeks suspicion among people, he corrupts them."
"95","89. Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn al-Ala' ibn Zibriq al-Himsi and Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Wasiti both narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Damdam ibn Zur'ah, from Shurayh ibn Ubayd, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, Kathir ibn Murrah, Amr ibn al-Aswad, al-Miqdam and Abu Umamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"96","90. Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Yazid ibn Abd Rabbihi al-Himsi narrated to us: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Ayyash, from Damdam, from Shurayh ibn Ubayd, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, Amr ibn al-Aswad and Abu Umamah, both saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: When the commander seeks suspicion among people, he corrupts them. Abu Ja'far said: Its meaning with us is that Allah has commanded His servants to cover, and not to lift from them His covering with which He covered them, in what they commit of what He forbade them, before others among people. There was narrated from him about that:"
"97","91. What Nasr ibn Marzuq Abu al-Fath narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us: Anas ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id: Abd Allah ibn Dinar narrated to me, from Ibn Umar: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) stood after stoning the Aslami and said: Avoid this filth Allah has forbidden; whoever falls into it, let him cover himself with Allah's covering, exalted, and repent to Allah — for whoever shows us his page, we shall enforce Allah's Book upon him."
"98","92. And what Yunus narrated to us: Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi informed me, from Yahya: Abd Allah ibn Dinar, freedman of Ibn Umar, narrated to me: that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) ... then he mentioned this hadith word for word."
"99","93. And what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us: Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us: Abu Salamah narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Nu'aym ibn Hazzal — and Hazzal had sought stoning for Ma'iz — saying: Among his household was a slave girl pasturing sheep; Ma'iz fell upon her. Hazzal seized him, schemed with him and deceived him, saying: Go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and inform him of what you did — perhaps a Quranic verse shall come down about you. The Prophet of Allah (peace be upon him) ordered him stoned, and he was stoned. When the touch of stones hurt him he set out running; a man met him with a camel's jawbone, struck him and felled him. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said: O Hazzal, had you covered him with your garment, it would have been better for you. Abu Ja'far said: When the commander pursues what Allah commanded leaving unpursued, the people follow that from him — and in that is their corruption. If one says: How can what you mentioned be as you mentioned, when the Prophet (peace be upon him) ordered Unays al-Aslami to go to the wife of the man reported to him about her that she committed adultery, to ask her about that, and to stone her if she confessed it to him? He mentioned about that:"
"100","94. What Yunus and Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi both narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubayd Allah, from Abu Hurayrah, Zayd ibn Khalid and Shibl, saying: We were sitting at the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man stood to him and said: I adjure you by Allah but to judge between us by Allah's Book. His opponent stood — more versed than him — and said: He is right; judge between us by Allah's Book and permit me. He said: Speak. He said: My son was a hireling of this man, and committed adultery with his wife. I ransomed him with a hundred sheep and a servant. Then I asked men of knowledge, who informed me that upon my son is a hundred lashes and a year's exile, and upon this man's wife is stoning. He said: By Him in whose hand is my soul, I shall judge between you by Allah's Book: the hundred sheep and the servant are returned to you; upon your son a hundred lashes and a year's exile. Go, O Unays, to this man's wife; if she confesses, stone her. He went to her in the morning; she confessed, and he stoned her."
"101","95. And what al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Abu Hurayrah and Zayd ibn Khalid, that they informed him: that two men disputed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). One said: O Messenger of Allah, judge between us by Allah's Book. The other — the more versed of the two — said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah, judge between us by Allah's Book, and permit me to speak. He said: Speak. He said: My son was a hireling of this man, and committed adultery with his wife. I was told my son incurs stoning, so I ransomed him with a hundred sheep and a slave girl. Then I asked people of knowledge, who informed me that upon my son is a hundred lashes and a year's exile, and stoning is only upon his wife. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Him in whose hand is my soul, I shall judge between you by Allah's Book: your sheep and your slave girl are returned to you. He lashed his son a hundred and exiled him a year, and ordered Unays al-Aslami to go to the other's wife; if she confessed, he was to stone her. She confessed, and he stoned her. Malik said: al-asif is the hireling."
"102","96. And what Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Yunus and Malik informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah, from Abu Hurayrah and Zayd, both saying: We were sitting at the Prophet (peace be upon him) — then he mentioned the like of it. It was said to him: Al-Shafi'i used to say about that, as al-Muzani narrated to us from him in his compendium, his saying: The leader has no right, when a man is accused of adultery, to send to him asking him about that — for Allah, exalted, says: And do not spy. If one confuses another with the Prophet's (peace be upon him) sending Unays to a man's wife, saying: If she confesses, stone her — that was a woman whom the adulterer's father mentioned as having committed adultery. He was bound to ask; if she confessed, she was punished, and the penalty fell from her accuser; if she denied, her accuser was punished. Abu Ja'far said: I say in answer to its sayer: this hadith did not fully convey to us what passed of the two disputants and of one's son at the Prophet (peace be upon him). That is because in it one of them said: My son was a hireling of this man — meaning the other of them — and committed adultery with his wife. I was told my son incurs stoning, so I ransomed him with a hundred sheep and a servant. We know with certainty he feared for his son not from his own confession against him; we know he feared for him only from his confessing that of himself — for none is taken by another's confession against him. Since we understood that, we understood that this disputant's son was either truthful in what he mentioned of himself — his adultery with his father's disputant's wife — so what is upon him therein is the penalty of adultery, nothing else; or lying in that — so what is upon him therein is the penalty of slander against his father's disputant's wife, for accusing her of adultery, nothing else. When the Prophet (peace be upon him) established that a penalty of those two penalties was due upon him, not knowing which, necessity called him therein to learn what the accused woman says in that: whether she confirms her accuser — so what is upon her therein is the penalty of adultery, nothing else — or belies him therein — so what is upon him is the penalty of slandering her, in accusing her of adultery, nothing else. This with us — and Allah knows best — is the reason the Prophet (peace be upon him) ordered Unays to go in the morning to that woman. Success is with Allah."
"103","Chapter 12: Clarifying what was difficult in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) that the son of Adam was created upon three hundred and sixty joints: when he magnifies Allah, exalted, declares His oneness, praises Him, seeks His forgiveness, glorifies Him, removes bone, stone and thorn from people's path, commands right and forbids wrong, he counts that as three hundred joints. 97. Ja'far al-Firyabi narrated to us: Hudbah narrated to us: Aban al-Attar narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated to us, that Zayd — meaning Ibn Sallam — narrated to him: that Abu Sallam narrated to him that Abd Allah ibn Farrukh narrated to him — Abu Ja'far said: he is the freedman of Abu Talhah — that A'ishah narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: The son of Adam was created upon three hundred and sixty joints. When he magnifies Allah, declares His oneness, praises Allah, seeks Allah's forgiveness, glorifies Allah, removes bone from people's path, stone and thorn from people's path, commands right and forbids wrong, he counts that as three hundred — Abu Ja'far said: I deem dropped from the hadith: and sixty joints — he spends that evening having removed himself from the Fire. Abu Ja'far said: We reflected upon that to establish the meaning by which reward is assigned to each of these joints, and whether we find a like thereof in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) besides this hadith."
"104","98. We found that Yunus narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us: Ibn Abi Dhi'b informed us, from al-Harith ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Allah wrote upon every limb its share of adultery: the eye commits adultery, its adultery being looking; the tongue commits adultery, its adultery being speech; the hand commits adultery, its adultery being seizing; the foot commits adultery, its adultery being walking; the ear commits adultery, its adultery being listening; and the private part confirms that or belies it. Since what is in this hadith, in blameworthy matter, covers all limbs, praiseworthy matter likewise covers all limbs. By what we mentioned, the meaning of these two hadiths agrees, and their intent is clear — and Allah knows best. Then we found from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) a hadith clarifying the meaning of the hadith we mentioned at the opening of this chapter. It is what:"
"105","99. Ahmad ibn Abd al-Mu'min al-Marwazi narrated to us: Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us: al-Husayn ibn Waqid informed us, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, who said: I heard my father saying: [I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying:] In man are three hundred and sixty joints; upon him is to give charity for each joint thereof. They said: Who can bear that, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Phlegm in the mosque, which you bury, or something you remove from the road; if you cannot, two rakahs of forenoon prayer suffice you. By this we established that the intent in the first hadith is charity for each of those joints mentioned therein, for what is mentioned in this second hadith. We ask Allah for success."
"106","Chapter 13: Clarifying what was difficult for us in what we narrated from the Prophet (peace be upon him) in his saying: The fighters must hold back, the nearest then the nearest, even if a woman. 100. Muhammad ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, from al-Awza'i: Hisn narrated to me, from Abu Salamah, who said: A'ishah narrated to me: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: The fighters must hold back, the first then the first, even if a woman."
"107","101. Abu Zur'ah al-Nasri al-Dimashqi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak — he is al-Suri — narrated to us: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i: Hisn narrated to me, from Abu Salamah, from A'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The fighters must hold back, the first then the first, even if a woman."
"108","I heard Abu Zur'ah saying: Sulayman — meaning Ibn Abd al-Rahman — also narrated to me this hadith, from al-Walid ibn Muslim, adding in it — he said: al-Awza'i said: Women have no pardon."
"109","102. Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us: Abd al-Wahhab ibn Najdah al-Hawti narrated to us: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it with its chain, without mentioning what Abu Zur'ah told us from Sulayman in his hadith from al-Awza'i about women's pardon. Abu Ja'far said: We asked more than one of our shaykhs about this hadith's interpretation. As for Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam, his answer to us in that was saying: al-Firyabi — meaning Muhammad ibn Yusuf — said: I asked al-Awza'i about this hadith's interpretation, and he said: I do not know what it is. Muhammad ibn Abd Allah said: If its narrator does not know its interpretation, we are more entitled not to know its interpretation. As for Isma'il ibn Yahya al-Muzani, he said: Its interpretation with me — and Allah knows best — is about fighters of the qiblah fighting by interpretation: insight may overtake some of them, and one it overtakes needs to turn from his blameworthy station to the praiseworthy station; if he finds no path to pass thereby, he remains in his first place, and may be killed in it — so they were commanded with what is in this hadith for this reason. As for Ahmad ibn Abi Imran, his answer in that was narrating from Abu Ubayd, that he claimed this hadith is conveyed by people contrary to what it truly is, mentioning that it reached him from al-Walid ibn Muslim that he narrated it from al-Awza'i, from Hisn, from Abu Salamah, from A'ishah: that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: The slain man's family may hold back, the nearest then the nearest, even if a woman. Abu Ubayd said: This holding back is pardon of blood; and in this hadith is what indicates women's pardon of deliberate blood is permitted, as men's pardon thereof is permitted — all this from Abu Ubayd's speech. Abu Ja'far said: We reflected upon... [Note: source continues; the Syrian narrators who narrated this hadith from him are the authority in his hadith; they narrated it from him contrary to what reached Abu Ubayd from him that he narrated it. What they narrated thereof is more entitled than what reached him — especially with their hearing it from al-Walid, while he has only its reaching him from al-Walid. Bishr ibn Bakr followed them in that from al-Awza'i, narrating it from al-Awza'i as they narrated it from al-Walid from al-Awza'i. Once that is ruled out, its interpretation is no better than what we mentioned therein from al-Muzani — except that some scholars mentioned that fighters among Muslims in their fighting the people of war also enter into that, since those of war's people bearing numbers permitting them to turn from fighting them to the Muslims' band, gaining strength thereby against their enemy, may befall them, fighting them with them — and this interpretation is not far from what he said.] Abu Ja'far said: We mentioned in this chapter, after this hadith, al-Awza'i's saying: Women have no pardon — indicating al-Awza'i held this hadith to be, as Abu Ubayd conveyed from al-Walid, about pardon of blood; then al-Awza'i differed with it, saying: Women have no pardon."
"110","Chapter 14: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him): The son of Mary (peace be upon him) is about to descend among you as a just judge, breaking the cross, killing the pig, and abolishing the poll tax. 103. Muhammad ibn Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Salih Abu Shurayh narrated to us: al-Firyabi narrated to us: al-Awza'i narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) used to say: By Him in whose hand is my soul, the son of Mary is about to descend among you as a just judge, breaking the cross, killing the pig, abolishing the poll tax, and wealth shall overflow until none accepts it."
"111","104. Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us: al-Zuhri narrated to us, from Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace be upon him) the like of it — except that he said: a just judge."
"112","105. Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us: my father and Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth informed us, both saying: al-Layth narrated to us, from Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Ata' ibn Mina', freedman of Ibn Abi Dhubab, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The son of Mary shall descend as a just ruler, breaking the cross, killing the pig, abolishing the poll tax; milch camels shall be left, none striving upon them; hatred, enmity and envy shall depart; he shall call to wealth, and none shall accept it. Abu Ja'far said: We reflected upon these two hadiths and established that when wealth returns among people until none accepts it, they thereby all become rich; poverty, need, and all the categories for whose people Allah assigned charity, by His saying, exalted: Charities are only for the poor and the needy ... to His saying: and the wayfarer — vanish. There remain no zakah recipients to place it in; when that is so, its obligation falls. Likewise the poll tax: Allah, exalted, imposed it upon whom He imposed it to be spent in what is needed of fighting and else what it must be spent in; when that vanishes and it has no recipients to spend upon, its obligation falls. This with us — and Allah knows best — is the sense of what was narrated in these two hadiths."
"113","Chapter 15: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace be upon him) about Satan running through the son of Adam as blood runs, and whether the Prophet (peace be upon him) was in that like others among people or unlike them. 106. Fahd narrated to us: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us: Shu'ayb informed us, from al-Zuhri: Ali ibn Husayn narrated to me: that Safiyyah, wife of the Prophet (peace be upon him), informed him that she came to the Prophet (peace be upon him) visiting him in his retreat in the mosque in the last ten of Ramadan. She spoke with him a while, then rose to return; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose with her to escort her. When she reached the mosque gate by Umm Salamah's door, two men of the Ansar passed them, greeted the Prophet (peace be upon him) and went on. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said to them: At your leisure — she is Safiyyah bint Huyayy. They said: Glory be to Allah, O Messenger of Allah! — and it weighed upon them. He said: Satan reaches of the son of Adam the reach of blood; I feared he might cast into your hearts."
"114","107. Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Hanzali informed us: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Ali ibn Husayn, from Safiyyah bint Huyayy — then he mentioned the like of it."
"115","108. Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Hushaysh al-Basri Abu al-Husayn narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Maslamah ibn Qa'nab narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was with one of his wives when a man passed him. He called him, saying: O so-and-so, she is my wife so-and-so. He said: O Messenger of Allah, whom I would suspect — I would never suspect you. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Satan runs through the son of Adam as blood runs. Abu Ja'far said: In these two hadiths we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is what may mean the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was in that like others among people, and may mean he was therein unlike them. So we examined what was narrated in this chapter besides these two hadiths: is in it anything indicating either?"
"116","109. We found that Fahd narrated to us, saying: Abd Allah ibn Raja' narrated to us. We found that Abu Umayyah narrated to us, saying: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us — both saying: Shayban informed us, from Mansur, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from his father, from Ibn Mas'ud, from the Prophet (peace be upon him), who said: There is none among you but his companion of the jinn has been assigned to him. It was said: And you? He said: And me — but Allah aided me against him, so he submitted; he commands me only to good."
"117","110. We found that Fahd narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us: Isa ibn Yunus informed us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Jabir, who said: The Prophet (peace be upon him) said to us: Do not enter upon women whose husbands are absent; for Satan runs through one of you as blood runs. It was said: And through you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: And through me — but Allah aided me against him, so he submitted."
"118","111. We found that Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us: Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah narrated to me, saying: I heard Abu al-Nadr saying: I heard Urwah saying: A'ishah said: I missed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one night — he had been with me on my bed — and found him prostrating, heels together, toes facing the qiblah. I heard him saying: I seek refuge in Your pleasure from Your wrath, in Your pardon from Your punishment, and in You from You; I cannot encompass all that is in You. When he finished he said: O A'ishah, did your devil seize you? I said: Have you no devil? He said: No son of Adam but has a devil. I said: And you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: And me — but I prayed to Allah, and He aided me against him, so he submitted. Abu Ja'far said: We established that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in this respect like all other people, and that Allah aided him against him, so he submitted by the submission to which He guided him — until he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, in safety from him, unlike others among people with their like. If one says: there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter something requiring the lifting of contradiction from it, and from what I narrated of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) being singled out with his devil's submission, that he be safe from him. He mentioned about that:"
"119","112. What Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah ibn Rashid al-Basri Abu Amr and Fahd both narrated to us, saying: Abu Mushir narrated to us: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to me: Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from Abu al-Azhar al-Anmari: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), when he took his bed at night, would say: In Allah's name I lay my side; O Allah, forgive my sin, drive away my devil, release my pledge, weight my scale, and place me in the highest assembly. It was said to him: This with us — and Allah knows best — was the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) before his devil's submission; once he submitted, it is impossible that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) should pray to Allah therein thus, with the submission he is upon."
"120","Chapter 16: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he commanded about traveling on camels in plenty and in drought. 113. Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Jarud narrated to us: Ruwaym al-Muqri al-Lu'lu'i narrated to us, saying: al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab: Anas informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: When the land is fertile, dismount from your backs, giving them their due of herbage; when the land is barren, press on over it with their marrow. Keep to night travel, for the earth is folded at night."
"121","114. Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to me: Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it — without mentioning Anas ibn Malik in it. Abu Ja'far said: We reflected upon this hadith and found in it the Messenger of Allah's (peace be upon him) commanding, in plenty, dismounting from backs that they take their need of herbage, and his commanding, in drought, pressing on over them with their marrow — with a choice — and his commanding them, along with that, that their travel upon them be at night, for the earth is folded therein, so distances upon backs therein are shorter than distances at other than night. There has also been narrated from him in that what falls under this topic."
"122","115. What Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated to us: Malik narrated to us, from Suhayl, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: When you travel in plenty, give camels their due, and keep to night travel, for the earth is folded at night."
"123","116. And what Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn Minhal al-Anmati narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Suhayl narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: When you travel in plenty, give camels their due; when you travel in drought, hasten travel; and when you intend a night halt, turn aside from the road. Abu Ja'far said: The meaning of Abu Umayyah's hadith is aiming at travel upon them at night; and in Ibn Khuzaymah's hadith is what indicates that, by its mentioning the night halt — and halting in this sense is only at night, not by day."
"124","Chapter 17: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) about the span between the placing of the Sacred Mosque and the Farthest Mosque upon earth. 117. Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, which mosque was placed upon earth first? He said: The Sacred Mosque. I said: Then which? He said: Then the Farthest Mosque. I said: How long between them? He said: Forty years. Wherever prayer overtakes you, pray — for it is a mosque. An objector said: The builder of the Sacred Mosque is Ibrahim (peace be upon him), while the builder of the Farthest Mosque is Dawud and his son Sulayman (peace be upon them) after him — and between Ibrahim and them were as many generations as Allah willed there to be: after Ibrahim his son Ishaq, after his son Ishaq his son Ya'qub, after Ya'qub his son Yusuf, after Yusuf Musa, after Musa Dawud — besides the tribes between them and other prophets of Allah besides them — in that, spans far exceeding forty by multiples. Our answer to him in that is: the builder of these two mosques is whom he mentioned; Abu Dharr's question to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was not about the span between their building — he asked him about the span between their placing — and he answered him with what he answered him. It may be that the placer of the Farthest Mosque was some prophet of Allah before Dawud and before Sulayman; then Dawud and Sulayman built it at the time they built it. There is nothing in this hadith, by Allah's praise, necessitating impossibility. Likewise the interpretation of its like must be borne thereupon, as Ali ibn Abi Talib said."
"125","As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Ali, who said: When you are narrated a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), think of the Messenger of Allah in the most wholesome, godfearing and guided manner."
"126","Chapter 18: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) about the two refuge-seeking surahs, and what was narrated from him necessitating that they are of the Quran. 118. Al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah and Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said: I asked Ubayy ibn Ka'b about the two refuge-seeking surahs, saying to him: Your brother Ibn Mas'ud erases them from the codex. He said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and he said: It was said to me: Say — so I said. We say as the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said."
"127","119. Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Raqqi narrated to us: al-Humaydi narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us: Abdah ibn Abi Lubabah and Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated to us, that they heard Zirr ibn Hubaysh saying: I asked Ubayy ibn Ka'b about the two refuge-seeking surahs — then he mentioned the like of it."
"128","120. Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Abd Allah ibn Yunus narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, who said: I said to Ubayy: Abd Allah says of the two refuge-seeking surahs: Do not attach to the Quran what is not of it. He said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about them, and he said: It was said to me: Say — so I said. Ubayy said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: Say — so we say."
"129","121. Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us: Malik ibn Mighwal narrated to us, from Asim, from Zirr, who said: I said to Ubayy: O Abu al-Mundhir, the two surahs not in Abd Allah's codex? He said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about them, and he said: It was said to me: Say — so I said to you. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us — so we say as he said. Abu Ja'far said: What we narrated from Ubayy in these reports, of his answer to Zirr, contains nothing affirming from him that they are of the Quran, nor removing them from it. Then we examined what was narrated from the Prophet (peace be upon him) about them besides that: do we find therein his affirming that they are of the Quran, or that they are not of it?"
"130","122. We found that Malik ibn Yahya al-Hamdani narrated to us, saying: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us: Isma'il narrated to us, from Qays, from Uqbah ibn Amir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah sent down upon me verses the like of which He never sent down upon me: the refuge-seeking ones — then he recited them both."
"131","123. Al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, saying: Abdah ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Qays, from Uqbah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There were sent down upon me verses the like of which I was never shown — or: I never saw — meaning the two refuge-seeking surahs."
"132","124. We found that Yahya ibn Uthman ibn Salih narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Wasiti narrated to us: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Ibn Jabir, from al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Uqbah: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led them in the dawn prayer, reciting for them: Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak, and: Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of men. Then he passed by me, saying: See, O Uqbah — recite them both whenever you sleep and whenever you rise."
"133","125. We found that al-Rabi' narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, saying: Ibn Jabir narrated to us, from al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman: Uqbah ibn Amir narrated to me, saying: While I was leading the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a mountain pass among those passes, he said to me: Will you not ride, O Uqbah? I deemed the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) too great to ride his mount; then I feared it be disobedience, so I rode a while, then dismounted. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rode, and I led him. He said to me: O Uqbah, shall I not teach you of the best two surahs people recited? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah — may my father and mother be ransom for you. He said: Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of men; Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak. When prayer was established, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited them both, then passed by me, saying: How did you find it, O Uqbah — recite them both whenever you sleep and rise."
"134","126. We found that Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, saying: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us: Haywah ibn Shurayh al-Himsi narrated to us: Baqiyyah narrated to us, from Bahir ibn Sa'd, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Uqbah: that a white mule was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he rode it, and Uqbah took to leading it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Uqbah, recite. He said: What shall I recite, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Recite: Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak, from the evil of what He created. He repeated it to me until I recited it, then said: Perhaps you deemed it light — you never stood praying with aught like them."
"135","127. We found that Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, saying: Hajib ibn al-Walid narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from his father, from Uqbah ibn Amir, who said: I was traveling with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) between al-Juhfah and al-Abwa' when wind and darkness befell us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began seeking refuge with: Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak, and: Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of men, saying: O Uqbah, seek refuge — for no seeker of refuge sought with the like of them. Then I heard him leading us with them in prayer."
"136","128. We found that Abu Umayyah narrated to us, saying: Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from a man of his people: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) passed by him and said: Recite in your prayer the two refuge-seeking surahs. Abu Ja'far said: In what we narrated is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) affirming that they are of the Quran. All we narrated from him about that thus agrees, once sound and its meanings emerge — none of it contradicting any. We ask Allah for success."
"137","Chapter 19: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) about the occasion in which was revealed: You never screened yourselves lest your hearing testify against you — to His saying: they are not of those admitted to excuse. 129. Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us: Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us; and Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Kathir al-Abdi narrated to us: Sufyan al-Thawri informed us; and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from Wahb ibn Rabi'ah, from Abd Allah, who said: I was screened by the Ka'bah's curtains when three men came — a Thaqafi and his two Qurashi in-laws — fat of belly, scant in hearts' understanding. They conversed among themselves, and one said: Does Allah hear what we said? One said: I deem He hears when we raise, and hears not when we lower. The other said: If He hears any of it, He hears it all. I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and Allah sent down: You never screened yourselves lest it testify against you — until he reached: of those admitted to excuse."
"138","130. Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us — Yahya said: Sufyan said: Mansur narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Abu Ma'mar Abd Allah ibn Sakhbarah al-Azdi, from Abd Allah — the like of it. Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Saminah al-Baghdadi narrated to us, saying: Qabisah ibn Uqbah said: Qutbah ibn Abd al-Aziz said to me: Sufyan and I were reviewing al-A'mash's hadith; I mentioned Abd Allah's hadith — I was clinging to the Ka'bah's curtains — saying: from Umarah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abd Allah. Sufyan said to me: Umarah — from Wahb ibn Rabi'ah, from Abd Allah. I rose at once to al-A'mash, saying: O Abu Muhammad, have you Abd Allah's hadith — I was clinging to the Ka'bah's curtains? He said: Umarah — from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid. I said: Sufyan says: Umarah — from Wahb ibn Rabi'ah. He said to me: Wait. He hummed as a camel hums, then said: Sufyan is right. Abu Ja'far said: We reflected upon these verses mentioned in this hadith and found one among people saying: If these verses, of the surah they are in, indicate the impossibility of what is in this hadith — since their revelation was on its account — namely His saying, exalted: And the day Allah's enemies are mustered to the Fire, and they are held ... until, when they reach it, their hearing testifies against them — the verse. That concerns something on the Resurrection; then He followed it with His saying: And they shall say to their skins — to His saying: and to Him you are returned. That concerns a saying from them then — an address to their skins when they testify against them with what they testified against them therewith — all occurring on the Day of Resurrection, not of this world. Then He, exalted, said reproaching them: You never screened yourselves — to His saying: If they endure, the Fire is their abode; if they seek excuse, they are not of those admitted to excuse — i.e., then. In that is what negates that what is in Ibn Mas'ud's hadith, which I narrated, be as it is — for in it is Allah's sending it down upon His Prophet for what those ignoramuses did in this world. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's success, is: Allah, exalted, may have sent down upon His Messenger, in the report Ibn Mas'ud mentioned to him — what he mentioned to him of those ignoramuses — a reproach of them, and Allah's informing them thereby of what He informed them therein; then Allah sent down upon him after that: And the day Allah's enemies are mustered to the Fire — to His saying: and to Him you are returned. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placed that in the place he placed it, of what matches that, joining it thereto — since all that is of what the people of the Fire are addressed with on the Day of Resurrection. Among what strengthens this possibility we mentioned is what... [Note: source row continues into the Uthman codex report, translated in the next row.]"
"139","131. Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Humran al-Humrani narrated to us: Awf the Bedouin narrated to us, from Yazid al-Farisi, from Ibn Abbas, who said: I said to Uthman: What moved you to take al-Anfal — of the oft-repeated — and Bara'ah — of the hundreds — joining them, writing no line: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, between them, and placing them among the seven long ones? What moved you to that? He said: Uthman said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — time would pass while surahs of number came down upon him. When something came down upon him, one of his scribes would enter, and he would say: Place this in the surah in which such-and-such is mentioned. When verses came down upon him he would say: Place these verses in the surah in which such-and-such is mentioned. Al-Anfal was of the first revealed in Madinah, and Bara'ah was of the last of the Quran; their stories resembled each other, so I thought it of them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died without clarifying to us that it is of them; for that reason I joined them, wrote no line: In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful, between them, and placed them among the seven long ones. Uthman thus informed that they were commanded to place some revealed verses in a surah complete before that; and in his saying — may Allah be pleased with him — their stories resembled each other's, is what indicates they were only commanded to place later-revealed verses with what resembles them of earlier-revealed thereof. In what we mentioned is what indicates the possibility we described, by which we resolve the interpretation we mentioned from him what we mentioned. We ask Allah for success."
"140","Chapter 20: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) about the intent of Allah's saying, exalted: Then on the Day of Resurrection you shall dispute before your Lord. 132. Yunus narrated to us: Anas ibn Iyad al-Laythi informed me, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Zubayr, who said: When this verse came down: You are mortal, and they are mortal — to His saying: you shall dispute — al-Zubayr said: O Messenger of Allah, shall what was in this world be repeated upon us, along with private sins? He said: Yes, until every rightful one is given his right."
"141","Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Mansur ibn Salamah al-Khuza'i narrated to us: Ya'qub al-Qummi narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Abi al-Mughirah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Umar, who said: This verse came down, and we knew not about what it came down: Then on the Day of Resurrection you shall dispute before your Lord. A speaker said: Whom shall we dispute — there is no dispute between us and the People of the Book — whom shall we dispute? Until the tribulation fell, and Ibn Umar said: This is what our Lord promised us — disputing therein. Abu Ja'far said: One supposing supposed that what is in these two hadiths necessitates contradiction of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the occasion in which this verse came down. We examined that and found it, by Allah's praise and favor, free of that; for the hadith of Ibn Umar thereof contains only what they said upon the verse's revelation, and what became clear at the tribulation's advent that it is intended therein — and that was interpretation from him, not his reporting it as heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him). What is in al-Zubayr's hadith is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answer to him when he asked him what he mentioned of asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about in his hadith — and the Messenger of Allah's (peace be upon him) answer thereto of what he answered him. Nothing else in Ibn Umar's hadith, nor besides it as we know, contradicts it. We ask Allah for success."
"142","Chapter 21: Clarifying the difficulty in what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) in his saying: Narrate of the Children of Israel, no harm. 133. Yunus narrated to us: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us; and al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us: Bishr narrated to us, from al-Awza'i: Hassan ibn Atiyyah narrated to us: Abu Kabshah al-Saluli narrated to me, saying: I heard Abd Allah ibn Amr saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) saying: Convey from me, though a verse; narrate of the Children of Israel, no harm; whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire."
"143","134. Bakkar ibn Qutaybah and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq both narrated to us, saying: Abu Asim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Hassan ibn Atiyyah, from Abu Kabshah al-Saluli, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — and they mentioned the like of it."
"144","135. Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: Narrate of the Children of Israel, no harm. We reflected upon what is in this hadith, in his saying to his community: Narrate of the Children of Israel, no harm — with us, and Allah knows best, his intending that they know the wonders among them; and for their affairs were governed by prophets."
"145","136. As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Abu Ma'mar Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn Abi al-Hajjaj al-Minqari narrated to us: Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Juhadah, from Furat al-Qazzaz, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Children of Israel were governed by prophets; whenever a prophet died, a prophet arose. Abu Ja'far said: In what they narrate thereof is what may admonish them and warn them against leaving adherence to Allah's religion, as the Children of Israel left it — lest He punish them as He punished them. Along with that he (peace be upon him) narrated to them thereof. [Note: the source row ends here; the chapter discussion evidently continues beyond row 145.]"
"146","137 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb al-Washihi narrated to us, Abu Hilal al-Rasibi narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Abu Hassan, from 'Imran ibn Husayn who said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would spend most of his night narrating about the Children of Israel, not rising except for the main prayer.} Abu Ja'far said: And his saying came after what he had commanded them of narrating about the Children of Israel, 'and there is no harm,' i.e., there is no harm upon you if you do not narrate about them, just as he said in what has been narrated from him besides that:"
"147","138 - As Bakkar and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Husayn al-Hubrani, from Abu Sa'id al-Khayr, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever applies kohl, let him do so an odd number of times; whoever does it has done well, and whoever does not, there is no harm. Whoever cleans himself with stones, let him do so an odd number of times; whoever does it has done well, and whoever does not, there is no harm. Whoever goes to relieve himself, let him conceal himself, and if he finds nothing but a heap of sand, let him gather it and place it behind him, for the devils play with the sitting places of the sons of Adam; whoever does it has done well, and whoever does not, there is no harm. Whoever eats food, whatever he removes with a toothpick, let him throw it away, and whatever he moves with his tongue, let him swallow it; whoever does it has done well, and whoever does not, there is no harm.} He said: So whatever he commanded of these mentioned things in this hadith, each command was followed by his saying 'and there is no harm,' i.e., there is no harm upon you if you do not do what I commanded you of that, since what he commanded them was by way of choice, not obligation. Likewise what he commanded them of narrating about the Children of Israel, which his saying 'and there is no harm' followed, was also by way of latitude from him to them, that they need not narrate about them if they wish, because what he commanded them was by way of choice, not obligation. And that was a favor Allah bestowed upon him after his saying to them 'Convey from me even a verse,' which he had commanded them as an obligation upon them; then he followed that in his command with what he commanded them of narrating about the Children of Israel, clarifying that it differed from what preceded it, since what preceded was obligatory and what followed was optional."
"148","Chapter 22 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibiting the sale involving thunya (an exception). 139 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us, Hammad, i.e. Ibn Zayd, narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Zubayr and Sa'id ibn Mina, from Jabir: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) {prohibited muhaqalah, muzabanah and mukhabarah.} One of them said: and mu'awamah (selling years ahead), and the other said: selling for years; and he prohibited thunya. He said: And he permitted 'araya."
"149","140 - Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Firyabi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Hammad, i.e. Ibn Zayd, narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu al-Zubayr and Sa'id ibn Mina, from Jabir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he {prohibited muzabanah, muhaqalah, mu'awamah and mukhabarah.} One of them said: and selling for years, and thunya, and he permitted the sale of 'araya. The outward meaning of the hadith was a prohibition of the sale involving thunya absolutely, and in that, if it was not literally contrary to its outward meaning, was prevention of the sale in which thunya occurs. So we examined what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on this topic besides this hadith, whether we find in it what clarifies the true meaning of his intent in that."
"150","141 - So we found that Ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, saying: Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, 'Abbad, i.e. Ibn al-'Awwam, narrated to us, from Sufyan ibn Husayn who said: The trustworthy Yunus ibn 'Ubayd narrated to me, from 'Ata, from Jabir {that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibited the sale involving thunya until it is known.} By that the true meaning of what the prohibition fell upon in the hadith of Abu al-Zubayr and Sa'id regarding the sale with thunya became clear to us: that it is the thunya which is not known, and that the known thunya is otherwise, and that what is excepted therein is permissible when it is known and when what remains after it of the sale is known for a known price. And 'Ata ibn Abi Rabah preserved from Jabir, in what he narrated to them of that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what neither Abu al-Zubayr nor Sa'id preserved; so what he narrated from Jabir on that is more worthy than what they both narrated from him. The people of knowledge differed about a sale when it involves a part of the sold item as an exception. Malik ibn Anas used to say regarding that:"
"151","As Yunus narrated to us - Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Malik said: The agreed-upon practice with us is that when a man sells the fruit of his orchard, he may except from it up to one third of the fruit, not exceeding that; and whatever is less than a third, there is no harm in it if he considers it a third or less. Most of the scholars opposed him in that, among them Abu Hanifah, Zufar, Abu Yusuf, Muhammad and al-Shafi'i, who permitted the sale with this exception and did not distinguish therein between whether the excepted portion is less than a third, a third, or more, so long as the fruit remaining after it is known. And in the hadith of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we have narrated in this chapter, from the hadith of 'Ata from Jabir, prohibiting the sale with thunya until it is known, there is evidence for what they said, when what enters the sale after the thunya is known and its fruit is known. This view is the worthier of the two views with us on that, due to the agreement of the people of knowledge with what we have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on it."
"152","Chapter 23 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about which of his daughters was the best of them. 142 - Al-Rabi' al-Jizi, Yusuf ibn Yazid Abu Yazid, and Fahd narrated to us, saying: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, Ibn al-Had narrated to us, 'Umar ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Urwah narrated to me, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'A'ishah {that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived in Madinah, his daughter left Makkah with Banu Kinanah, and they went out after her, and Habbar ibn al-Aswad caught up with her and kept prodding her camel until it threw her, so she miscarried what was in her womb and bled profusely. He carried her onward, and Banu Hashim and Banu Umayyah disputed over her. Banu Umayyah said: We have more right to her, and she was married to their cousin Abu al-'As ibn Rabi'ah ibn 'Abd Shams, and she stayed with Hind bint Rabi'ah, and Hind used to say to her: This is because of your father. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Zayd ibn Harithah: Will you not go and bring Zaynab? He said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Take this ring of mine and give it to her. Zayd set out, proceeding discreetly and leaving his camel, until he came to a shepherd and said: For whom do you graze? He said: For Abu al-'As ibn Rabi'ah. He said: And whose sheep are these? He said: Zaynab bint Muhammad's. He walked with him a while, then said to him: Would you take something to give to her without mentioning it to anyone? He said: Yes. So he gave him the ring, and the shepherd drove his sheep in and gave her the ring, and she recognized it and said: Who gave you this? He said: A man. She said: Where did you leave him? He said: At such-and-such place. She waited until night, then went out to him, and he said to her: Ride in front of me. She said: No, rather you ride. So he rode and she rode behind him until she came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: She is the best of my daughters; she was afflicted for my sake. That reached 'Ali ibn Husayn ibn 'Ali, so he went to 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr and said: What is this hadith I heard that you narrate, in which you diminish the right of Fatimah? 'Urwah said: I would not love to have what is between the east and the west if it meant I diminish Fatimah of a right that is hers; but henceforth, I owe it to you that I shall never narrate it again.} Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith there is something requiring reflection and attention to its meaning: the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Zayd ibn Harithah, 'Will you not go and bring Zaynab?', while Zayd was neither her mahram nor her husband, though he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had prohibited a woman from traveling except with a mahram. Reports have been narrated from him on that, some unrestricted without mention of a known duration for that travel, some mentioning the extent of that travel in time, and some with the exception 'unless her husband or a mahram of hers is with her.' We shall mention this chapter and what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on it later in this book, Allah willing. But we reflected on what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in this hadith in permitting Zayd to travel with Zaynab, and we found that Zayd was then under the Messenger of Allah's adoption of him, such that he was called Zayd ibn Muhammad, and he remained so until Allah abrogated that, removing him from his sonship and returning him to his true father by His saying: {Muhammad is not the father of any of your men, but the Messenger of Allah and the seal of the prophets}, and by His saying regarding Zayd and his likes among the adopted: {Call them by their fathers; that is more just with Allah, but if you do not know their fathers, then they are your brothers in religion and your freed allies}, and by His saying: {Nor has He made your claimed sons your sons}, and by what was revealed concerning Zayd specifically permitting the marriage of Zaynab bint Jahsh, who had previously been Zayd's wife, and by what was revealed on that: {So when Zayd had fulfilled his need of her, We married her to you} up to His saying {need}. So we concluded that what he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had commanded Zayd before that concerning Zaynab, and his permitting her and him to travel each with the other, was according to the first ruling, in the state when Zayd was a brother to Zaynab, and thus her mahram, for whom traveling with her was permissible, just as a blood brother of hers traveling with her would be permissible. This is the explanation of this aspect of this hadith, and Allah knows best. As for what is mentioned in it of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preferring Zaynab over his other daughters, that was at a time when he had no other daughter deserving virtue besides her, due to her faith in him and following him, and due to what befell her body for his sake as we mentioned. Then afterwards, among what Allah granted him and delighted his eye with in his daughter Fatimah was His guiding her to pure righteous deeds, and what He granted her of children who became his offspring and progeny, which none of his other daughters shared with her. Before that, at the time when Zaynab deserved the virtue she deserved, Fatimah was a minor not yet adult, among those for whom no reward runs for obedience nor punishment for disobedience. The proof of that is her young age then and her falling short of puberty."
"153","As Ahmad ibn Sahl al-Razi narrated to us: Abu 'Abdullah narrated to us, Musa ibn 'Abdullah ibn Musa ibn 'Abdullah ibn Hasan ibn Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn Abi Talib narrated to us, my father 'Abdullah ibn Musa narrated to me, my father Musa ibn 'Abdullah narrated to me, from his father 'Abdullah ibn Hasan who said: Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri and I entered upon 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, and he asked him about the age of Fatimah, and Ibn Shihab hastened to answer that before me, so I said to him: Ask this one about his mother, and ask me about my mother. Then I said to him: Her age - meaning that at which she died - was twenty-five years. Then we examined the time in which her death occurred, at what time it was."
"154","143 - So we found that Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb had narrated to us, saying: My uncle 'Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us; and Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us; then they both concurred, each one of them saying: Al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from 'Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'A'ishah who informed him that Fatimah bint Rasul Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to Abu Bakr asking him for her inheritance from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Madinah and Fadak, and what remained of the fifth of Khaybar. Abu Bakr said to her: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity. The family of Muhammad only eat from this wealth. By Allah, I shall not change anything of the charity of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from the state it was upon during the life of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I shall act in it as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) acted. So Abu Bakr refused to give Fatimah anything of it, and Fatimah was angry with Abu Bakr over that and boycotted him, not speaking to him until she died. She lived after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) six months. When she died, her husband 'Ali ibn Abi Talib buried her at night and did not inform Abu Bakr of her, and 'Ali prayed over her. Abu Ja'far said: Then there was, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), his clarifying to people the superiority of Fatimah over his other daughters, and over the rest of the believing women besides her and besides them."
"155","144 - As Bakkar narrated to us: Abu Dawud, the author of al-Tayalisah, narrated to us; and as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Yahya ibn Hammad narrated to us; then they concurred, Bakkar saying: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, and Ibrahim saying: Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, from Firas, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, 'A'ishah narrated to me: {The women had gathered at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), not one of them absent, when Fatimah came walking, her gait not missing the gait of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When he saw her he welcomed her and said: Welcome to my daughter, and he took her and seated her at his right, or at his left, then he whispered to her and she wept, then he whispered to her a second time and she laughed. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose, I said to her: You have, apart from his women, the distinction of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singling you out from among us with secrets, yet you weep! I adjure you by whatever right I have over you: why did you weep, and why did you laugh? She said: I would not disclose the secret of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, I said to her: I adjure you by whatever right I have over you to inform me. She said: As for now, yes. When he whispered to me the first time he said: Jibril used to review the Qur'an with me once every year, and this year he reviewed it with me twice, and I think only that my term has come, so fear Allah, and what an excellent predecessor I am for you. She said: So I wept the weeping you saw. Then he whispered to me the second time and said: Are you not pleased to be the mistress of this ummah, or the mistress of the believing women? She said: So I laughed.}"
"156","145 - And as Fahd narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from Firas, from al-Sha'bi, from 'A'ishah who said: She came walking - meaning Fatimah - as if her gait was the gait of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he mentioned the rest of this hadith just as in the hadith of Bakkar and Ibrahim, and he did not mention what is in their hadith before that."
"157","146 - And as Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, saying: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, from Nafi' ibn Yazid, Ibn Ghaziyyah - meaning 'Umarah - narrated to me, from Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr ibn 'Uthman: that his mother Fatimah bint al-Husayn narrated to him that 'A'ishah used to say: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the illness in which he was taken, to Fatimah: O my little daughter, bend over me. So she bent over him and he confided to her for a while, then she moved away from him weeping, with 'A'ishah present. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said after that, after a while: Bend over me, O my little daughter. So she bent over him and he confided to her for a while, then she moved away from him laughing. 'A'ishah said: O my little daughter, what did your father confide to you? Fatimah said: Am I quick to disclose it? He confided to me in confidence, then do you think I would inform you of his secret while he is alive? That was hard upon 'A'ishah, that there should be a secret excluding her. When Allah took him, 'A'ishah said to Fatimah: Will you not inform me of that report? She said: As for now, yes. He confided to me the first time and informed me that Jibril used to review the Qur'an with him once every year, and this year he reviewed it with him twice. And she informed me that he informed her that no prophet followed by another prophet lived but half the age of the one before him, and he informed me that 'Isa lived one hundred and twenty years, and I see myself only departing at sixty, and that made me weep. And he said: O my little daughter, there is no woman among the Muslim women with a greater calamity than you, so do not be the least patient of women. Then he confided to me the second time and informed me that I am the first of his family to join him, and he said: You are the mistress of the women of Paradise, except for al-Batul Maryam bint 'Imran, so I laughed at that.}"
"158","147 - And as 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah, Abu al-Hasan, narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Ma'in narrated to us, 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from Qatadah, from Anas: {That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sufficient for you among the women of the worlds are: Maryam bint 'Imran, Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, Fatimah bint Muhammad, and Asiyah the wife of Pharaoh.}"
"159","148 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: 'Ali ibn 'Uthman al-Lahiqi al-Basri narrated to us, Dawud ibn Abi al-Furat narrated to us, from 'Alba ibn Ahmar, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas who said: {The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drew four lines, then said: Do you know what this is? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: The best women of Paradise are: Khadijah bint Khuwaylid, Fatimah bint Muhammad, Maryam bint 'Imran, and Asiyah bint Muzahim the wife of Pharaoh.}"
"160","149 - And as Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us: Muthanna ibn Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh narrated to us, Layth ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us - Mubarak ibn Fudalah said: he narrated to us from al-Hasan - 'Imran ibn Husayn said: {I went out one day and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said to me: O 'Imran, Fatimah is ill; would you like to visit her? He said: I said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, and what honor is nobler than this? He said: Go. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) set out and I set out with him until he came to the door. He said: Peace be upon you, may I enter? She said: And upon you, enter. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I and whoever is with me? She said: By the One who sent you with the truth, I have nothing on but this cloak. With the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was a worn sheet, so he threw it to her and said to her: Wrap it over your head. She did so, then she said: Enter. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, and I entered with him. He sat at her head and I sat near him. He said: O my little daughter, how do you find yourself? She said: By Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I am in pain, and it adds pain to my pain that I have nothing to eat. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wept, Fatimah wept, and I wept with them both. He said to her: O my little daughter, be patient, two or three times, then he said to her: O my little daughter, are you not pleased to be the mistress of the women of the worlds? She said: Would that she had died - and where is Maryam bint 'Imran? He said to her: O my little daughter, she is the mistress of the women of her world, and you are the mistress of the women of your world. By the One who sent me with the truth, I have married you to a master in this world, a master in the Hereafter; none hates him but a hypocrite.} Abu Ja'far said: In what we have narrated is evidence that the age of Fatimah at the time the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) arrived in Madinah and commanded Zayd to go to Zaynab and bring her to him was a dozen or so years, an age at which she may well not have reached puberty. And we understood, from what we narrated of the report of 'A'ishah about the time in which she died, that it was six months after the death of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); that indicated that her puberty and the binding of rulings upon her came after what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Zayd concerning Zaynab. Then what Allah bestowed upon Fatimah of virtue, as we mentioned, necessitates her superiority over Zaynab and over others whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preferred in the reports we narrated in this chapter. If someone says: It has been narrated, in the mention of those whom the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) preferred and described as perfect among women, women whom he mentioned and Fatimah is not among them. And he mentioned on that:"
"161","150 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Qabisah narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from Murrah, i.e. Ibn Sharahil, from Abu Musa who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Many men have attained perfection, and of women none attained perfection but Maryam bint 'Imran and Asiyah the wife of Pharaoh; and the superiority of 'A'ishah over women is like the superiority of tharid over other food.} It was said to him: It is possible that what is in this hadith came before the puberty of Fatimah and her attaining the rank which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned of her. So all that we narrated in this chapter returns, by Allah's praise, to there being no contradiction in it, and no necessitating of uncovering its meanings beyond what was mentioned as necessitating it; and every virtue mentioned for other than Fatimah, of what Fatimah may have surpassed, is possibly from when she was then small, then she grew up afterwards and attained the station Allah placed her in and mentioned her with, and singled her out with on the tongue of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And Allah we ask for guidance."
"162","Chapter 24 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his commanding 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah to eat from what is in front of him of food, apart from the rest of it, and what else enters into this meaning. 151 - Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus al-Kufi, Abu Ja'far known as al-Susi, narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from Hisham ibn 'Urwah, from Abu Wajzah, from a man of Muzaynah, from 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah who said: {I entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was eating in my mother's house, and he said: Sit, O my son, mention Allah's name, eat with your right hand, and eat from what is in front of you. He said: So my way of eating thereafter never changed.}"
"163","152 - Fahd ibn Sulayman narrated to us: Hisham ibn 'Abd al-Wahid narrated to us, Yazid ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us, from Hisham, from Abu Wajzah, from a neighbor of 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah, from 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah, then he mentioned the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: This hadith with us is of corrupt chain, since it is from the narration of the neighbor of 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah, who was not named to us in it and whom we do not know. So we sought it from the narration of other than Abu Mu'awiyah and other than Yazid ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz, from Hisham."
"164","153 - So we found that Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, saying: 'Abdullah ibn al-Sabbah ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, saying: 'Abd al-A'la, i.e. Ibn 'Abd al-A'la, narrated to us, saying: Ma'mar narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, from 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah {that he entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while there was food with him, and he said: Come near, O my son, mention the name of Allah, eat with your right hand, and eat from what is in front of you.} Abu Ja'far said: The outward of this hadith, were it not for what opposed it of what we narrated before it, would be sound in chain; but since it was opposed in chain by what we narrated before it, it counterbalanced it, and their mutual inconsistency became necessary because of that. Then we sought it from other than the hadith of Hisham."
"165","154 - So we found that Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Makhlad al-Qatawani narrated to us, saying: Malik narrated to us, from Abu Nu'aym Wahb ibn Kaysan, from 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: {Mention Allah's name, and eat from what is in front of you.}"
"166","155 - And we found that Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud had narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Salih al-Wuhazi narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, from 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah {that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Come near, mention Allah's name, eat with your right hand, and eat from what is in front of you.} This hadith was of fair chain, except that we found it from the narration of Ibn Wahb, from Malik, in his Muwatta, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, as a stopped report."
"167","156 - As Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Malik narrated to him, from Wahb ibn Kaysan, that he said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought food, and with him was his stepson 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Mention Allah's name, and eat from what is in front of you.} Then we sought it from other than the hadith of Malik from Wahb."
"168","157 - So we found that Rawh ibn al-Faraj Abu al-Zinba' had narrated to us, saying: Hamid ibn Yahya al-Balkhi narrated to us, Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Kathir al-Madini narrated to us that he heard Wahb ibn Kaysan say: I heard 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah say: {I was an orphan boy in the care of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I ate with him, and my hand used to wander around the dish. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O boy, when you eat, mention Allah's name; when you eat, eat with your right hand; and when you eat, eat from what is in front of you. He said: So that remained my way of eating thereafter.}"
"169","158 - And we found that Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, saying: Muhammad ibn Mansur al-Jawwaz narrated to us, Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Kathir narrated to us, saying: I heard Wahb ibn Kaysan say: I heard 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah say: {I was a boy in the care of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and my hand used to wander around the dish, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O boy, mention Allah's name, eat with your right hand, and eat from what is in front of you.} So the chain of this hadith became established for us from this route. Then we examined, after that, a hadith narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that enters into this meaning."
"170","159 - And it is what Abu Umayyah had narrated to us: Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from 'Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Blessing is in the middle of the bowl, so eat from its sides and do not eat from its top.}"
"171","160 - And we found that Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, saying: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, 'Ata ibn al-Sa'ib informed us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr - and he did not mention Ibn 'Abbas - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Eat from the bottom of the dish, for blessing descends upon its top.} So al-Thawri and Hammad ibn Salamah differed over 'Ata'; both are proof regarding him in the chain of this hadith: al-Thawri connected it, and Hammad stopped it at Ibn Jubayr."
"172","161 - And we found that Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah had narrated to us, saying: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, saying: Hammam ibn Yahya narrated to us, from 'Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Eat from the edges of the bowl, for blessing descends from its middle.} Abu Ja'far said: We only included in this chapter what Hammam narrated from 'Ata', even though those whom the people of knowledge of chains count as proof regarding 'Ata ibn al-Sa'ib are only four to the exclusion of others: Shu'bah, al-Thawri, Hammad ibn Zayd and Hammad ibn Salamah; because Hammam's hearing from 'Ata' was at Basra when he came to them there, and Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, when 'Ata' came to them there, had said to people: Go to him and ask him about his hadith from his father from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr about tasbih after prayer. As Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, saying: 'Ata ibn al-Sa'ib came to us at Basra, and Ayyub said to us: Go to him and ask him about the hadith of tasbih. Al-Qawariri said: meaning the hadith of his father from 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr. Abu Ja'far said: So Hammam's hearing from him, since it was at Basra, became strong in our hearts, because his confusion only came after his return to Kufah. And we examined this hadith of Ibn 'Abbas: does it contradict the hadith of 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah which we narrated from him? Since in the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas is {Eat from the sides of the dish}, nothing was found in that necessitating contradiction of 'Umar's hadith, since his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) {Eat from the sides of the dish} may mean that each one of you eats from his own side of its sides, not going beyond it to other sides. It may also mean that what is in this hadith of Ibn 'Abbas is intended for eating alone, not eating with others, since reaching beyond what is in front of him of the bowl he eats with others from is bad manners toward them; whereas when eating alone, there is no bad manners toward anyone in eating from wherever he eats of the dish except its middle. Then we examined what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter besides these two hadiths: is there in it anything indicating something of what we sought in the hadith of Ibn 'Abbas of the two?"
"173","162 - So we found that Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb had narrated to us, saying: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Malik ibn Anas, from Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah: that he heard Anas ibn Malik say: {A tailor invited the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to food he had made. Anas said: So I went with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to that food, and he served the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) barley bread and dried meat containing pumpkin. Anas said: So I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) seeking out the pumpkin from around the dish, and I have loved pumpkin ever since that day.} In this hadith is mention of {the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eating from other than what was in front of him of the bowl in which he was eating that food.} By that we understood that what is in the hadith of 'Umar ibn Abi Salamah of {the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding him to eat from other than what was in front of him of the bowl in which he was eating with him} was only for eating with others, and that what is in the hadith of Anas ibn Malik of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) eating from other than what was in front of him of the bowl in which he was eating was only for eating alone. By that, all that we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter was freed from contradiction, and we understood that it is upon two meanings, each one contrary to the other. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"174","Chapter 25 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what Jabir narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): that he sought permission from him and said to him, 'Who is this?' So Jabir said, 'I.' And the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him, 'I, I,' as if he disliked that. 163 - 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from {Jabir ibn 'Abdullah who said: I sought permission from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Who is this? I said: I. He said: I, I, as if he disliked that.}"
"175","164 - And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Bishr ibn 'Umar al-Zahrani and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, saying: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from {Jabir who said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) regarding a debt upon my father, and I knocked on the door. He said: Who is it? I said: I. He said: I, I, as if he disliked that.} Abu Ja'far said: The meaning of this, Allah knows best, is that when Jabir knocked on the door, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him 'Who is this?' since he did not recognize him, so that he might recognize him; Jabir answered him with what he answered, by which he did not recognize him. So his question to him 'Who is this?' required an answer which had not come from Jabir until then. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked that from him and wanted from him an answer that would inform him of who had knocked on the door. And with Allah is guidance."
"176","Chapter 26 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibiting sitting on the roadsides, and his permitting that upon the conditions he stipulated in permitting it. 165 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Sinan al-Harawi narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Jarir ibn Hazim who said: I heard Ishaq ibn Suwayd narrating from Ibn Hujayr al-'Adawi who said: I heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab say: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came upon us while we were sitting on the road, and he said: Beware of sitting on these roads, for they are the sittings of Satan. If you must, then give the road its right. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went on, and I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, 'Give the road its right,' but I did not ask him what it is. So I caught up with him and said: O Messenger of Allah, you said such-and-such; what is the right of the road? He said: The right of the road is to return greetings, lower the gaze, remove harm, guide the lost, and help the distressed.}"
"177","166 - And Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, saying: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Suwayd, from Yahya ibn Ya'mar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this hadith with a broken chain as we mentioned, and without the speech that is in the hadith of Yazid ibn Sinan."
"178","167 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, 'Uthman ibn Hakim narrated to us, Ishaq ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Talhah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, saying: Abu Talhah said: {We were sitting in the courtyards when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by us and said: What is with you and these roadside sittings? We said: We gathered for no wrong, to remind and converse. He said: Then give the sittings their right. They said: And what is their right, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Lowering the gaze, returning greetings, and good speech.}"
"179","168 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us: al-Aswad ibn 'Amir narrated to us, Huraym ibn Sufyan al-Bajali narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn Sa'id al-Maqburi, from his father, from Abu Shurayh al-Khuza'i, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: {Beware of sitting on the roadsides; whoever sits on a road, let him give it its right. They said: And what is its right, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Lowering the gaze, returning greetings, and enjoining good and forbidding evil.}"
"180","169 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Hafs ibn Maysarah narrated to me, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Beware of sitting on the roads. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we must have our sittings in which we converse. The Messenger of Allah said: If you refuse but the sittings, then give the road its right. They said: And what is the right of the road, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Lowering the gaze, removing harm, returning greetings, and enjoining good and forbidding evil.}"
"181","170 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to me, saying: I heard al-Bara ibn 'Azib say: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by people of the Ansar and said: If you must do it, then spread greetings, help the wronged, and guide the way.}"
"182","171 - Fahd narrated to us: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Bara: {That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by some seated people of the Ansar and said: If you must do it...}, then he mentioned the like of it exactly, except that he said in it: Shu'bah said: Abu Ishaq did not hear this hadith from al-Bara. Abu Ja'far said: This is a severe disagreement over Shu'bah in this hadith, because Hajjaj mentions therein Abu Ishaq's hearing it from al-Bara, while Abu al-Walid denies that. And Allah knows best what is correct in it."
"183","172 - Fahd narrated to us: Abu Ghassan al-Nahdi Malik ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Isra'il ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara who said: {The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a gathering of the Ansar and said: If you refuse but to sit, then return greetings, guide the way, and help the wronged.} Abu Ja'far said: We reflected on what is in these reports and found in them the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) against sitting on the roadsides, then his permitting after that what he permitted of sitting therein upon the conditions he stipulated for those to whom he permitted it. By that we concluded that his prohibition of sitting therein was only upon sitting unaccompanied by the conditions he stipulated when permitting sitting therein for whoever preferred to sit therein; and that his permitting sitting therein is bound by the conditions he stipulated in permitting sitting therein for those to whom he permitted it. In that is evidence of the distinctness of his prohibition and his permission, and that each of them is for a meaning not in the other. And in these reports is evidence for people's permissibility to use of their public roads whatever causes no harm to any of their people. When that is so, it is intelligible that sitting therein, if it constricts passersby by the sitters constricting it, is not included in what he permitted of it, and that it returns to what is in the hadith of Sahl ibn Mu'adh al-Juhani from his father: {That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a caller in one of his expeditions, when people constricted the dwellings and cut off the roads, who called out that whoever constricts a dwelling or cuts off a road has no jihad.} We have mentioned this hadith earlier in this book. Those of understanding must comprehend from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what he addresses his ummah with, for he addresses them only to apprise them of the limits of their religion, the manners they employ therein, and the rulings they judge by therein; and to know that there is no contradiction in them, and that each meaning he addresses them with, whose wording differs from the wording he had addressed them with before of that kind of meaning - they should seek what is in each of those two meanings when it occurs in their hearts that there is contradiction or disagreement therein, and they will find it contrary to what they supposed; and if that is hidden from some of them, it is only due to the deficiency of his knowledge of it, not because there is in it what he supposed of contradiction or disagreement; for what Allah has undertaken is contrary to that, as He said: {Had it been from other than Allah, they would have found therein much discrepancy}. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"184","Chapter 27 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about which of His names is the Greatest Name. 173 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Aswad ibn 'Amir narrated to us, saying: Sharik ibn 'Abdullah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq and Malik ibn Mighwal, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father: {The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard a man saying: O Allah, I ask You by my bearing witness that there is no god but You, the One, the Eternal, who neither begets nor is begotten, and there is none like unto Him. So he said: He has asked Allah by His name which, when He is called by it He answers, and when He is asked by it He gives.}"
"185","174 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr al-Hamdani narrated to us, saying: Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to me, 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muslim narrated to me, from Ibrahim ibn 'Ubayd ibn Rifa'ah, from Anas who said: {The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a man praying, while he was saying: O Allah, to You is praise; there is no god but You, O Bestower, O Originator of the heavens and the earth, O Possessor of majesty and honor. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a group of his companions: Do you know what the man supplicated with? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: He supplicated his Lord by His Greatest Name, which when He is called by it He answers, and when He is asked by it He gives.}"
"186","175 - Fahd narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Hafs ibn 'Umar, from {Anas who said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a circle when a man stood praying; when he bowed, prostrated, sat and testified, he supplicated saying: O Allah, I ask You by Your praise being Yours; there is no god but You, Originator of the heavens and the earth, O Possessor of majesty and honor, O Living, O Sustainer. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know what he supplicated with? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: He supplicated by His Great Name, which when He is called by it He answers, and when He is asked by it He gives.} Abu Ja'far said: These reports have been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) agreeing that the Greatest Name of Allah is Allah, glorified and exalted. And something has been narrated from Abu Hanifah on this which we shall mention in this chapter."
"187","And it is what Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-'Abbas al-Razi permitted us, informing us that he heard it from Musa ibn Nasr al-Razi, and that Musa ibn Nasr narrated it to us from Hisham ibn 'Ubaydullah al-Razi who said: Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated to us, from Abu Hanifah who said: The Greatest Name of Allah is Allah. Muhammad said: Do you not see that al-Rahman is derived from mercy, and al-Rabb from lordship, and he mentioned things like this, while Allah is not derived from anything. Hisham ibn 'Ubaydullah al-Razi said: I do not know whether Muhammad explained this from his own saying or from the saying of Abu Hanifah. Then someone said: There has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), in other than these reports, what indicates contrary to what is in these reports, and he mentioned:"
"188","176 - As Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Shayzari narrated to us: Hisham ibn 'Ammar narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn al-'Ala narrated to us that he heard al-Qasim Abu 'Abd al-Rahman narrating from Abu Umamah, raising it, who said: {The Greatest Name of Allah, by which when He is called He answers, is in three surahs: al-Baqarah, Al 'Imran, and Ta-Ha.}"
"189","177 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, saying: Abu Hafs 'Amr ibn Abi Salamah al-Dimashqi narrated to us, saying: I heard 'Isa ibn Musa say to Ibn Zabr: O Abu Zabr, did you hear Ghaylan ibn Anas? He said: I heard al-Qasim Abu 'Abd al-Rahman narrating from Abu Umamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: {The Greatest Name of Allah is in three surahs of the Qur'an: al-Baqarah, Al 'Imran, and Ta-Ha.} Abu Hafs said: So I looked into these three surahs and saw in them things like which there is nothing in the Qur'an: Ayat al-Kursi: {Allah, there is no god but He, the Living, the Sustainer}, and in Al 'Imran: {Allah, there is no god but He, the Living, the Sustainer}, and in Ta-Ha: {And faces shall be humbled to the Living, the Sustainer}. Abu Ja'far said: Our answer to him on that, by Allah's guidance, is that what Abu Hafs extracted from Surah al-Baqarah contains 'Allah,' and what he extracted from Al 'Imran likewise contains 'Allah,' so that was not outside the reports we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter, nor contrary to what is in them. What he extracted of what is in Ta-Ha may be as he extracted, so it is established thereby that the Greatest Name of Allah is al-Hayy al-Qayyum; and it is possible that it is what is in Ta-Ha besides that, namely Allah's saying therein: {And if you speak aloud, He knows the secret and what is more hidden. Allah, there is no god but He} the verse - so what is in Ta-Ha returns to the like of what the contents of Surah al-Baqarah and Surah Al 'Imran returned to: that it is Allah. And there has been narrated from Asma bint Yazid al-Ansariyyah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that what contradicts the hadith from which Abu Hafs extracted what he extracted."
"190","178 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Makki ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from Shahr ibn Hawshab, from {Asma bint Yazid that she heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: In these two verses is the Greatest Name of Allah: {And your god is one God; there is no god but He}, and {Alif Lam Mim. Allah, there is no god but He, the Living, the Sustainer}.}"
"191","179 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Abu 'Asim al-Nabil narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Ziyad, from Shahr, from Asma: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ... the like of it. In these two hadiths the locus of Allah's name from Surah al-Baqarah and from Surah Al 'Imran is with what contains in neither of them mention of al-Hayy al-Qayyum, while in both of them together is Allah. So in that is what necessitates understanding that what is in Surah Ta-Ha is that also, not what Abu Hafs mentioned. And in what we mentioned is what agreed with what Abu Hanifah held. Their saying 'Allahumma' was originally 'Ya Allah'; when they dropped the Ya from the beginning of the word, they added the mim at its end so that the meaning in 'Ya Allah' returns. And in what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), parts confirm parts, and disagreement is removed from it."
"192","Chapter 28 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his supplication: O Allah, strengthen my weakness in Your obedience. 180 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, saying: 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Ma'ni narrated to us, saying: Mindal ibn 'Ali narrated to us, from al-'Ala ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Dawud al-Hamdani, from Buraydah who said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Shall I not teach you words which whoever Allah intends good for, He teaches them to him, then he never forgets them: O Allah, I am weak, so strengthen my weakness in Your pleasure, take my forelock to good, make Islam the utmost of my pleasure. O Allah, I am weak, so strengthen me; I am lowly, so honor me; I am poor, so enrich me.}"
"193","181 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us: 'Asim ibn 'Ali ibn 'Asim narrated to us, Mindal ibn 'Ali narrated to us, al-'Ala ibn al-Musayyab narrated to us, from Abu Dawud al-Hamdani, from Buraydah al-Aslami, then he mentioned the like of it except that he said: Then {he never asked Him for them ever.} We reflected on what is in these two hadiths from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and found that weakness never becomes strength, and strength never becomes weakness, because each is the opposite of its counterpart, and a thing is never the opposite of itself, only of another. Weakness and strength do not stand by themselves; they exist as states in the bodies of living beings, of the sons of Adam and others, so what weakness settles in of them becomes weak, and what strength settles in of them becomes strong. By that we understood that his supplication (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Allah to make his weakness strong means, Allah knows best, that He make what weakness is in of him - his body - strong. This is the best we found in interpreting this hadith, and Allah we ask for guidance."
"194","Chapter 29 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: When one of you prostrates, let him not kneel as the camel kneels, but let him place his hands then his knees. 182 - Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Amr ibn al-Harith al-Ansari narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad al-Darawardi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn al-Hasan narrated to me, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {When one of you prostrates, let him not kneel as the camel kneels, but let him place his hands, then his knees.} Someone said: This is impossible speech, because he prohibited him, when prostrating, from kneeling as the camel kneels, while the camel descends upon its hands, then he followed that by saying {but let him place his hands before his knees} - so what is prohibited in the beginning of this hadith is commanded at its end. We reflected on what he said of that and found it absurd, but found what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith sound, with no absurdity in it: the camel's knees are in its forelegs, as with every four-legged animal, while the sons of Adam are otherwise, for their knees are in their legs, not in their hands. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this hadith prohibited the worshipper from falling upon his knees that are in his legs, as the camel falls upon its knees that are in its forelegs; rather he should fall in prostration otherwise, falling upon his hands in which his knees are not, unlike the camel's falling upon its forelegs in which its knees are. It became clear, by Allah's praise and favor, that what is in this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is sound speech with no contradiction nor impossibility in it. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"195","Chapter 30 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The sun and the moon are two folded bulls in the Fire on the Day of Resurrection. 183 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: Mu'alla ibn Asad al-'Ammi narrated to us, saying: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar narrated to us, from 'Abdullah al-Danaj who said: I witnessed Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman sitting in a mosque in the time of Khalid ibn 'Abdullah ibn Khalid ibn Asid. He said: Al-Hasan came and sat with him, and they conversed. Abu Salamah said: Abu Hurayrah narrated to us, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: {The sun and the moon are two folded bulls on the Day of Resurrection.} Al-Hasan said: What is their sin? He said: I only narrate to you from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and al-Hasan fell silent. What al-Hasan did in this hadith - objecting to Abu Salamah - was, Allah knows best, only because it occurred to his heart that they are cast into the Fire to be punished thereby, but Abu Salamah had no answer to him about that. Our answer to him on that, on behalf of Abu Salamah: the sun and the moon are only folded in the Fire to punish the people of the Fire, not to be punished in the Fire; they are, in punishing those in the Fire, like the rest of Allah's angels who punish its people. Do you not see His saying: {O you who believe, guard yourselves and your families against a Fire whose fuel is men and stones; over it are angels stern and severe who disobey not Allah in what He commands them} - i.e., in punishing the people of the Fire - {and do what they are commanded.} Likewise the sun and the moon are therein in this station, punishing the people of the Fire for their sins, not punished therein, since they have no sins. And there has been narrated from Anas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning the sun and the moon, this meaning also, with the addition that they are hamstrung."
"196","184 - Ishaq ibn Ibrahim al-Baghdadi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Salih al-Qurashi narrated to us - Abu Ja'far said: he is the one called Ibn al-Nattah, whose clientage is attributed to Ja'far ibn Sulayman al-Hashimi - Durust ibn Ziyad al-Qushayri narrated to us, Yazid narrated to us - Abu Ja'far said: he is al-Raqashi - Anas narrated to us, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The sun and the moon are two hamstrung bulls in the Fire.} Abu Ja'far said: The meaning of the hamstringing mentioned as theirs in this hadith, according to the people of knowledge of language, was not intended as hamstringing for them as punishment for them, since that is not permissible regarding them, as they were in this world in Allah's worship as He mentioned them in His Book by His saying: {Have you not seen that to Allah prostrate whoever is in the heavens and whoever is on earth, and the sun and the moon}, mentioning with them whom He mentioned with them in this verse, until reaching His saying therein: {But many deserve punishment.} So He informed that His punishment only becomes due upon other than those who prostrate to Him in this world. Rather they were in this world swimming in the orbit in which they swam, as He said: {It is not for the sun to overtake the moon} the verse. Then on the Day of Resurrection He returned them entrusted with the Fire like others of His angels entrusted with it, cutting them off thereby from what they were in of swimming in this world, so by their being cut off from that they became like the crippled by hamstringing; so they were called 'hamstrung' by borrowing this name for them, not upon the reality of hamstringing befalling them. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"197","Chapter 31 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Evil is the mount of a man, 'they claim.' 185 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Maymun al-Baghdadi Abu Bakr narrated to us: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, Abu Qilabah narrated to me, Abu 'Abdullah narrated to me, saying: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Evil is the mount of a man, 'they claim.'"
"198","186 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilabah who said: Abu Mas'ud said to Abu 'Abdullah, or Abu 'Abdullah said to Abu Mas'ud: Did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say regarding 'they claim': {Evil is the mount of a man.} Abu Ja'far said: We reflected on what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in describing 'they claim' with what he described it, and his mentioning it as evil mount of a man, and we found that 'they claim' (za'ama) never came in the Qur'an except in reports about blameworthy people for blameworthy things that were from them. Of that is Allah's saying: {Those who disbelieve claim they shall never be raised}, then He followed that with His saying: {Say: Yes, by my Lord, you shall surely be raised, then you shall surely be informed of what you did.} Of that is His saying: {Say: Call upon those you claimed besides Him}, then He followed that with informing of their inability when they call them by His saying: {They possess no power to remove harm from you nor to divert it.} Of that is His saying: {And we see not with you your intercessors whom you claimed were partners concerning you}, then He rebutted them with His saying: {Assuredly all ties between you are cut} the verse. Of that is His saying: {And they assigned to Allah of crops and cattle a portion, saying: This is for Allah, by their claim.} Of that is His saying: {And they say: These cattle and crops are forbidden; none shall eat them but whom we will, by their claim.} Of that is His saying: {Where are your partners whom you used to claim?} Of that is His saying: {Have you not seen those who claim they believe in what was revealed to you and what was revealed before you} the verse. All these things are reports from Allah about blameworthy people in blameworthy states of theirs, with sayings from them in which they were lying, fabricating against Allah. So it was disliked for anyone to adhere to the morals of the blameworthy in their morals, the disbelievers in their religions, the liars in their sayings. And worthier for the people of faith is to adhere to the morals of the believers who preceded them in faith, and what they were upon of praiseworthy ways and truthful sayings for which Allah praised them. May Allah's pleasure and mercy be upon them. And with Allah is guidance."
"199","Chapter 32 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his commanding whoever owes his brother a wrong in honor or wealth to seek his absolution in this world. 187 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to me, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id al-Maqburi, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever has a wrong against his brother, of his honor or his wealth, let him seek his absolution before it is taken from him when there will be neither dinar nor dirham. If he has righteous deeds, they will be taken from him according to his wrong; otherwise, his companion's evil deeds will be taken and loaded upon him.}"
"200","188 - Al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us: Khalid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"201","189 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: And Malik narrated to me: Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever has with him a wrong against his brother in honor or in wealth, let him go to him and have him absolve him of it, for there is neither dinar nor dirham there, before it is taken for his brother from his good deeds; and if he has no good deeds, his brother's evil deeds will be taken and cast upon him.} Abu Ja'far said: We reflected on this hadith. What is in Ibn Abi Dhi'b's narration of it - {Whoever has a wrong against his brother, of his honor or his wealth, let him seek his absolution} - its meaning with us, Allah knows best, is: let him seek his absolution with what such is absolved with: payment of wealth in place of wealth, and pardon for a due punishment for violating his honor, because such violation necessitates punishment upon the violator in his body - like a man saying to a man: O evildoer, or O wicked one, or O thief, without proof establishing for him that he is so; upon that speaker is punishment, and pardon of it belongs to the one to whom that punishment is due. There is no disagreement among the people of knowledge on that, and that is the absolution intended from this punishment, Allah knows best. In Malik's hadith, in place of that is 'let him go to him and have him absolve him of it' - that is upon going to the one owed the wrong, not upon going to the one upon whom it is. That is far in meaning, for the one owed the wrong is not feared for from it in the Hereafter; fear in the Hereafter is only upon the one upon whom it is. By what we mentioned it became clear that the worthier of what Malik and Ibn Abi Dhi'b differed in this hadith is what Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated of it, not what Malik narrated of it. Then we returned to what is in both their hadiths of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: {Before it is taken from him when there will be neither dinar nor dirham; if he has righteous deeds, they will be taken from him according to his wrong, otherwise his companion's evil deeds will be taken and loaded upon him} - that with us, Allah knows best, returns upon the wrong in wealth, not upon the wrong in honor, because the wrong in wealth necessitates wealth, namely dinars and dirhams; when they cannot be obtained, the owner of the wrong returns in his right by his wrong to the good deeds of his wrongdoer, taken from them according to his wrong; and if he has no good deeds, his evil deeds are taken and cast upon his wrongdoer according to his wrong. Not so the wrong in honor, for what is due by it is punishment in the wrongdoer's body by flogging him for it, and that can be obtained in the Hereafter from his body as it could be obtained from him in this world. And of what strengthens what we said on that:"
"202","190 - As Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad, i.e. Ibn 'A'ishah, narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Nu'aym, from Abu Hurayrah who said: Abu al-Qasim, the Prophet of Repentance (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: {Whoever slanders his slave with adultery, while he is innocent of what he said to him, a hadd will be carried out upon him on the Day of Resurrection, unless it is as he said.}"
"203","191 - And as 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, 'Abdullah, i.e. Ibn al-Mubarak, narrated to us, from Fudayl ibn Ghazwan, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Nu'm al-Bajali, from Abu Hurayrah who said: Abu al-Qasim, the Prophet of Repentance (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), said: {Whoever slanders his slave with adultery, while he is innocent of what he said, the hadd will be carried out upon him on the Day of Resurrection, unless it is as he said.}"
"204","192 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Suwayd ibn Nasr narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from al-Fudayl ibn Ghazwan, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, but did not say: with adultery."
"205","193 - And as Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya, i.e. Ibn Sa'id, narrated to us, from Fudayl ibn Ghazwan, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Nu'm, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever slanders a slave, while he is innocent of what he said, he will be flogged on the Day of Resurrection, unless it is as he said.} Abu Ja'far said: The slave in this world was unable to carry out the hadd upon his slanderer, whether his master or another, due to the bondage in him; when Allah removed it from him in the Hereafter and returned him to the rulings of other sons of Adam deserving hadds upon their slanderers, the meaning that prevented him from taking it in this world departed, so he takes it for him in the Hereafter as he would have taken it in this world had taking it been open to him therein. If someone says: The address in the hadith of absolution from backbiting which you narrated came with the wrong in honor and wealth together, so how is it permissible for part of the conjoined speech to return upon part of what was begun with to the exclusion of the rest? It is said to him: The Arabs do this often: they address with something following the mention of two things, intending by their address one of those two things together. Of that is Allah's saying: {He released the two seas meeting; between them is a barrier they do not transgress}, then He said: {From them emerge pearls and coral}, though they emerge from only one of them. Of that is His saying: {O assembly of jinn and men, did there not come to you messengers from among you}, though the messengers were only from men, not from jinn. Of that is what is narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him):"
"206","194 - Of what Yunus narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Idris, from 'Ubadah who said: {We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a gathering and he said: Pledge to me that you shall not associate anything with Allah ... the verse. Whoever of you fulfills, his reward is upon Allah; whoever commits something and is punished for it, it is expiation for him; and whoever commits something of that and Allah conceals it for him, his affair is with Allah: if He wills He punishes him, and if He wills He has mercy on him.} Abu Ja'far said: We know that whoever associates partners with Allah and is punished for his polytheism, that punishment is not expiation for him, for Allah says: {Allah does not forgive that partners be associated with Him, and forgives what is besides that for whom He wills}; and that if he is not punished and is concealed for, he is not of those for whom Allah may forgive. So his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) {whoever commits something of that} is only upon some of those things, not all of them. Likewise his saying about transferring some of the wrongdoer's good deeds to the wronged, and transferring some of the wronged's evil deeds to the wrongdoer, is not regarding wrong in honors, but regarding wrong in wealth, not wrong in honors. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"207","Chapter 33 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who kills himself deliberately: may he be forgiven or not? 195 - Al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad and Malik ibn Anas narrated to me, from Abu al-Zinad, from 'Abd al-Rahman, i.e. Ibn Hurmuz al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The one who strangles himself strangles himself in the Fire; the one who stabs himself stabs himself in the Fire; and the one who pierces himself pierces himself in the Fire.}"
"208","196 - Fahd narrated to us: 'Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath al-Nakha'i narrated to us, my father narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, Abu Salih narrated to us, Abu Hurayrah narrated to us, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: {Whoever kills himself with an iron, his iron is in his hand in the Fire of Hell, thrusting it into his belly in the Fire of Hell, abiding eternally therein forever; whoever throws himself from a mountain and kills himself is falling in Hell, abiding eternally therein forever; and whoever kills himself with poison has his poison in his hand, sipping it in the Fire of Hell, abiding eternally therein forever.}"
"209","197 - Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Zayd al-Makki narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Qawwas narrated to us, 'Abd al-Majid ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj who said: Fafah informed me, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: This Fafah is a man of Kufah, of the people of the Qur'an, named Isma'il ibn Ziyad. Then someone said: There has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a hadith contradicting this, then he mentioned:"
"210","198 - As Abu Umayyah, Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud, and Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad al-Baghdadi Abu Bakr narrated to us, saying: Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Hajjaj al-Sawwaf, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir {that al-Tufayl ibn 'Amr al-Dawsi came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, will you take a fortified fortress - with him was a fortress belonging to Daws in Jahiliyyah - but the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) refused that due to what was reserved for the Ansar. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) emigrated to Madinah, al-Tufayl ibn 'Amr emigrated to him, and a man emigrated with him, but they disliked Madinah's climate. He fell ill and panicked, so he took his arrows and cut his finger joints with them; his hands bled profusely until he died. Al-Tufayl ibn 'Amr saw him in a dream in a good state, but covering his hands. He said to him: What did your Lord do with you? He said: He forgave me for my emigration to His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Why do I see you covering your hands? He said: It was said to me: We shall never repair for you what you spoiled. Al-Tufayl recounted it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, forgive his hands too.} Our answer to him on that, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that it is possible that the man mentioned in this hadith did to himself what he did of what is mentioned therein upon his considering it a treatment by which the remainder of his hands would survive - so he did what he did that his soul might be saved and the remainder of his hands remain; thus he was not blameworthy in that, like a man afflicted in his hand with something, fearing that if he does not cut it off the rest of his body will go with it and destroy his soul - he is at liberty to cut it. If he does not cut it, seeing thereby his remaining body would be saved and his soul safe, then dies of it, he is not blamed in that nor punished for it. Likewise this man in what he did to his finger joints until his soul perished by his act - contrary to one who kills himself stabbing it, or throwing himself from place to place to destroy his soul, or sipping poison to kill himself with it. So by Allah's praise there appeared no contradiction nor disagreement in what we narrated in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). If someone says: In this hadith the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated for this man's hands for forgiveness, and his supplication for his hands thereby is supplication for him, and that can only be for an offense from him against his hands deserving punishment, for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated forgiveness for his hands, making it forgiveness for him. It is said to him: There is no evidence in this hadith for what you mentioned, for it is possible that what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did of that supplication for that man's hands was out of compassion for him and the fear of Allah at work in his heart, so he supplicated for him thereby for this meaning, not for other than it - as has been narrated from him of what he taught Husayn al-Khuza'i, the father of 'Imran ibn Husayn, and commanded him to supplicate with."
"211","199 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bishr al-'Abdi narrated to us, Ibn Abi Za'idah informed us, Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir narrated to us, saying: Rib'i ibn Hirash narrated to us, from 'Imran ibn Husayn: {That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught his father Husayn after he embraced Islam, saying: Say: O Allah, forgive me what I concealed and what I disclosed, what I erred in, what I intended, what I was ignorant of, and what I knew.} In this hadith is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) teaching Husayn to supplicate Allah to forgive him what he erred in - meaning error contrary to intent - which is not taken against him nor punished for, for Allah said: {There is no blame upon you for what you erred in, but only for what your hearts intended.} Error unaccompanied by hearts' intent is pardoned, its doer not taken for it. The Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanding Husayn to supplicate Allah for His forgiving it to him was upon reverence for Allah, veneration of Him, and fear of what may mingle in the erring one's heart at the time of his error, of inclination to what he erred in. Likewise what is in Jabir's hadith of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplication of forgiveness for the man mentioned therein may also be for the like of this. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"212","Chapter 34 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning his sending Muhammad ibn Maslamah to kill Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf, repelling contradiction from what some people supposed contradicted what is in it. 200 - Yunus narrated to us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from 'Amr, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Who is for Ka'b, for he has harmed Allah and His Messenger? Muhammad ibn Maslamah stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, do you wish that I kill him? He said: Yes. He said: Then permit me to say something. He said: Say.} He said: {So he came to him and said: This man has asked us for charity and burdened us, and I have come to you to borrow from you. He said: And by Allah, you shall surely tire of him. He said: We have followed him, and we dislike leaving him until we see what his affair comes to. He said: What will you pledge to me? They said: What do you want from us? He said: Pledge to me your women. They said: You are the most handsome of the Arabs; how shall we pledge you our women? They refused and he refused. They said: That would be a disgrace upon us. He said: Pledge to me your children. They said: Glory be to Allah - the son of one of us would be reviled, it being said: He was pledged for a wasq or two wasqs. They said: We shall pledge you our weapons. He said: You mean arms. So he promised to come to him, and he came to him at night. When he came to him he called him and he came out to him perfumed. When he sat with him - and he had come with three or four men - the scent of perfume wafting from him, they mentioned to him, saying: I have such-and-such woman, the most perfumed of women. He said: Permit me to smell. He said: Yes. So he placed his hand in his head and smelled it. He said: May I return? He said: Yes. When he gained control of his head he said: Take him. So they struck him until they killed him.}"
"213","Bahr ibn Nasr ibn Sabiq al-Khawlani narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to me, from 'Umar ibn Sa'id, brother of Sufyan al-Thawri, from his father, from 'Abayah. He said: The killing of Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf was mentioned before Mu'awiyah, and Ibn Yamin said: His killing was treachery. Muhammad ibn Maslamah said: O Mu'awiyah, is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) accused of treachery before you without your objecting? By Allah, no roof of a house shall ever shade me and you together, nor shall this one's blood be lawful to me unless I kill him. So someone supposed that in what we narrated of what Muhammad ibn Maslamah and his companions did, they had entered into contrary to what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"214","201 - Of what 'Ali ibn Ma'bad ibn Nuh narrated to us: Yunus ibn Muhammad al-Mu'addib narrated to us, Hammad, i.e. Ibn Salamah, narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Rifa'ah ibn Shaddad who said: I used to stand at the head of al-Mukhtar; when his lying became clear to me, I resolved by Allah to draw my sword and strike his neck with it, until I remembered a hadith 'Amr ibn al-Hamiq narrated to me. He said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Whoever grants a man security for his life then kills him shall be given a banner of treachery on the Day of Resurrection.}"
"215","202 - And of what Ayyub ibn Nasr al-'Usfuri narrated to us, saying: Ahmad narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah informed us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Rifa'ah ibn Shaddad who said: I used to stand at the head of al-Mukhtar; when I heard his lying I resolved to draw my sword and strike his neck with it, until I remembered a hadith 'Amr ibn al-Hamiq narrated to me: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever grants a man security for his life then kills him shall be given a banner of treachery on the Day of Resurrection.} 'Ali and Ayyub differed in the word we mentioned their differing in, namely amana and amina; Ayyub said: amina, and it is correct."
"216","203 - And of what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Salt narrated to us, 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to me, from Nusayr ibn Abi Nusayr, from al-Suddi, from Rifa'ah al-Fityani - Abu Ja'far said: {And Fityan is of Bajilah. He said: I entered upon al-Mukhtar and there were two cushions laid out, and he said: O slave girl, bring a cushion for so-and-so. I said: What is with these two? He said: Jibril rose from one of them, and Mika'il from the other} - and nothing prevented me from killing him but a hadith 'Amr ibn al-Hamiq narrated to me. I said: And what did he narrate to you? He said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {If a man trusts someone with his blood and he kills him, I am free of him, even if the slain is a disbeliever.} What is in this hadith of Ibn Abi Dawud's narration - 'if a man trusts him' - confirmed the correctness of what Ayyub narrated in the first hadith wherein 'Ali opposed us. What this supposer supposed was ignorance of the Arabic language and its breadth, since the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying in the hadith of 'Amr ibn al-Hamiq is upon one who was secure - either by Islam, by dhimmah, or by a grant of security by the Muslims making him thereby secure for his life, his blood thereby in that state forbidden to the people of the millah and the people of dhimmah together. So the meaning of his saying therein 'whoever is trusted' is: of those of this description, a man, for his life, then kills him, shall be given a banner of treachery on the Day of Resurrection. What is in Jabir's hadith in the story of Muhammad ibn Maslamah and his companions regarding Ka'b ibn al-Ashraf, and his trusting Muhammad ibn Maslamah for his life, was only by a disbeliever's security whose granting is not lawful for one of the millah nor a dhimmi, nor can one of the millah or a dhimmi grant him that - due to his harming Allah and His Messenger. Had a man of the millah granted him security, he would not be secure thereby, nor his blood forbidden thereby. That indicates that Ka'b's trusting Muhammad ibn Maslamah for his life was as no trusting, and that he remained after it lawful in blood as he was before his trusting Muhammad ibn Maslamah for what he trusted him with of his life. So these hadiths of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned to freedom from contradiction, each category of them turning to other than the category to which others of them turned."
"217","Chapter 35 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from Hakim ibn Hizam of his saying: I pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that I would only fall prostrate standing. 204 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Sa'id ibn 'Amir al-Duba'i narrated to us, Sa'id narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, from Hakim ibn Hizam who said: {I pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that I would only fall prostrate standing.} People differed in interpreting this hadith. Some said: Its meaning is that he pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that his prostration would only be falling from his standing, so that his prayer contains nothing of what was narrated from {the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that if something of it is from its performer, Allah does not look at his prayer.}"
"218","205 - And it is what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Bishr ibn 'Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, Sulayman al-A'mash narrated to me, saying: I heard 'Umarah ibn 'Umayr, from Abu Ma'mar, from Abu Mas'ud, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {There is no prayer for whoever does not straighten his spine in bowing and prostration.}"
"219","206 - And as 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan narrated to us: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-A'mash, from 'Umarah, from Abu Ma'mar, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {No prayer suffices in which a man does not straighten his spine when raising his head from bowing and prostration.} He said: So Hakim informed in this hadith of his that he pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that his prayers would be the prayer the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught them, not the prayer Allah dislikes from them and does not look at. Others said: Falling here means falling by death, from the state of standing and sitting to the ground to which one falls from standing and sitting; so he informed that what he pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - he dies only while standing upon it, namely Islam - intending by standing that standing which is resolve, as Allah said regarding the People of the Book: {And among them is he who, if you trust him with a dinar, will not return it to you unless you remain over him standing} - i.e., demanding it of him, seeking its taking from him. And others said: His pledging to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was unto death, the noblest of pledges, which may only be pledged to other than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - no, to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alone - for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was infallible, unsuspected of the state by which his pledge upon his pledgers was established ceasing; others are not so. Of what was narrated of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was pledged unto likewise:"
"220","207 - As 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us, Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, 'Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini narrated to us, from 'Abbad ibn Tamim who said: When the time of al-Harrah came, a man came to 'Abdullah ibn Zayd and said: There is Ibn Hanzalah pledging people unto death. He said: I shall not pledge anyone to this after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So what Hakim informed in his hadith of what he pledged to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was this pledge, the noblest of pledges, which is only permissible to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). All these bases upon which this hadith of Hakim was interpreted are possibly what Hakim intended, and Allah knows best what he intended of them and of other than them of what it may be upon."
"221","Chapter 36 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the muezzins: they are the longest-necked of people on the Day of Resurrection. 208 - Bakkar and Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, saying: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Talhah ibn Yahya, from 'Isa ibn Talhah who said: I heard Mu'awiyah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The muezzins are the longest-necked of people on the Day of Resurrection.} We reflected on what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that, what its meaning is, and found the muezzins among those working in this world in Allah's obedience by what they undertake of adhan. And we found Allah mentioned them in His Book with the finest mention He mentioned anyone working in this world in His obedience: by His saying: {And who is better in speech than one who calls to Allah} the verse. And the workers in Allah's varieties of obedience in this world await on the Day of Resurrection the reward of their deeds in this world, so their necks stretch toward that, and they are in loftiness thereby opposites of what Allah described the people of His disobedience and departure from His command in this world with, by His saying: {So their necks remained humbled to it.} And since the muezzins, in what they undertook of their adhan in this world and raising their voices with it above what others of the people of obedience besides them were upon in their undertaking it in this world, it was possible that by their raising their voices in their adhan which they undertook in this world, their persistence upon it every day and night five times, their following it with iqamahs of prayers, their striving therein with their voices, and their mounting the places in which they give adhan - along with the hardship therein, unhidden - they were made in the length of their necks on the Day of Resurrection, toward their reward for it, above others of the people of deeds in Allah's obedience besides it, in awaiting reward for it and recompense upon it. We found no interpretation of this hadith of what people said on it better than this interpretation we mentioned on it. And Allah knows best what His Messenger intended in that, and Him we ask for guidance."
"222","Chapter 37 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to his wives, may Allah be pleased with them: The quickest of you to join me is the longest-handed of you. 209 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Isma'il, i.e. Ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza: {That 'Umar said takbir over Zaynab bint Jahsh four times, then sent to the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Who shall lower her into her grave? They said: Whoever used to enter upon her in her life. And he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to say: The quickest of you to join me is the longest-handed of you. So they used to stretch their hands, and that was only because she was skilled - meaning in what stands in Allah's path.}"
"223","210 - Yahya ibn Isma'il al-Baghdadi, Abu Zakariyya, at Tabariyyah, narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from 'Amrah, from 'A'ishah who said: {The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his wives: The longest-handed of you shall follow me.} 'A'ishah said: So when we gathered in the house of one of us after the death of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), we would stretch our hands against the wall, competing. We kept doing that until Zaynab bint Jahsh ibn Ri'ab, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), died - and she was a short woman, may Allah have mercy on her, and was not the longest-handed of us - so we knew then that what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) meant was charity. She said: Zaynab was a woman skilled with her hand: tanning, stitching, and giving it in charity in Allah's path. So what we mentioned in this hadith of what the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knew after his death, of the death of his wife Zaynab, of the saying that was from him in his life - despite the shortness of her hands - for the good she earned with them, that she is the longest-handed of them, i.e., in good, not in other than it, sufficed us from speaking on its interpretation with other than what he said on it. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"224","Chapter 38 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about breeding donkeys upon horses. 211 - Fahd narrated to us: Abu Ghassan narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from 'Uthman ibn Abi Zur'ah, from Salim, from 'Alqamah, from 'Ali who said: {A mule was gifted to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I said: What is this? They said: A mule. I said: And what is it? He said: The donkey is bred upon the mare, producing the like of this. I said: Shall we not breed so-and-so upon so-and-so? He said: Only those who do not know do that.}"
"225","212 - Al-Hasan ibn 'Abdullah ibn Mansur narrated to us: al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, except that he said: from 'Ali ibn 'Alqamah. Abu Ja'far said: This Salim is Ibn Abi al-Ja'd."
"226","213 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Aws narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from 'Uthman ibn al-Mughirah, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from 'Ali who said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us to breed donkeys upon mules.}"
"227","214 - Al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us: Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth narrated to us, al-Layth informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Ibn Zurayr, i.e. 'Abdullah ibn Zurayr, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib who said: {A mule was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he rode it. 'Ali said: Had we bred donkeys upon horses, we would have the like of this. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Only those who do not know do that.}"
"228","215 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Layth narrated to us; and Yazid narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from 'Abdullah ibn Zurayr, from 'Ali, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"229","216 - Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us; and Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb al-Washihi narrated to us, both saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abu Jahdam, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abbas, from Ibn 'Abbas who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not single us out with anything to the exclusion of people but three: performing ablution thoroughly, not eating charity, and not breeding donkeys upon horses."
"230","217 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Humayd ibn Mas'adah narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"231","218 - And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Isma'il ibn 'Ulayyah narrated to us, Musa ibn Salim narrated to us, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Ubaydullah, from Ibn 'Abbas the like of it. Then someone said: These two hadiths are contradictory, for in the first of them is the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying {Only those who do not know do that} when 'Ali said to him: Had we bred donkeys upon horses we would have the like of this - that being from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a prohibition to people altogether of breeding donkeys upon horses. And in the second of them is Ibn 'Abbas's saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled them - meaning Banu Hashim - out with not breeding donkeys upon horses. So his prohibition in this hadith did not go beyond Banu Hashim to others, while his prohibition in the first hadith encompassed all people. Our answer to him on that, by Allah's guidance and aid: the first hadith was the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answer therein to 'Ali ibn Abi Talib regarding what he said to him - 'Had we bred donkeys upon horses, the like of this would come to us' - that only those who do not know do that; i.e., that donkeys bred upon horses produce mules, in whose keeping there is no reward, nor shares for them in spoils for whoever raids upon them; whereas horses bred upon horses produce horses, in whose keeping is the reward Allah promised, upon the tongue of His Messenger, to those who keep them and their keeping them."
"232","219 - As Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Numayr al-Hamdani narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: {Horses have good tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection.}"
"233","220 - And as Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us, Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"234","221 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: al-Qa'nabi narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"235","222 - And as Fahd narrated to us: 'Umar ibn Hafs narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ash'ath ibn Sawwar, from Abu Ziyad al-Taymi, from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"236","223 - And as 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan narrated to us: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Yunus ibn 'Ubayd, from 'Amr ibn Sa'id, from Abu Zur'ah, from Jarir ibn 'Abdullah who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Horses have good tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection: reward and spoils.}"
"237","224 - And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us, Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us, from Yunus ibn 'Ubayd, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it."
"238","225 - Fahd narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, 'Urwah al-Bariqi narrated to us: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Horses have good tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection: reward and spoils.}"
"239","226 - And as Fahd narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Idris and Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Ibn Idris and Ibn Fudayl, from Husayn, from al-Sha'bi, from 'Urwah al-Bariqi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Good is tied in the forelocks of horses. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what is that? He said: Reward and spoils until the Day of Resurrection.} Ibn Idris added: {And camels are honor for their people, and sheep are blessing.}"
"240","227 - Fahd narrated to us: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Fitr ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq who said: 'Urwah al-Bariqi stood over us while we were in our gathering, and narrated to us, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Good is tied in the forelocks of horses forever until the Day of Resurrection.}"
"241","228 - And as Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni Abu Qurrah narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Dimashqi narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Salim narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Sulayman al-Aftas narrated to us, al-Walid ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Jurashi narrated to me, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, Salamah ibn Nufayl al-Sakuni narrated to us, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Horses have good bound in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection, and their people are aided upon them.} And on that there are hadiths entering into this type from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of which we selected some for what may be worthier of it than what comes later in this book, Allah willing. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed 'Ali ibn Abi Talib in his answer to him about his saying 'Had we bred donkeys upon horses' by his saying {Only those who do not know do that} - i.e., that those who produce what has no reward in its producing, nor share in spoils with raiding upon it, and abandon producing what has reward in its producing and shares in spoils, are those who do not know. This is the explanation of what is in 'Ali's hadith which we narrated, and Allah knows best. As for what is in Ibn 'Abbas's hadith, it was upon the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singling them out with not breeding donkeys upon horses for a meaning in them which 'Abdullah ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn Abi Talib mentioned, clarifying therein the meaning for which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) singled them out with that."
"242","229 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, Murajja ibn Raja narrated to us, Abu Jahdam narrated to us, 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah narrated to me, from Ibn 'Abbas who said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not single us out but with three: not eating charity, performing ablution thoroughly, and not breeding a donkey upon a mare.} He said: So I met 'Abdullah ibn al-Hasan while he was circumambulating the House, and narrated it to him, and he said: He spoke truth. Horses were few among Banu Hashim, so he wished them to multiply among them. So it became clear, by Allah's praise and favor, that there is no contradiction in either of these two hadiths toward the other, and that what is in each is for a meaning other than the meaning in the other. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"243","Chapter 39 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about humiliation through cultivation. 230 - Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam, Muhammad ibn Humayd ibn Hisham al-Ru'ayni, Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud, and Fahd ibn 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah al-Makhzumi al-Kufi Abu al-Hasan narrated to us, saying: 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, 'Abdullah ibn Salim al-Himsi narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Alhani narrated to us, saying: I heard Abu Umamah - and he saw a plowshare and some farming implements - say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {These do not enter the house of a people but Allah brings humiliation into it.} We reflected on what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of this and found the administration of land taxes, collection of its revenues and placing them in their due places, belongs to the Muslims, undertaken by their rulers, until they take it from those upon whom it is and place it where it must be placed. What the rulers of the Muslims undertake for the Muslims is as the Muslims undertake for themselves. Whoever of the Muslims enters into what entails tax upon him returns thereby sought for what he was before a seeker of; in that is humiliation entering upon them. And there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he informed of his provision, and of humiliation and abasement shifting from him and binding his opponents:"
"244","231 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Wahb ibn 'Atiyyah narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to us, from Hassan ibn 'Atiyyah, from Abu Munib al-Jurashi, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {I was sent with the sword before the Hour, that Allah alone, without partner, be worshipped; my provision was placed under my spear; humiliation and abasement were placed upon whoever opposes me; and whoever imitates a people is of them.}"
"245","Chapter 40 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah of his saying, when dividing justly among his wives: O Allah, this is my division in what I own, so blame me not for what You own and I own not. 232 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi, from 'A'ishah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) {used to divide among his wives, saying: O Allah, this is my division in what I own, so blame me not for what You own and I own not.}"
"246","233 - 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Ubayd ibn 'Imran al-Tabarani at Tabariyyah, Abu Ayyub, known as Ibn Khalaf, narrated to us: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi, from 'A'ishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: We reflected on what is in this hadith and what meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended therein by his saying {O Allah, this is my division in what I own, so blame me not for what You own and I own not} - though he is not blamed in that, since it is of what he has no action in. Its meaning with us, Allah knows best, is upon compassion and mercy from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Allah, lest He have known of him in his division among them - though he did not depart therein from justice - an inclination of his heart to some of them beyond the like to the rest, which is prohibited, and in which servants are alike, as has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in warning against the like of that:"
"247","234 - As Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Qasim al-Kufi Abu al-Hasan narrated to us: Waki' ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, from Hammam ibn Yahya, from Qatadah, from al-Nadr ibn Anas, from Bashir ibn Nahik, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever has two wives and inclines with one away from the other shall come on the Day of Resurrection with one of his sides leaning - or he said: fallen.} And there has been narrated in interpreting Allah's saying {You shall never be able to be just between women, even if you strive} that thereby is meant what occurs in your hearts for some of them to the exclusion of others, which is pardoned for them, since they cannot repel it from their hearts - though it is possible it increases beyond that by what they draw to their hearts. So what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did of what he sought from his Lord was upon compassion and fear of what precedes to his heart, of what he can repel from himself, alongside its nearness to overcoming him. This with us, Allah knows best, is like what is in the hadith of Husayn al-Khuza'i, of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught him to supplicate his Lord to forgive him what he erred in and what he intended - what he erred in being untaken against him - since he feared for him that his drawing near be of what he intended. We have narrated this hadith earlier in this book, and Allah we ask for guidance."
"248","Chapter 41 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his prohibiting his ummah from saying: What Allah wills and Muhammad wills, and his commanding them to say in its place: What Allah wills, then what Muhammad wills. 235 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us, Shayban, i.e. al-Nahwi, narrated to us, from al-Ajlah, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, from Ibn 'Abbas who said: {A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and debated him in some speech, saying: What Allah wills and you will. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Have you made me equal with Allah? No, rather what Allah alone wills.}"
"249","236 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: 'Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Shu'bah - Mansur ibn al-Mu'tamir said: he informed me, saying: I heard 'Abdullah ibn Yasar, from Hudhayfah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Do not say: What Allah wills and so-and-so wills, but say: What Allah wills, then so-and-so wills.}"
"250","237 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Bahr al-Qattan narrated to us, Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samurah who said: {A man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw in a dream some Jews; their appearance pleased him, so he said: You are a people, were it not that you say: 'Uzayr is the son of Allah? He said: And you are a people, were it not that you say: What Allah wills and Muhammad wills. Then he saw some Christians; their appearance pleased him, so he said: You are a people, were it not that you say: The Messiah is the son of Allah. He said: And you are a people, were it not that you say: What Allah wills and Muhammad wills. When morning came he recounted that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I used to hear it from you and it hurt me. So do not say: What Allah wills and Muhammad wills, but say: What Allah wills, then Muhammad wills.}"
"251","238 - Salih ibn Shu'ayb ibn Aban al-Basri Abu Shu'ayb narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us, Yahya, i.e. Ibn Sa'id, narrated to us, from al-Mas'udi who said: Ma'bad ibn Khalid narrated to me, from 'Abdullah ibn Yasar, from Qutaylah bint Sayfi al-Juhaniyyah who said: {A Jewish scholar came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Muhammad, you are an excellent people, were it not that you associate partners. He said: Glory be to Allah, and what is that? He said: You say when you swear: By the Ka'bah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) waited, then said: It is said: whoever of you swears, let him swear by the Lord of the Ka'bah. Then he said: O Muhammad, you are an excellent people, were it not that you set up a rival to Allah. He said: Glory be to Allah. He said: You say: What Allah wills and so-and-so wills. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) waited, then said: Someone has said it. Whoever says 'What Allah wills,' let him say with it 'then you will.'}"
"252","239 - Fahd narrated to us: Musa ibn Dawud narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Ma'bad ibn Khalid, from 'Abdullah ibn Yasar al-Juhani, from Qutaylah bint Sayfi al-Juhaniyyah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: In what we narrated in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is {his prohibiting his ummah from saying: What Allah wills and you will, and his commanding them to say in its place: What Allah wills, then you will.} Someone said: In Allah's Book is what indicates the permissibility of this prohibited matter in these hadiths, then he mentioned His saying: {Give thanks to Me and to your parents} - without saying 'then to your parents.' Our answer to him on that, by Allah's guidance, is that this was permissible before the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prohibiting the like of it in these hadiths, then he prohibited what he prohibited in these hadiths, and that was abrogation of what had been permissible of what you recited before that. Our doctrine is that the Sunnah may abrogate the Qur'an, for each of them is from Allah, abrogating what He wills of them by what He wills of them; and because we found Allah's Book itself indicating that, namely His saying therein: {And those of your women who commit indecency} the verse. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said after that: {Take from me; Allah has made a way for them: the virgin with the virgin, one hundred lashes and exile for a year; the previously married with the previously married, one hundred lashes and stoning.}"
"253","240 - As Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us, Shu'bah informed us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Hittan ibn 'Abdullah, from 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit, {from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Take from me; Allah has made a way for them: the virgin with the virgin, the previously married with the previously married; the virgin is flogged and exiled, the previously married is flogged and stoned.}"
"254","241 - And as Yunus narrated to us: Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Hittan al-Raqashi, from 'Ubadah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it."
"255","242 - And as Salih ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ansari narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, Mansur informed us, from al-Hasan, Hittan narrated to us, from 'Ubadah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Take from me; Allah has made a way for them: the virgin with the virgin, one hundred lashes and exile for a year; the previously married with the previously married, one hundred lashes and stoning.} Abu Ja'far said: Do you not see that Allah said in His Book regarding the women who commit indecency what He said, then said: {Or Allah makes a way for them}? Their hadd before He made a way for them was what He mentioned in this verse; then He made a way for them therein with a hadd contrary to that hadd mentioned in that verse. That indicates that the Sunnah may abrogate the Qur'an as the Qur'an abrogates the Qur'an. And with Allah is guidance."
"256","Chapter 42 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited of His saying {and the wombs} at the beginning of Surah al-Nisa: was it accusative or genitive? 243 - Bakkar narrated to us: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, 'Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah narrated to me, saying: I heard Mundhir ibn Jarir ibn 'Abdullah narrating from his father who said: {We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the forenoon when barefoot, naked people came to him, wearing striped woolen cloaks with slits, girded with swords - most of them of Mudar, indeed all of them of Mudar. He said: So I saw the face of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) change at the destitution he saw in them. Then he entered his house, then came out and ordered Bilal, who called the adhan and iqamah, and he prayed Zuhr. Then he said - or addressed: {O mankind, fear your Lord who created you from a single soul} to the end of the verse, {and let a soul look at what it has sent forth for tomorrow.} Let a man give charity from his dinar, his dirham, his garment, his sa' of wheat, his sa' of dates, until he said: even half a date. He said: Then a man of the Ansar came with a bag his hand could barely carry - indeed could not carry - then people followed, until I saw two heaps of food and clothing. And I saw the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) brighten as if gilded. Then he said: Whoever institutes in Islam a good practice shall have its reward and the reward of whoever acts by it after him, nothing diminishing from their rewards; and whoever institutes in Islam an evil practice shall bear its burden and the burden of whoever acts by it after him, nothing diminishing from their burdens.}"
"257","244 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Abu 'Awanah narrated to us, Raqabah ibn Masqalah al-'Abdi narrated to us, from 'Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah, from al-Mundhir ibn Jarir, from Jarir ibn 'Abdullah who said: I was sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said therein, then he said: {To Bilal: hasten the prayer.}"
"258","245 - 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us: Isma'il ibn 'Umar al-Wasiti narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr, from Ibn Jarir, from his father who said: {People came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Mudar, girded with swords, wearing slit woolen cloaks - al-Mas'udi said: al-nimar is wool - in severe hardship and need. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: {And fear Allah by whom you ask one another, and the wombs; Allah is ever over you a watcher.} Give charity before you cannot give charity; let a man give charity from his dinar, from his dirham, from his wheat, from his barley, from his dates. He said: Then a man came with a charity of weight, placed it in his hand, and that delighted and pleased him. Then people hastened after, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever institutes a good practice and it is acted upon after him shall have the like of the reward of whoever acts by it, nothing diminishing from their rewards; and whoever institutes an evil practice and it is acted upon after him shall bear the like of the burden of whoever acts by it, nothing diminishing from their burdens.} Abu Ja'far said: In these narrations is the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting to people {And fear Allah by whom you ask one another, and the wombs; Allah is ever over you a watcher} when urging them to kinship ties, due to what he saw of hardship, distress and need among its people. That was evidence that he recited it accusative, meaning: fear the wombs lest you sever them. What those who recited it genitive carried it upon - their asking one another by Allah and the wombs - was not the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reciting it to those he recited to upon asking; it was upon urging mutual connection and abandoning severing kinship. In that is evidence that he recited it accusative, not genitive."
"259","And likewise it was narrated from Ibn 'Abbas that he used to recite it thus. As Yahya ibn 'Uthman narrated to us: Yusuf ibn 'Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, 'Aththam ibn 'Ali narrated to us, from al-A'mash who said: I heard Mujahid say: Ibn 'Abbas used to recite this verse {by whom you ask one another, and the wombs} accusative, saying: Fear Allah and the wombs."
"260","And most reciters recited it thus. As Ibn Abi 'Imran Ahmad Abu Ja'far narrated to us: Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated to us, saying: 'Asim recited 'and the wombs' accusative, Nafi' likewise, Abu 'Amr likewise. And as Ahmad narrated to us: Khalaf narrated to us, from al-Khaffaf, from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan: 'and the wombs' accusative, saying: And the wombs, do not sever them. Likewise al-Kalbi said. Khalaf said: And it is the recitation. I heard Ibn Abi 'Imran say: I heard Khalaf say: 'Asim's recitation was taken from Yahya ibn Adam, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash from him. Abu Ja'far said: And we afterwards took 'Asim's recitation by hearing, from Rawh ibn al-Faraj, who narrated it to us letter by letter, from Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Ju'fi, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash himself, from 'Asim."
"261","Chapter 43 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: When a man dies, his deeds are cut off except from ongoing charity, beneficial knowledge, and a righteous child who supplicates for him. 246 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, saying: Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from al-'Ala ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {When a man dies, his deeds are cut off from him except three: except ongoing charity, beneficial knowledge, and a righteous child who supplicates for him.}"
"262","247 - Al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb ibn Sa'id al-Azdi Abu 'Ali narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Baytari narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from al-'Ala ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: Then a questioner asked, saying: Does this contradict what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which you mentioned in the chapter before this chapter, about whoever institutes a good practice and it is acted upon by those after him, and what you mentioned in other than this place, meaning:"
"263","248 - As Yunus narrated to us: Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us, from 'Asim, from Abu Wa'il, from Jarir: that {some Bedouin people came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing slit woolen cloaks. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urged charity, but they seemed slow with it, until that was seen in the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then a man of the Ansar came with a piece of unwrought gold and cast it down, and people followed, until that was recognized in the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever institutes a good practice and it is acted upon by those after him shall have the like of the reward of whoever acts by it, nothing falling from their rewards; and whoever institutes an evil practice and it is acted upon by those after him shall bear the like of the burden of whoever acts by it, nothing diminishing from their burdens.}"
"264","249 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Shayban, i.e. al-Nahwi, narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Muslim ibn Subayh and Musa ibn 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hilal al-'Absi, from Jarir ibn 'Abdullah who said: {Bedouin people came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he saw privation and hardship upon them. So he addressed people, praised Allah and extolled Him, then commanded them with charity, urged them to it and incited them to it. But they were slow, until that was seen in his face. Then a man of the Ansar came with a handful of silver and gave it to him. Then another came, then people followed with charity, until joy was seen in his face. So he said: Whoever institutes in Islam a good practice ...} then he mentioned the rest of the hadith we mentioned before it."
"265","250 - Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-'Allaf narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Saw'a narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from 'Abd al-Rahman, from Jarir al-Bajali: that he narrated to them in the quarter of the Kufah mosque that {a man of the Ansar stood to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a bag of gold filling what is between the fingers, saying: O Messenger of Allah, this is in Allah's path. Then Abu Bakr stood and gave, then 'Umar stood and gave, then the Muhajirun and Ansar stood and gave. The face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) beamed until we saw joy in his face. Thereupon he said: Whoever institutes a practice ...}, then he mentioned the rest of the hadith before it. Abu Ja'far said: In these hadiths is {Whoever institutes in Islam a good practice shall have its reward and the reward of whoever acts by it after him; and whoever institutes in Islam an evil practice shall bear its burden and the burden of whoever acts by it after him.} And Hudhayfah narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on that of what enters into this meaning."
"266","251 - As Bakkar narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Hisham ibn Hassan narrated to us, from Muhammad, i.e. Ibn Sirin, from Abu 'Ubaydah ibn Hudhayfah, from his father who said: {A beggar stood asking in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and people withheld. Then a man of the people gave, and people gave. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever institutes good and it is followed, he shall have its reward and of the rewards of those who follow him, nothing diminishing from their rewards; and whoever institutes evil and it is followed, he shall bear its burden and of the burdens of those who follow him, nothing diminishing from their burdens.} Abu Ja'far said: This is the likelier of the two meanings with us to the truth, Allah knows best, for the follower of his predecessor has action with him, while his predecessor's action in the like of that has been cut off; so it is intelligible with us that the follower has therein more than the originator, and likewise is the reward of each of them therein. Our answer on that, by Allah's guidance and aid, is that there is no disagreement in that with Abu Hurayrah's hadith which we mentioned, for what is in these narrations is mention of the instituted practice - it is of the knowledge benefited from. And a questioner asked, saying: Does Abu Hurayrah's hadith which you mentioned contradict what Fudalah ibn 'Ubayd narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? And he mentioned:"
"267","252 - As Yunus and 'Isa al-Ghafiqi narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, saying: Abu Hani al-Khawlani informed me, from 'Amr ibn Malik al-Janbi: that he heard Fudalah narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: {Whoever dies upon one of these ranks shall be raised upon it on the Day of Resurrection.}"
"268","253 - And as Bakr ibn Idris ibn al-Hajjaj ibn Harun al-Azdi Abu al-Qasim narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri narrated to us, Haywah and Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us, saying: Abu Hani narrated to us: that Abu 'Ali al-Janbi narrated to him that he heard Fudalah narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it."
"269","254 - Al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Hurrah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, adding: {And do not bring perfume near him.} Abu Ja'far said: And this questioner mentioned with that:"
"270","255 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us: al-Nabil Abu 'Asim narrated to us, from Sufyan, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir who said: {Every servant shall be raised upon what he died upon. It was said to him: From the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes.} Our answer to him on that is that this is nothing of Abu Hurayrah's hadith, for this is regarding what its doer was upon of good deeds until his death cut him off from them - so he remains after his death upon the intention he died upon, and there is written for him after his death of reward what would have been written for him had he not died. The like of that is what has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the pilgrim who dies in his ihram."
"271","256 - As Yunus narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: 'Amr ibn Dinar heard: Sa'id ibn Jubayr narrated to us with a report from Ibn 'Abbas, which he heard him say: {We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey when a man fell from his camel, was crushed and died while in ihram. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wash him with water and lote, bury him in his two garments, do not cover his head, for Allah shall raise him on the Day of Resurrection chanting the talbiyah.}"
"272","Yunus said to us: Sufyan said to us: And Ibrahim ibn Abi Hurrah added therein, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {And do not bring perfume near him.}"
"273","257 - And as al-Muzani narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Sufyan, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas: {That a man fell from his camel, was crushed and died. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Wash him with water and lote, shroud him in his two garments, do not cover his head, for he shall be raised on the Day of Resurrection chanting the talbiyah or the labbayk.} And the like of it is what has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the martyr."
"274","258 - As Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed us: that Ibn Shihab narrated to him, from 'Abdullah ibn Tha'labah al-Zuhri - {and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had wiped his face} - that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to the slain of Uhud who were killed in Allah's path, finding them mutilated: Shroud them with their wounds, for no wound is suffered in Allah's path but it shall come on the Day of Resurrection, its color the color of blood and its scent the scent of musk. This - I mean Fudalah's hadith - and Ibn 'Abbas's hadith, and 'Abdullah ibn Tha'labah's hadith, contain mention of the states of one who worked in Allah's obedience until his death cut him off from it, and mention of the states upon which he shall be raised on the Day of Resurrection. Abu Hurayrah's hadith contains mention of renewed deeds after the death of possessors of benefited-from knowledge, whose reward flows upon them after their deaths in addition to what was from them therein in their lives."
"275","Chapter 44 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: Beware of 'if only,' for it opens the work of Satan. 259 - Yunus narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: {The strong believer is better and more beloved to Allah than the weak believer; in each is good. Strive for what benefits you, do not despair; if something escapes you, say: Allah decreed, and He did what He willed. And beware of 'if only,' for it opens the work of Satan.} We examined this hadith's chain: is it connected, or has tadlis entered it from Ibn 'Ajlan, narrating it from al-A'raj without hearing it from him?"
"276","260 - So we found that Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Kufi al-Dhuhali Abu al-'Ala narrated to us, saying: Ahmad ibn Jamil al-Marwazi narrated to us: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan narrated to us, from Rabi'ah, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The strong believer is better and more beloved to Allah than the weak believer; in each is good. Strive for what benefits you and do not despair; if a matter overcomes you, say: Allah decreed, and He did what He willed. And beware of 'if only,' for it opens from Satan.} Then: I heard it from Rabi'ah, and my memorization of it is from Muhammad."
"277","261 - And we found that Yahya ibn 'Uthman had narrated to us, saying: Nu'aym ibn Hammad narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, then he mentioned with his chain the like of it, saying at its end: Then I heard it from Rabi'ah ibn 'Uthman, though Rabi'ah was not mentioned at its beginning. By that we concluded that Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan only narrated it from al-A'raj by tadlis of it from him, and that he had only taken it from Rabi'ah ibn 'Uthman from him. Then we examined Rabi'ah's hadith from al-A'raj: is it his hearing it from him, or upon tadlis of it from him?"
"278","262 - So we found that Fahd had narrated to us, saying: Ahmad ibn Humayd al-Kufi, son-in-law of 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa, narrated to us: 'Abdullah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Rabi'ah ibn 'Uthman, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The strong believer is better and more beloved to Allah than the weak believer; in each is good. Strive for what benefits you, seek Allah's aid and do not despair; if something escapes you, say: Allah decreed, and He did what He willed. And beware of 'if'; for 'if' opens the work of Satan.} By that we concluded that the origin of this hadith in its chain is only from Ibn 'Ajlan, from Rabi'ah ibn 'Uthman, from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban, from al-A'raj. Then the meaning of the warned-against 'if' in this hadith became clear to us after our concluding that 'if' is not disliked in all things, since Allah mentioned in His Book its permissibility in something He mentioned it in: His saying to His Prophet in what He mentioned of His answer to whoever asked him about the Hour: {Had I known the unseen, I would have amassed good, and no harm would have touched me} - since His Messenger had mentioned it in what he mentioned it in."
"279","263 - As 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Abu Kabshah al-Anmari who said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck for us the parable of this world as four: a man to whom Allah gave wealth and knowledge, acting by his knowledge in his wealth; a man to whom Allah gave knowledge but not wealth, saying: Had Allah given me like what He gave so-and-so, I would have done therein like what he does - so they are equal in reward; a man to whom Allah gave wealth but not knowledge, withholding it from its right and spending it in falsehood; and a man to whom Allah gave neither wealth nor knowledge, saying: Had Allah given me like what He gave so-and-so, I would have done therein like what he does - so they are equal in burden.} 'If' was not disliked in what we mentioned, so we knew thereby that it is only disliked, warned against, in other than what we described. Then we examined that to determine the place in which it is disliked. We found Allah mentioned in His Book what was from a people He censured for speech from them: His saying: {They say: Had we any part in the affair} - and He rebutted that upon them with His saying: {Say: The affair wholly belongs to Allah.} Then He returned informing about them of what they were upon therein of what they concealed from His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: {They conceal in themselves what they do not show you.} Then afterward He returned informing about them of what they used to say, saying: {They say: Had we any part in the affair, we would not have been killed here.} So He rebutted that upon them with what He commanded His Prophet to say to them, saying: {Say: Had you been in your houses, those for whom killing was decreed would have gone forth to their resting places.} Then afterward He returned to the believers, warning them against being like them, saying: {O you who believe, be not like those who disbelieved and said to their brothers when they traveled in the land or were raiding: Had they been with us, they would not have died nor been killed.} Then He informed the believers of the meaning by which those disbelievers were afflicted with that, saying: {That Allah may make it anguish in their hearts.} Then He informed them of the realities of matters upon which creation runs, of death and life, saying: {And Allah gives life and causes death} the verse. And we found Him, glory and exalted, saying in His Book: {Lest a soul say: O my regret over what I neglected} to His saying {of the doers of good} - and Allah rebutted that upon them with His saying: {Yes, My signs came to you but you denied them} the verse. He said: So in what we recited of the 'ifs' is understood wherein it is not censured, and wherein it is censured; likewise in what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter of Abu Kabshah's hadith. Then we found the Arabs censuring 'if' and warning against it, saying: Beware of 'if' - meaning a man's saying: Had I known this would befall me, I would have done good. Of what was mentioned is evidence that the disliked 'if' is what is in Abu Hurayrah's hadith which we narrated, and that the 'if' which is not disliked is the 'if' mentioned in Abu Kabshah's hadith which we also narrated."
"280","And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Hajjaj al-Azdi, from Salman, that he said: Belief in destiny is to know that what struck you could not have missed you, and what missed you could not have struck you; and do not say for anything that struck you: Had I done such-and-such. Abu Ja'far said: meaning: it would have been such-and-such, and such-and-such would not have been. What we explained and mentioned makes clear that there is no contradiction nor disagreement in anything narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter, and that what we recited of Allah's Book supports that, testifying to it. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"281","Chapter 45 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about whoever of the dead has a group of Muslims pray over him and intercede for him: they intercede for him when they are a number whose extent was mentioned in what was narrated from him on that. 264 - Yunus narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed me: that Ayyub ibn Abi Tamimah narrated to him: that Abu Qilabah informed him: that 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, the foster-brother of 'A'ishah, informed him: that 'A'ishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), informed him: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {No Muslim man dies and a community of Muslims reaching one hundred prays over him and intercedes for him but they shall be allowed intercession for him.}"
"282","265 - Husayn ibn Nusayr narrated to us: 'Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, 'Ubaydullah, i.e. Ibn 'Amr, narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, from 'A'ishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: No one of the Muslims dies and a community of Muslims reaching one hundred prays over him and intercedes for him but they shall be allowed intercession for him."
"283","266 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: 'Amr ibn Zurarah narrated to us, Isma'il, i.e. Ibn Ibrahim, narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, the foster-brother of 'A'ishah, from 'A'ishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"284","267 - Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi, from 'A'ishah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {No Muslim dies and a community of people reaching one hundred prays over him but they shall be allowed intercession for him.} Abu Ja'far said: Thus Hammad says in this hadith's chain, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi, while people oppose him therein, saying: 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, the foster-brother of 'A'ishah - and it is likelier to be correct therein, Allah knows best. And 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi is a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) several hadiths, among them:"
"285","268 - As Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Husayn, from Abu Burdah who said: I was sitting with a commander he named, and heads of the Khawarij were repeatedly brought to him. He said: Whenever I saw a head of them I said: To the Fire. 'Abdullah ibn Yazid said: O son of my brother, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {The punishment of this ummah is in its world.} Abu Ja'far said: And Muhammad ibn Sa'd mentioned him in the Book of Tabaqat, saying: 'Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Khatmi is of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who settled in Kufah, marked out a house there, and 'Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr appointed him over it. Then we returned to what we were upon of the number of those praying over the funeral, interceding for its owner:"
"286","269 - Ibn Ma'bad narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, Abu Hamzah, i.e. al-Sukkari, whose name is Muhammad ibn Maymun, narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: {Whoever has one hundred Muslims pray over him shall be forgiven.}"
"287","270 - And we found that Abu Umayyah had narrated to us, saying: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Shayban, i.e. Abu Mu'awiyah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Nahwi, narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: {Whoever has one hundred Muslims pray over him shall be forgiven.} And Ibn 'Abbas narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the number of the congregation allowed intercession in this meaning:"
"288","271 - As 'Isa ibn Ibrahim al-Ghafiqi narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Abu Sakhr Humayd ibn Ziyad narrated to me, from Sharik ibn 'Abdullah ibn Abi Namir, from Kurayb, from Ibn 'Abbas, that a son of his died at Qudayd or 'Usfan. He said to Kurayb: See how many people gathered for him. He said: So I went out and people had gathered. He said: Bring him out, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {No Muslim man dies and forty men who associate nothing with Allah stand over his funeral but Allah shall allow them intercession for him.} And we found from Anas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what agrees with what we narrated in this chapter from 'A'ishah and Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and contradicts what we narrated therein from Ibn 'Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him):"
"289","272 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us: Suwayd ibn Nasr informed us, 'Abdullah, i.e. Ibn al-Mubarak, informed us, from Sallam ibn Abi Muti', from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from 'Abdullah ibn Yazid, the foster-brother of 'A'ishah, from 'A'ishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: {No dead person has a group of Muslims reaching one hundred pray over him, interceding, but they shall be allowed intercession for him.} Sallam said: So I narrated it to Shu'ayb ibn al-Habhab, and he said: Anas narrated it to me, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then someone said: Whence came this disagreement in these narrations? Our answer on that, by Allah's guidance, is that it is possible that Allah favored His believing servants with forgiveness for whoever has one hundred of them pray over him by their intercession for him, then favored him with forgiveness by the intercession of forty of them. So Ibn 'Abbas's report of that was the last of what was from Him of what He favored thereby with forgiveness for the prayed-over of the believers by their intercession. 'A'ishah's and Abu Hurayrah's reports preceded that. He said: Why did you carry that upon what you mentioned, rather than carrying 'A'ishah's and Abu Hurayrah's hadiths as the later, and Ibn 'Abbas's hadith as the earlier? Our answer to him on that, by Allah's guidance and aid: Allah is not of the description to favor with forgiveness by a meaning, then retract the forgiveness by that meaning; though it is possible He favors with forgiveness by a meaning, then favors with forgiveness by less than that meaning, and easier upon His creatures whom He favored thereby. By what we mentioned the aspect from which the difference of the two numbers came in the reports we narrated became clear. And Allah we ask for guidance."
"290","Chapter 46 - Clarification of the problematic aspect of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: The grave has a squeeze; had anyone been saved from it, Sa'd ibn Mu'adh would have been saved from it, may Allah be pleased with him. 273 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Nafi', from the Mother of the Believers: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The grave has a squeeze; had anyone been saved from it, Sa'd ibn Mu'adh would have been saved from it.} Thus Ibn Marzuq narrated it to us without inserting between Nafi' and the Mother of the Believers anyone."
"291","274 - Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb ibn Sulayman al-Kaysani Abu Muhammad narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd who said: I heard Nafi' narrating from the wife of Ibn Umar, from A'ishah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"292","275 - Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Salim al-Sa'igh al-Makki Abu Ja'far narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr al-Kirmani, the judge of Kirman, narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us - Sa'd said: He informed me, he said: I heard Nafi', from the wife of Ibn Umar, from A'ishah, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - then he mentioned the like of it. Sufyan ibn Sa'id differed with Shu'bah in the chain of this hadith from Sa'd, and narrated it from him."
"293","276 - As Fahd narrated to us, Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Sa'd, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {If anyone were to escape the punishment of the grave, Sa'd would have escaped it. Then he spoke with his three fingers, joining them as though turning them, then said: He was squeezed, then he was relieved.} Then an objector said: Can this be contradictory to what has been narrated from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As on this topic? And he mentioned:"
"294","277 - What Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal narrated to us, from Rabi'ah ibn Sayf, from Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {There is no Muslim who dies on the day of Friday or the night of Friday except that he is cleared of the trial of the grave.} Our answer to him regarding that, by Allah's guidance and help, was that this hadith is disconnected (munqati'), for Rabi'ah ibn Sayf never met Abd Allah ibn Amr; he only used to narrate it from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli from him. The proof of that is:"
"295","278 - That al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Jizi narrated to us, he said: Abu Zur'ah narrated to us, Haywah informed us, Rabi'ah ibn Sayf al-Ma'afiri narrated to me, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, {from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he saw his daughter Fatimah (peace be upon her) and said to her: From where have you come, O Fatimah? She said: I have come from behind the funeral of this man. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to her: Did you reach al-Kuda? She said: How could I reach it, when I have heard from you what I have heard? He said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, if you had reached al-Kuda, you would not see Paradise until the grandfather of your father sees it.} Then we returned to seeking who stands between Rabi'ah ibn Sayf and Abd Allah ibn Amr in this hadith."
"296","279 - We found that Yunus narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, from Rabi'ah ibn Sayf: That Abd al-Rahman ibn Qahzam informed him that a son of Fayyad ibn Uqbah died on a Friday, and his grief over him was intense, so a truthful man said to him: O Abu Yahya, shall I not give you glad tidings of something I heard from Abd Allah ibn Amr - I heard him say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {There is no Muslim who dies on the day of Friday or the night of Friday except that he is cleared of the trial of the grave.}"
"297","280 - Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, my father narrated to us, and Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth, from al-Layth, Khalid - meaning Ibn Yazid - narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Hilal, from Rabi'ah ibn Sayf: That Abd al-Rahman ibn Qahzam informed him that a son of Fayyad ibn Uqbah - then he mentioned the like of it exactly. He added beyond Yunus in its chain by inserting between al-Layth and Rabi'ah ibn Sayf Khalid ibn Yazid and Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, and it is more resembling the correct view in our opinion, and Allah knows best. By that we established the corruption of the chain of this hadith, and that the like of it cannot dislodge anything that the hadith of A'ishah necessitates its inclusion in. We ask Allah for help in that, and we seek His assurance in what we hoped for."
"298","47 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning what the sun sets in. 281 - Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, from his father, from Abu Dharr who said: {I entered the mosque and there was the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sitting, and when the sun had set he said: O Abu Dharr, do you know where this goes? He said: I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: It goes to seek permission to prostrate, and permission is granted to it, and it is as though it has been said to it: Rise from where you came, so it rises from its place of setting. He said: Then he recited in the reading of Abd Allah (That is its appointed course).} In this is what indicates that the sun sets in the sky. And there has also been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning what it sets in."
"299","282 - What Ali ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud al-Harrani narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, {from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he recited {in a muddy spring}} - and this hadith seems to be among what no one raised (to the Prophet) from the hadith of Hammad ibn Salamah except Abd al-Ghaffar ibn Dawud, and it is among what the people of hadith declare him mistaken in, saying: It is halted (mawquf) upon Ibn Abbas. His companions of Hammad differed with him in it and did not raise it. Among those of them who differed with him in it: Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Khurasani, and Hajjaj ibn Minhal al-Anmati."
"300","As Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj ibn Sulayman al-Hadrami Abu Ja'far narrated to us, Khalid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Uthman, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas that he used to recite it: {in a muddy spring}, pronouncing the hamzah."
"301","And as Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Abd Allah ibn Uthman, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it, and he did not raise it. This hadith has been narrated from Abd Allah ibn Abbas, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in agreement with this meaning."
"302","283 - As Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Dinar - meaning al-Tahi - narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Aws, from Misda' Abu Yahya, from {Ibn Abbas who said: Ubayy taught me to recite as the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) taught him to recite: {it sets in a muddy spring}, unaccented (mukhaffafah)}."
"303","284 - And as Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Dinar narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it, and he did not say: unaccented."
"304","285 - And as Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Qays ibn Hafs al-Darimi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Dinar narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it, and he did not say: unaccented. In what we narrated in the hadith of Ibn Abbas from Ubayy is what confirms the reading of whoever read this word as we have mentioned it therein, and it is the reading of Nafi' and most of the people of Madinah, and that has been strengthened by:"
"305","What Yunus narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah informed us, from Amr, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas who said: Amr ibn al-As differed with me while we were with Mu'awiyah. Ibn Abbas said: {a muddy spring}, and Amr said: {a hot one}. He said: So we asked Ka'b, and he said: In the revealed Book of Allah it indeed sets in black mud. Yunus narrated to us, Amr ibn Khalid narrated to us regarding the attestation of {muddy}."
"306","Muhammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Abu Hadir al-Himyari, from Ibn Abbas who said: I was with Mu'awiyah, and with him was Abd Allah ibn Amr. Mu'awiyah said to Abd Allah: How do you read this word {he found it setting in a spring}? He said: {in a hot spring}. Then Ibn Abbas said - I said to Mu'awiyah: Do you ask this one about the Qur'an, when it was only revealed in my house? He said: How do you read it, O Ibn Abbas? I said: {he found it setting in a muddy spring}. Abu Hadir said: So I said to Ibn Abbas: I support your saying with the saying of our companion Tubba': al-Qarnayn and tha't; for al-khulub in our language is mud, al-tha't is black mud (ham'ah), and al-harmad is black. I mentioned that to Abu Muhammad ibn Salamah, may Allah have mercy on him, and he said to me: These are differing rhymes. I have seen the people of knowledge of poetry, among them Abu Bijad al-Harithi al-Basri and others among the people of knowledge of poetry, reciting the first of these verses other than what you mentioned to me from Yunus, and it is: al-Qarnayn. Abu Ja'far said: And this is the correct view, so that the rhymes of these verses harmonize and all return to the same letter and do not differ."
"307","Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Nu'aym narrated to us, Abdah - and he is Ibn Sulayman al-Kilabi - narrated to us, from Amr ibn Maymun, Ibn Hadir - or Abu Hadir - informed us, from Ibn Abbas who said: Mu'awiyah recited in al-Kahf {he found it setting in a hot spring}, so I said: We recite it {muddy}. Mu'awiyah asked Abd Allah ibn Amr about it and he said: As you recited it. Ibn Abbas said: So I said: In my house the Qur'an was revealed. He said: So Mu'awiyah sent to Ka'b asking him: Where do you find the sun setting in the Torah? He said: In water and mud. He said: So I said to Ibn Abbas: Had I been with you I would have reinforced you with what would increase your insight concerning muddy. Ibn Abbas said: And what is it? He said: I said: We find in what was of the saying of Tubba', what he mentioned concerning Dhu al-Qarnayn of his devotion to knowledge and his pursuing it: and tha't. Ibn Abbas said: What is al-khulub? He said: I said: Mud in their speech. He said: What is al-tha't? I said: Black mud. He said: What is al-harmad? I said: Black. So Ibn Abbas said to a man: Write down what this man says. Then an objector said: This hadith of Ibn Abbas from Ubayy contradicts the hadith of Abu Dharr which you narrated at the beginning of the chapter; for in the hadith of Abu Dharr is the setting of the sun in the sky, while in this is its setting in black mud, and mud is only in the earth, not in the sky. Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance, was that mud may be in the sky just as it is in the earth. Allah the Exalted has indicated that in what He mentioned of the guests of Ibrahim (peace be upon him), in what was their answer to Ibrahim, in His saying: {Then what is your business, O messengers? They said: We have been sent to a criminal people, to send upon them stones of clay} - so that indicated that clay is in the sky just as it is in the earth. This objector said: In the poetry of Tubba' which you narrated, that is among what has indicated that he saw its setting place, and that it is in the earth, not in the sky. Our answer to him about that was that what we narrated from Abu Dharr is from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the authority in language and in all else besides it. Along with this, it is possible that that vision which Tubba' meant was a vision of certainty and knowledge of the heart, not a vision of the eye, as Allah the Exalted said: {And you had wished for death before you met it, and you have seen it while you look on} - so that was upon the vision of hearts and their certainty, not upon the vision of eyes. By that, all that we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter comes out harmonized, with no contradiction in it nor disagreement. This word - I mean {muddy} - has been read by others than Ibn Abbas contrary to how Ibn Abbas read it, and it is {hot}; among them Ibn Mas'ud."
"308","As Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Hisham narrated to us, al-Khaffaf narrated to us, from Harun, from Asim, from Zirr, from Ibn Mas'ud that he used to recite {hot}, meaning: heated. And among them Ibn al-Zubayr."
"309","As Ahmad narrated to us, Khalaf narrated to us, Ubayd ibn Aqil narrated to us, from Shibl, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhaysin, from his father, from Ibn al-Zubayr: hot, with an alif, likewise. In the first section, from the one who was with Ibn Abbas at Mu'awiyah's - from Amr and from his son Abd Allah - is this reading also. We know of no one among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) besides Ibn Abbas agreeing with Ibn Abbas concerning {muddy}, and the majority of them are upon {hot}. We have narrated of that what we narrated, and left what besides it whose chains are not connected. Among those who read this word also were Asim, Sulayman al-A'mash, and Hamzah. Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz mentioned to us, from Abu Ubayd, that he used to hold to that and prefer it, due to the large number of reciters; and because Asim has for his recitation, in soundness of channel, what the recitation of others does not have."
"310","I heard Ahmad ibn Abi Imran say: I heard Yahya ibn Aktham say: If recitation is taken by soundness of channel, we know of no recitation with the soundness of channel that the recitation of Asim has; for he says: I recited the Qur'an to Abu Abd al-Rahman, and Abu Abd al-Rahman recited to Ali, and Ali recited to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: I used to leave Abu Abd al-Rahman and pass by Zirr ibn Hubaysh, and recite to him as I had recited to Abu Abd al-Rahman, and he would not change anything against me. He said: And Zirr recited to Ibn Mas'ud, and Ibn Mas'ud recited to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"311","Abu Ja'far said: And he spoke the truth. We had taken the recitation of Asim letter by letter from Rawh ibn al-Faraj, and he narrated to us that he took it from Yahya ibn Sulayman al-Ju'fi, and that he said to them: Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: I recited to Asim. Abu Bakr said: So I said to Asim: From whom did you recite? He said: From al-Sulami, and he recited to Ali, and Ali recited to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Asim said: I used to make my way via Zirr and recite to him, and Zirr recited to Ibn Mas'ud, and he recited to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"312","Ibrahim ibn Ahmad ibn Marwan al-Wasiti narrated to me, Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Abd Allah al-Wasiti narrated to us, he said: I heard Hafs ibn Sulayman al-Kufi, from Asim who said: Abu Abd al-Rahman said: I recited to Ali at length, and held to him at length, and I taught al-Hasan and al-Husayn until they completed the Qur'an. I met Zayd ibn Thabit with the letters of the Qur'an, and he did not differ with Ali in a single letter. If one were to attribute all of the recitation of Asim to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he would not be blamed. Among what strengthens that is:"
"313","286 - What Fahd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abd Allah, Abu Mu'awiyah, and Waki' narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Zibyan who said: I said to Ibn Abbas: Upon the first reading, do you recite the reading of Ibn Mas'ud? He said: Rather the reading of Ibn Mas'ud is the last. {Jibril (peace be upon him) used to present the Qur'an to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in every Ramadan, and in the year in which he passed away he presented it twice} - so Abd Allah witnessed what of it was abrogated and what was altered."
"314","287 - And what Fahd narrated to us, Abu Ghassan narrated to us, Isra'il ibn Yunus narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas that he said to his companions: Which of the two readings do you consider last? They said: The reading of Zayd. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to present the Qur'an to Jibril every year, and in the year in which he passed away he presented it to him twice, and Ibn Mas'ud witnessed it, so the reading of Abd Allah was last. Abu Ja'far said: The disagreement over these two readings in this word is among the easiest of disagreement; for if we validate what was narrated concerning the spring in which the sun sets, both black mud and heat are thereby deserved as its attributes, both being among its descriptions. Whoever read {hot} described it by one of its attributes, and whoever read {muddy} described it by its other attribute, and that is broad, not constricting upon anyone who narrated a reading of these two readings."
"315","48 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated {from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning his answer to his two wives Umm Salamah and Maymunah, may Allah's pleasure be upon them, when the blind Ibn Umm Maktum entered upon him while they were with him after the veil had been revealed: Veil yourselves from him. So we said: O Messenger of Allah, he is blind, he does not see us nor recognize us - and concerning his saying to them: Are you two blind?} 288 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from {Nabhan the freedman of Umm Salamah, that Umm Salamah informed him that she was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Maymunah. She said: While we were with him, Ibn Umm Maktum came, and entered upon him, and that was after veiling had been commanded. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Veil yourselves from him. We said: O Messenger of Allah, is he not blind, neither seeing us nor recognizing us? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Are you two blind? Do you two not see him?}?"
"316","289 - Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us, Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to me, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, from Nabhan the freedman of Umm Salamah, {from Umm Salamah who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and with him was Maymunah. Ibn Umm Maktum sought permission, and that was after the veil. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Stand up. I said: O Messenger of Allah, he is blind, he does not see us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Are you two blind?} In this hadith is what has indicated that when Allah the Almighty veiled the Mothers of the Believers from people, preventing them from seeing them by His saying: {And when you ask them for anything, ask them from behind a veil} - that in that was the veiling of people from them just as He veiled them from people, and that it is forbidden for them to look at people to whom looking at them is forbidden. The blind and the seeing both enter into that. Then one imagining imagined that what is in this hadith of what we mentioned contradicts what is in the narrated hadith concerning A'ishah, may Allah's pleasure be upon her."
"317","290 - And it is what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Urwah, from A'ishah who said: {I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) screening me with his cloak while I looked at the Abyssinians playing, and I was a young girl. So estimate the measure of a young, lively girl of recent age.}"
"318","291 - And what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, he said: Amr said, from Abu al-Aswad, from Urwah, from A'ishah {that one day he was with me - meaning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and the Sudanese played with shields and spears. Either I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), or he said: Do you wish to look? I said: Yes. So he stood me behind him level with his cheek, and he was saying: Take your share, O Banu Arfidah - until I grew weary. He said: Is it enough for you? I said: Yes. He said: Go.}"
"319","292 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, and Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to me, from Ibn al-Had, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith, from Abu Salamah, from A'ishah who said: {The Abyssinians entered the mosque playing, and he said to me: O Humayra', would you like to look at them? I said: Yes. So he stood at the door, and I came to him and placed my chin upon his shoulder and rested my face against his cheek. Of their speech that day: Abu al-Qasim, pleasant. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Enough for you. I said: O Messenger of Allah, do not hasten. So he stood, then said: Enough for you. I said: Do not hasten, O Messenger of Allah. She said: It was not love of looking at them in me, but I wished that the women would reach knowledge of his standing toward me, or my place with him.}"
"320","293 - And what Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to me, Ibn Shihab narrated to me, Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me, from Abu Hurayrah who said: {Umar ibn al-Khattab entered while the Abyssinians were playing in the mosque, and he scolded them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave them, O Umar, for they are the Banu Arfidah.} Our answer to him about that was that what is in this hadith of A'ishah has not appeared to us as contradicting the hadith of Umm Salamah and Maymunah which we narrated in the first section of this chapter. What is in the hadith of Umm Salamah and Maymunah is open in meaning, establishing that it was after the revelation of the veil, and that what is in it of the address of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Umm Salamah and Maymunah, his two wives, was to two adult women upon whom worship was binding. The hadith of A'ishah has in it no mention of the prior revelation of the veil concerning the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from people, nor of the veiling of people from them. No one may carry it as being after the revelation of the veil without his opponent carrying it as being before the revelation of the veil, so they counterbalance in that, and when they counterbalance it falls away. It is also possible that what is in the hadith of A'ishah was when she had not yet reached the age of women, so acts of worship had not attached to her, and that which was from her simply was, with no devotional obligation upon her. This objector said: In what you narrated from A'ishah is what must be rejected and left unaccepted; for in it is the play of the Sudanese with shields in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and that is of amusement which is not proper in other mosques, so how in it, despite its sanctity surpassing theirs, other than the Sacred Mosque. That was connected with what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"321","294 - Of what Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Bakr al-Sahmi narrated to us. Abu Ja'far said: He was not of the Sahm of Quraysh; he was of the Sahm of Bahilah - from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik."
"322","And what Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Humayd narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik who said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah while they had two days in which they played in the Jahiliyyah, so he said: Allah has replaced them for you with better than them: the day of Fitr and the day of Sacrifice.} Our answer to him in that, by Allah's guidance, was: That which is in the hadith of A'ishah of what the Sudanese did in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is not of blameworthy amusement; for it is of what is needed of its like in war, so that is praiseworthy from them in the mosque and in other than it. What is in the hadith of Anas of what they used to do in the Jahiliyyah of play was in the manner of amusement, with whose like no enemy is met, nor is there benefit in it for Islam or its people, so that is blameworthy from its people, not praiseworthy from them. There has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning a kind of amusement that returns to it that it is an instrument in fighting the enemy, and that it is praiseworthy."
"323","295 - As Bakkar narrated to us, Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Hisham al-Dastuwa'i narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Sallam, from Abd Allah ibn Zayd al-Azraq, from Uqbah ibn Amir, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: {Allah the Almighty admits three into Paradise by a single arrow: its maker who seeks reward in his making, the one who shoots it, and the one who hands it. So shoot and ride, and that you shoot is dearer to me than that you ride. There is no amusement except in three: a man's training of his horse, his dallying with his wife, and his shooting with his bow. Whoever abandons shooting after learning it, it was a blessing he has been ungrateful for.}"
"324","296 - Al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Hisham ibn Zakariyya narrated to us with its chain."
"325","297 - And as al-Rabi' narrated to us, Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abu Raja' narrated to us, Abu Sallam narrated to me, Khalid ibn Zayd narrated to me, he said: Uqbah ibn Amir said to me: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... then he mentioned the like of it. What we narrated of this hadith of Uqbah has indicated that whatever of amusement is intended for teaching the instrument of war, of what is commanded, its people are praiseworthy for it. It became clear from what we mentioned, by Allah's guidance and help, that there is nothing in what we narrated in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) contradicting anything of what we narrated from him therein, and that each kind of it is for a meaning he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended by it, and that distinguishing that and placing it in its places is taken from the people of knowledge of its like, not from others besides them. Praise be to Allah."
"326","49 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated {from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning his saying to Umm Salamah, his wife: When one of you has a mukatab (contracted slave) and he has what he can pay, then let her veil herself from him.} 298 - Al-Muzani narrated to us, al-Shafi'i narrated to us, Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Nabhan the freedman of Umm Salamah, that he was with her, and that she asked: How much remains upon you of your contract? He mentioned something he had named, so she commanded him to give it to her brother or her nephew, and she cast the veil from him, and said: Peace be upon you. And she mentioned from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he said: {When one of you has a mukatab, and he has what he can pay, then let her veil herself from him.} Sufyan said: I heard it from al-Zuhri, and Ma'mar confirmed it."
"327","299 - Muhammad ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Dawud ibn Abi Zanbar narrated to us, Malik ibn Anas narrated to us, Ibn Shihab narrated to me, that Nabhan the freedman of Umm Salamah narrated to him that while he was traveling with Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), on the road of Makkah, with two thousand dirhams remaining of his contract, he said: I used to cling to her so that I might enter upon her and see her. She said while traveling: What remains upon you of your contract, O Nabhan? I said: Two thousand dirhams. She said: Are they with you? I said: Yes. She said: Pay what remains upon you of your contract to Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Umayyah, for I have aided him with them in his marriage. And peace be upon you. Then she cast the veil before me. So I wept and said: By Allah, I shall never give them to him. She said: By Allah, O my son, you shall never see me again; {the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon us that when the mukatab of one of us has full payment of what remains upon him in his contract, then we draw the veil before him.}"
"328","300 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Musa al-Absi narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Isma'il ibn Mujammi' informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Nabhan the freedman of Umm Salamah, then he mentioned the like of it. We reflected upon what is in this hadith of what was mentioned of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) therein to his wife Umm Salamah, after standing upon it and upon other than it of the narrated reports concerning kitabah, that the mukatab is not freed by the casting of the veil between him and whoever contracted with him upon it. Then we reflected upon the meaning of this saying of his: When one of you has a mukatab and he has what he can pay - of what has been clarified in some of what we narrated thereof in this chapter, that it is full payment of what remains upon him of his contract - that she veil herself from him, while he is not yet freed; that is with him before his paying it on behalf of himself from his contract to whoever contracted with him. We found Allah the Exalted mention what He permitted the wives of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of looking at whom He permitted them to look at of people, and permitted to whom He permitted them that of it looking at them, by His saying: {There is no blame upon them concerning their fathers} to {nor what their right hands possess}. We found that whoever contracted with them of what we mentioned has entered into what their right hands possess, by the indication upon that from this hadith. What indicated concerning whoever they contracted with of mukatabah was of what, when the mukatab whose term has come pays it, he is freed by it, and looking at his mistress who is of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) becomes forbidden for him. His delaying that so that looking at her remains vast for him by her ownership of him is forbidden for him; for he withholds an obligation upon him so that there remains for him what becomes forbidden for him when he pays that obligation to whom it is due upon him. This is the aspect of his saying to his wife Umm Salamah: {When one of you has a mukatab, and he has what he can pay, then let her veil herself from him.} Among what is extracted from this hadith of rulings that enter into it, along with the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), others of people besides them: We found the mukatabah in her state of contract may pray without a head-cover, and when she is cleared of her contract by paying it to whoever contracted with her, that is not for her, and upon her is to pray as the rest of women pray, with a head-cover. Her withholding her contract so that that remains vast for her in her prayer is forbidden for her. We saw her in her waiting period from her husband's death or his divorcing her, observing half the waiting period of the free woman; when she pays and is freed, she shifts from that, and is in what is obligatory upon her of waiting periods like the rest of free women besides her. In her waiting period before paying her contract there is no mourning observance upon her therein, while after paying it, upon her therein is of mourning observance what is upon the rest of free women besides her in its like. If she withholds her contract so that what is lawful for her of that remains vast for her, and so that she be in her waiting period contrary to the rest of free women besides her, that is forbidden for her. We saw her in her contract may travel without a mahram wherever she wishes, while after paying her contract she is in that contrary to this ruling. If she withholds her contract so that this meaning remains vast for her, it is forbidden for her. We found the rest of mukatabs of males in their state of contracts have no zakah upon them in their wealth, while after their paying their contracts and their freedom thereby, they are therein contrary to that, of the obligatoriness of zakahs upon them as their obligatoriness upon the rest of those of zakah among them in their wealth. If they withhold their contracts for the dropping of zakahs from them in their wealth, had they paid their contracts, that is forbidden for them. These are aspects of fiqh found in the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with which he addressed his wife Umm Salamah, upon which the people of fiqh must stand and reflect, of benefits in the sayings of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and of meanings none knows but Allah the Exalted, of what He sends down in His Book and what He runs upon the tongue of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"329","50 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the lifting of knowledge from people and its seizure from them. 301 - Al-Rabi' narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, I heard al-Layth say: Ibrahim ibn Abi Ablah narrated to me, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jurashi, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr that he said: Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i narrated to me: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked to the sky one day and said: This is a time knowledge is lifted. An Ansari man called Labid ibn Ziyad said to him: O Messenger of Allah, is knowledge lifted when it has been established and hearts have retained it? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: I used to consider you among the most understanding of the people of Madinah. Then he mentioned the misguidance of the Jews and Christians despite what is in their hands of the Book of Allah the Exalted. He said: Then I met Shaddad ibn Aws and narrated to him the hadith of Awf, and he said: Awf spoke the truth. Shall I not inform you of the first of that to be lifted? Humility, until you see no humble one.}"
"330","302 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Khattab ibn Uthman al-Fawzi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Himyar narrated to us, Ibn Abi Ablah narrated to me, from al-Walid al-Jurashi, Jubayr narrated to us, from Awf, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said in place of Labid ibn Ziyad: Ziyad ibn Labid, and except that he said: {Is it lifted, O Messenger of Allah, while among us is the Book of Allah, and we have taught it to our sons and our women?}"
"331","303 - Al-Rabi' al-Jizi and al-Husayn ibn Nasr al-Baghdadi narrated to us, they said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed me, Abu Isma'il Ibrahim ibn Abi Ablah narrated to us, that al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to him from Jubayr, {from Awf who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: This is a time knowledge is lifted. We said: O Messenger of Allah, is knowledge lifted while with us is the Book of Allah - we have recited it and taught it to our children and our women? He mentioned the misguidance of the two Peoples of the Book, the Jews and the Christians, then said: Its going is by the going of its vessels. Jubayr said: Then I met Shaddad ibn Aws and mentioned to him the hadith of Awf, and he said: Awf spoke the truth. The first to be lifted is humility, until you see no humble one.}"
"332","304 - Fahd narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father {from Abu al-Darda' that he said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he fixed his gaze upon the sky and said: This is a time knowledge is snatched from people until they cannot retain anything of it. Ziyad ibn Labid al-Ansari said: O Messenger of Allah, how is it snatched from us when we have recited the Qur'an? By Allah, we shall surely recite it and teach it to our women and our sons. He said: May your mother be bereaved of you, O Ziyad. I used to count you among the jurists of the people of Madinah. This Torah and Gospel are with the Jews and Christians - of what avail are they to them?} Jubayr said: Then I met Ubadah ibn al-Samit and said to him: Do you not hear what your brother Abu al-Darda' says? And I informed him of what he said. He said: Abu al-Darda' spoke the truth. If you wish, I shall narrate to you the first knowledge to be lifted from people: humility. It is imminent that you enter the congregational mosque and see in it no humble one."
"333","305 - Al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad ibn Musa narrated to us, Waki' ibn al-Jarrah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, {from Ziyad ibn Labid who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned something, and that was at the time of the going of knowledge. We said: O Messenger of Allah, how does knowledge go when we recite the Qur'an and teach it to our sons and our women, and our sons shall teach it to their sons until the Day of Resurrection? He said: May your mother be bereaved of you, O son of Umm Labid. I used to see you among the most understanding men of Madinah. Are not these Jews and Christians reciting the Torah and the Gospel, understanding nothing of what is in them?} Abu Ja'far said: An objector rejected these hadiths and said: How can knowledge be lifted in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when his days are the blessed days that have no likes, and revelation only descended upon him therein? It is impossible that the knowledge which descends therein, and remains in people's hands for some of them to convey to others until the Day of Resurrection as they were commanded therein, be lifted in those days; for had that been so, conveyance would be cut off, people would remain in the days of the Messenger of Allah without knowledge, and after him in their departure from him they would be more erroneous - and this is impossible; for knowledge was only taught for successors to take from predecessors until the Day of Resurrection. Our answer to him in that was that this hadith is among the finest and soundest of hadiths, and that what is in it of the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looking to the sky, and of his saying at that: This is a time in which knowledge is lifted - is only a signal from him to a time in which knowledge shall be lifted, which may be a time after him; for this is only a word by which things are pointed to. Of that is His saying: {This is your day which you were promised} - they were not in it on the day that was revealed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Of it is His saying: {This is what you are promised, for every oft-returning keeper} - not upon anything visible on the day that was said to them, in many likes of this in the Qur'an. Like that is what is in the hadith of Awf: It is possible that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked to the sky, he was shown therein the time in which knowledge shall be lifted, so he said what he said on account of that. Among what indicates what we mentioned of this is his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) adducing the misguidance of the two Peoples of the Book, the Jews and Christians - with the Jews having among them the Torah and the Christians having among them the Gospel, yet they did not prevent them from misguidance; that was only after the going of their prophets (blessings of Allah be upon them), not in their days. Likewise what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) threatened his nation with in this hadith of Awf is possible to be after his days, and after the going of whoever followed him and succeeded him in right guidance of his Companions, may Allah's pleasure be upon them, and of the rest of his nation besides them. In the hadith of Awf which we mentioned is the saying of Jubayr: Then I met Shaddad ibn Aws and mentioned that to him, and he said: Awf spoke the truth. The first of that to be lifted is humility, until you see no humble one. The humility which Shaddad meant in this hadith - and Allah knows best - is submission, humbleness, and lowliness before Allah the Almighty."
"334","And likewise al-Walid ibn Muhammad al-Tamimi al-Nahwi, Abu al-Qasim known as Wallad, narrated to us, Abu Ja'far al-Masadiri narrated to us, from Abu Ubaydah Ma'mar ibn al-Muthanna concerning the saying of Allah the Exalted: {And it is indeed large except upon the humble} - he said: The humble are the submissive, the lowly. Abu Ja'far said: Meaning, before Allah the Exalted, until that is seen in them and is a mark for them, as He the Exalted said in describing the Companions of His Prophet: {And those with him are severe against the disbelievers} to His saying: {Their mark is in their faces from the trace of prostration}. The trace of prostration, in what has been narrated from the predecessors, is:"
"335","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Humayd al-A'raj, from Mujahid {Their mark is in their faces from the trace of prostration} - he said: Humility and lowliness."
"336","And with it, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Mujahid: {Their mark is in their faces from the trace of prostration} - he said: Humility."
"337","And what Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Habban ibn Hilal narrated to us, from Aban ibn Yazid, from Malik ibn Dinar, from Mujahid who said: {Their mark is in their faces} - he said: The trace of dust."
"338","And what Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Harun ibn Isma'il al-Khazzaz narrated to us, from Ibn al-Mubarak, from Malik ibn Dinar who said: I heard Ikrimah, asked about {Their mark is in their faces from the trace of prostration} - he said: The trace of dust. Abu Ja'far said: All of this is the description of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). How could we think these descriptions are lifted from them? In what we mentioned is what strengthens the interpretation upon which we interpreted what Awf narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to which we were carried by what has been narrated from Shaddad therein of proof of the lifting of knowledge in the time in which it is lifted - we seek refuge with Allah from it; for it is the time in which there is no humility among people, and when there is no humility with them, there is with them hardness and arrogance - we seek refuge with Allah from that. In the hadith of Yahya ibn Ayyub which returns to Awf and Shaddad, of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the going of knowledge, that it is the going of its vessels. Like that is what has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him):"
"339","306 - As Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: {Allah does not seize knowledge by snatching it away, but seizes it by seizing the scholars, until when He leaves no scholar, people take ignorant heads. They are asked and give fatwa without knowledge, so they go astray and lead astray.}"
"340","307 - And as Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Abu Yahya al-Asadi Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Kunasa narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"341","308 - And as Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us, Shuja' ibn al-Walid narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, then he mentioned with its chain."
"342","309 - And as Fahd narrated to us, Abu Ghassan narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwah informed me, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"343","310 - And as Yunus and Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil both narrated to us, they said: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it."
"344","311 - Fahd narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab who said: Urwah informed me, from A'ishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Abu Ja'far said: Thus Yunus ibn Yazid said in this hadith: from A'ishah in place of Ibn Amr, in what we narrated before it. Ma'mar differed with him in that from al-Zuhri, saying therein: from Ibn Amr."
"345","312 - As Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us, Mu'ammal ibn Yihab narrated to us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned this hadith. When this disagreement occurred in this hadith in its chain, we investigated that to stand upon the correct of it."
"346","313 - We found al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Azdi narrated to us, he said: Talq ibn al-Samh al-Lakhmi narrated to us, Abu Shurayh Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh narrated to us, Abu al-Aswad narrated to us, from Urwah, from A'ishah that she said to him: O son of my brother, I have been informed that Abd Allah ibn Amr ibn al-As shall perform Hajj in this year of mine, and that he has retained many hadiths from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Urwah met Abd Allah ibn Amr and informed him, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say - then he mentioned this hadith. It grew strong in our hearts that this hadith returns to Abd Allah ibn Amr, not to A'ishah, until we stood upon what is worthier than that."
"347","314 - And it is what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Harun ibn Sa'id al-Ayli informed me, Khalid ibn Nizar al-Ayli narrated to me, Qasim ibn Mabrul narrated to me, from Yunus, from Ibn Shihab, Urwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, and from A'ishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - then he mentioned this hadith. By that we established that the hadith was with Urwah from A'ishah and from Ibn Amr both. Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Ansari narrated this hadith from Urwah, returning it to Ibn Amr, not to A'ishah."
"348","315 - As al-Muttalib ibn Shu'ayb ibn Habban al-Azdi and Fahd narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to me, from Urwah, from Ibn Amr, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned this hadith. There has also been narrated in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from other than A'ishah and other than Ibn Amr:"
"349","316 - What Ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, al-A'mash informed us, from Shaqiq who said: I was with Abd Allah and Abu Musa in the mosque, and they said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Before the Hour are days in which ignorance descends, knowledge is lifted, and harj increases}; and harj is killing."
"350","317 - And what Fahd narrated to us, Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Ubaydah, from Abu Wa'il who said: Ibn Mas'ud and Abd Allah ibn Qays sat in a side of the right part of the mosque. Ibn Mas'ud said: Narrate to us, O Abu Musa. Narrate to us of the days you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that shall be before the Hour. Abu Musa said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {There shall come upon you days in which knowledge is seized, ignorance descends, and harj increases. Ibn Mas'ud said: What is harj? He said: It is killing, in Abyssinian (al-Habashiyyah).}"
"351","318 - And what Fahd narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Burqan narrated to us, from Yazid - meaning Ibn al-Asamm - from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Tribulations appear, harj increases. We said: What is harj? He said: Killing, and knowledge is seized. Umar said when he heard Abu Hurayrah transmitting it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): As for the seizure of knowledge, it is not a thing snatched from men's chests, but it is the passing away of scholars."
"352","319 - And what Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, Shayban - meaning al-Nahwi - narrated to us, from Asim, from Ziyad ibn Qays, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Woe to the Arabs from an evil that has drawn near. Knowledge is seized, and harj increases. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is harj? He said: Killing.}"
"353","320 - And what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to me, Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Zabban ibn Fa'id, from Sahl ibn Mu'adh, from his father: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The nation shall remain upon a law so long as three things do not appear among them: knowledge is seized from them, the child of sin increases among them, and the saqqarun appear among them. They said: What are the saqqarun, O Messenger of Allah? He said: A generation that shall be in the last age; their greeting among them when they meet is mutual cursing.} In what we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of these reports is what has indicated that the time of the lifting of knowledge is upon an age that had not yet come when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said therein what he said; it is only upon an age that shall be before the Hour. All the reports of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we narrated in this chapter have agreed, each confirming the other. Success is from Allah."
"354","51 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning whoever revelation would descend upon him while he was in her blanket. 321 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Affan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, Awf ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from his sister Rumaythah daughter of al-Harith, from Umm Salamah {that the women said to her: People seek out with their gifts the day of A'ishah, and we love good as A'ishah loves it. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) comes to you, say to him: People seek out with their gifts the day of A'ishah, and we love good as A'ishah loves it. If only you commanded people to gift to you wherever you are. She said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, I said it to him and he turned away from me. When he went out, they said to her: What did you do? She said: I said it to him and he turned away from me. They said: Repeat it to him. So I repeated it to him and he turned away from me, then said: O Umm Salamah, do not hurt me concerning A'ishah. By Allah, there is no woman among you upon whom revelation descends while I am in her blanket, except A'ishah. She said: I said: It is settled; by Allah, I shall never hurt you concerning her.} Then an objector said: There has been narrated from Umm Salamah in other than this hadith what contradicts what is in this hadith."
"355","322 - He mentioned what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, from Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar, from al-Zuhri, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Ka'b - and he was the guide of Ka'b when he went blind - who said: I asked Ka'b about his account when he stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the expedition of Tabuk. He mentioned that he narrated it to him, saying therein: Ka'b said: {Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - and she was kind in my affair - informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was with her that night - meaning the one in which his repentance descended. She said: When a third of the night remained, our repentance descended upon him. He said: O Umm Salamah, Ka'b and his two companions have been forgiven. She said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I not send to him to give him glad tidings? He said: Then people would crowd you and prevent you from sleep the rest of the night. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed of Allah's repentance upon us after he prayed Fajr.} Our answer to him about that, by Allah's guidance, was that what is in this hadith does not contradict what is in the first hadith; for what is in this hadith is only Umm Salamah's informing that the repentance of Ka'b and his two companions was sent down upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in her house and on her night, nothing besides that. It is possible that it descended upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was in other than her blanket. In the first hadith is Umm Salamah's affirmation from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by his saying: By Allah, there is no woman among you upon whom revelation descends while I am in her blanket, except A'ishah. In that is affirmation that the descent of revelation was upon him while he was in the blanket of A'ishah; that is not in the second hadith which we mentioned in this chapter. We ask Allah for success."
"356","52 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his forbidding the adorning of horses with bowstrings. 323 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Hibban ibn Musa narrated to us, Abd Allah - meaning Ibn al-Mubarak - informed us, Utbah ibn Abi Hakim informed me, al-Husayn ibn Harmalah narrated to me, from Abu Musabbih, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Horses have good tied in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection, their people are aided upon them, wipe their forelocks, invoke blessing for them, adorn them but do not adorn them with bowstrings.} This - I mean the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): nor adorn them with bowstrings - is of what people discussed concerning the intent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by it. Of what they said in that, of what Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz permitted us, from Abu Ubayd, as though narrating it from another speaker, who said: The bowstrings here are blood-revenge claims (dhuhul). He says: Do not seek upon them the revenge claims by which you were wronged in the Jahiliyyah. Abu Ubayd said: Other than this is more resembling the correct view in my opinion. I heard Muhammad ibn al-Hasan say: Its meaning is bowstrings; they used to adorn them with them and they would be strangled by them. He said: Of what confirms that is the hadith of Hushaym, from Abu Bishr, from Sulayman al-Yashkuri, from Jabir: {The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded the cutting of bowstrings from the necks of horses.} Abu Ubayd said: It reached me from Malik that he said: That was only done with them from fear of the evil eye upon them. Abu al-Mundhir al-Wasiti Isma'il ibn Umar narrated it to me from him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them to cut them; for they avert nothing of Allah's decree. Abu Ubayd said: This resembles what they used to do with amulets. Abu Ja'far said: As for what Abu Ubayd narrated from Abu al-Mundhir from Malik in the interpretation of this hadith, he only took it, in our view - and Allah knows best - from his hadith which:"
"357","324 - Yunus narrated it to us, Ibn Wahb informed us: That Malik narrated to him from Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from Abbad ibn Tamim: That {Abu Bashir al-Ansari informed him: That he was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in one of his journeys. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent a messenger. Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr said - I thought he said: While people were in their night lodging - That no necklace nor bowstring shall remain upon the neck of a camel except it be cut.} Malik said: I consider that from the evil eye."
"358","325 - Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated it to us, Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, from Malik, from Abd Allah, from Abbad, from Abu Bashir: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in one of his journeys sent a man and said: Leave no bowstring necklace, nor a necklace upon a neck - meaning - except you cut it.} Abu Ja'far said: We reflected upon the hadith of Jabir which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter, and found in it the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) command to adorn horses, by his saying: And adorn them. That was intelligible as his intending the adorning people do, which is the adorning of horses in their necks. Then he followed that with his saying: Nor adorn them with bowstrings. By that is negated that he intended blood-revenge claims, and established by it that what he adorns in their necks of what he commanded their adorning with is what is not feared for them, as is feared for them from bowstrings when adorned with them. By that became clear the soundness of what Muhammad ibn al-Hasan said in his interpretation of this meaning. We ask Allah for success."
"359","53 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: We are more deserving of doubt than Ibrahim - and what he mentioned with it besides it in the hadith in which that is mentioned. 326 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah and Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {We are more deserving of doubt than Ibrahim, when he said: {My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead} to His saying {my heart}. May Allah have mercy upon Lut; he certainly used to resort to a strong support. Had I remained in prison as Yusuf remained, I would have answered the caller.}"
"360","327 - Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Aban Abu Ali narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Isa ibn Talid narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, Bakr ibn Mudar narrated to me, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Yunus ibn Yazid, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah and Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: {My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead}, and did not say When he said: {My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead}."
"361","328 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Dawud ibn Abi Zanbar narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from al-Zuhri: That Ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Ubayd informed him, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Zakariyya also, exactly."
"362","329 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Asma' narrated to us, my uncle Juwayriyah ibn Asma' narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zuhri: That Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Ubayd informed him, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Zakariyya also, exactly. We reflected upon the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): We are more deserving of doubt than Ibrahim, when he said: {My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead}. We found Ibrahim (peace be upon him) had seen of Allah's signs in himself the sign whose like he had never seen: his enemies casting him into the fire, and it did nothing to him by Allah's revelation to it: {O fire, be coolness and safety upon Ibrahim} - so it was a miraculous sign whose like was never seen before it nor after it. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, to negate doubt from Ibrahim at his saying: {My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead} - meaning: We, who have not seen of Allah's signs the sign which Ibrahim was shown in himself, do not doubt; so Ibrahim, with his seeing it in himself, is worthier of not doubting. As for His saying to him: {Did you not believe? He said: Yes indeed} - that has verified that his saying: {My Lord, show me how You give life to the dead} was not upon doubt from him, but for other than that: his seeking Allah's answer in his asking Him that, so that his heart be reassured by it, and he know thereby the loftiness of his station with Him. As for his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: {May Allah have mercy upon Lut; he certainly used to resort to a strong support} - meaning his saying to his people: {If only I had power against you} - meaning: like the power of worldly people, by which some of them obtain justice from others - {or I resort to a strong support} - meaning: of the supports of the world by whose like they used to harm him. Along with that, he has the strong Support from Allah the Exalted than which there is no support like it. But since He, glorified and mighty, does not fear missing, He sometimes delays some punishments of sinners for what He wills to delay them for: respite, or gradual leading-on from where they know not, until He sends it down upon them at His willing that concerning them - as He sent down upon the disobedient of them of Pharaoh and the rest of nations that opposed Him, departed from His command, and stubbornly rejected what His messengers (blessings of Allah be upon them) brought them. We found from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) an aspect indicating that the cause of this saying of Lut was on its account."
"363","330 - And it is what al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Adi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, Abu Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {May Allah's mercy be upon Lut; he indeed used to resort to a strong support: {If only I had power against you, or I resort to a strong support}. Allah did not send after him any prophet except among the wealth of his people.} That indicated that this saying of Lut was because he was not among the wealth of his people who would form for him a support to resort to. As for his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: {Had I remained in prison as Yusuf remained, I would have answered the caller} - meaning: For when the caller came to Yusuf, {he said: Return to your lord and ask him what is the case of the women} - the verse - meaning: I would have answered the caller; for in that is my exit from the prison in which I was."
"364","54 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning Allah's intent by His saying: {And a witness from the Children of Isra'il testified to its like, then believed, while you were arrogant} - is it Abd Allah ibn Salam or other than him? 331 - Yunus, Yazid ibn Sinan, al-Rabi' al-Jizi, Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman, Amr ibn al-Harith, Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud, Fahd, and Malik ibn Abd Allah ibn Sayf al-Tujibi Abu Sa'd narrated to us, they said: Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us, he said: I heard Malik narrating from Abu al-Nadr, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from Sa'd who said: I never heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say {to anyone walking upon the earth: He is of the people of Paradise, except to Abd Allah ibn Salam. Concerning him this verse was revealed: {And a witness from the Children of Isra'il testified to its like, then believed, while you were arrogant}.} An objector rejected that Abd Allah ibn Salam is the one meant by this verse, mentioning that meant by it is other than him, that it is in a Makkan surah, that the Islam of Abd Allah was only in Madinah, and mentioning in that:"
"365","What Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Asim, from al-Sha'bi, concerning His saying: {And a witness from the Children of Isra'il testified to its like, then believed, while you were arrogant} - he said: It is not Abd Allah ibn Salam. A Makkan verse; Abd Allah ibn Salam only embraced Islam two years before the death of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and nothing of the Qur'an was revealed concerning him. This verse was only revealed concerning a man of the Children of Isra'il in whom his people believed, while you were arrogant to believe. Al-Sha'bi was agreed with in negating this verse as revealed concerning Ibn Salam, and in negating another verse which some people said was also revealed concerning him - His saying: {Say: Allah suffices as witness between me and you, and whoever has knowledge of the Book} - by Sa'id ibn Jubayr."
"366","As Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr who said: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr about the saying of Allah the Exalted: {And whoever has knowledge of the Book} - I said: It is Ibn Salam. He said: How can it be Abd Allah ibn Salam, when this surah is Makkan? He said: And Sa'id used to recite (And from His presence is knowledge of the Book). They used to strengthen that with what he narrates from Ibn Abbas."
"367","As Ahmad ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Hisham al-Bazzar narrated to us, al-Khaffaf narrated to us, from Harun al-Nahwi, from Ja'far ibn Abi Wahshiyyah, from Ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas that he used to recite: (And from His presence) with kasrah, saying: From Allah's presence is knowledge of the Book. We reflected upon this chapter: Did al-Sha'bi and Sa'id ibn Jubayr contradict anyone of their likes therein?"
"368","Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Warqa' narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: {And a witness from the Children of Isra'il testified} - he said: He is Abd Allah ibn Salam."
"369","And as Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Azhar ibn Sa'd al-Samman narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi concerning this verse: {And a witness from the Children of Isra'il testified to its like} - he said: They say: Ibn Salam. How can it be Ibn Salam, when this verse is Makkan? Ibn Awn said: I was informed that Muhammad - meaning Ibn Sirin - said: He spoke the truth; it is Makkan. Abu Ja'far said: Meaning the surah in which that verse is, and it is Surah al-Ahqaf. But a verse would descend and be commanded to be placed in such-and-such place. Abu Ja'far said: Meaning that a verse would descend in Madinah and be commanded to be placed in a surah that had descended in Makkah. Then we returned to the hadith of Malik which we narrated at the beginning of the chapter, and uncovered it to stand upon its reality."
"370","332 - We found Ibn Abi Dawud, Fahd, and Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn Safwan al-Nasri al-Dimashqi narrated to us, they said: Abu Mushir Abd al-A'la ibn Mushir al-Ghassani narrated to us, Malik narrated to us, from Abu al-Nadr, from Amir ibn Sa'd, from Sa'd who said: {I never heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say to anyone walking upon the earth that he is of the people of Paradise except Abd Allah ibn Salam.} He did not mention therein the revelation of that verse concerning him. Something of that fell into our hearts, so we uncovered it also until we stood upon the reality therein, by Allah's favor and help."
"371","333 - We found Yunus narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd Allah ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Malik, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. He said therein: Malik said: Concerning him was revealed: {And a witness from the Children of Isra'il testified to its like, then believed, while you were arrogant.}"
"372","334 - We found Ahmad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: My uncle narrated to us, then he mentioned with its chain the like of it, and with what he attributed to Malik therein the like of it. By that we established that the mention of the revelation of this verse in this hadith is not from the speech of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), nor from the speech of Sa'd; it is only from the speech of Malik. By that it is excluded as proof against al-Sha'bi and Sa'id ibn Jubayr in affirming the revelation of this verse as being concerning Abd Allah ibn Salam. Then we reflected upon what has been narrated concerning its revelation besides this hadith:"
"373","We found Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, he said: Sulayman ibn Dawud, the companion of al-Tayalisah, narrated to us, Shu'ayb ibn Safwan narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr narrated to us: That al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf said to Muhammad ibn Yusuf ibn Abd Allah ibn Salam: By your father, do you know a hadith your father narrated to Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, the Commander of the Believers? He said: Which hadith, may Allah have mercy on you? For many a hadith he narrated. He said: The hadith of the Egyptians when they besieged Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: I know that hadith. So he narrated it to him, and in it was that they said to Abd Allah ibn Salam when he warned them against killing Uthman: The Jew lied, the Jew lied. He said: You have lied, by Allah, and sinned. I am no Jew; I am one of the believers - Allah, His Messenger, and the believers know that. Allah has revealed in His Book: {Say: Allah suffices as witness between me and you, and whoever has knowledge of the Book}, and the other verse: {Say: Have you considered, if it is from Allah and you disbelieved in it, while a witness from the Children of Isra'il testified to its like, then believed, while you were arrogant.} What is in this hadith of Ibn Salam's informing of the revelation of these two verses concerning him is worthier, and he was most knowing of what was revealed concerning him. We found no one of the reciters to whom recitation is attributed, of the verse we recited - His saying: {And whoever has knowledge of the Book} - except likewise. We found no one who recited it with kasrah except Ibn Abbas and Ibn Jubayr."
"374","Ibn Abi Imran narrated to us, Khalaf narrated to us, he said: Al-A'mash recited: {And whoever has} with nasb; Asim and Hamzah likewise, Nafi' likewise, Ibn Kathir likewise, and Abu Amr likewise."
"375","We mentioned in what preceded from us in our book the channel of the recitation of Asim, its return to Ali, to Ibn Mas'ud, and to Zayd ibn Thabit. The recitation of Nafi' was taken from a group, among them Abu Ja'far Yazid ibn al-Qa'qa'. Abu Ja'far's taking it was from his freedman Abd Allah ibn Ayyash, and Abd Allah ibn Ayyash's taking it was from Ubayy ibn Ka'b. Likewise Rawh ibn al-Faraj narrated to me, from Ahmad ibn Salih, that he heard him say that."
"376","The recitation of Hamzah is taken, in what Ibn Abi Imran narrated to me of what he heard from Khalaf al-Bazzar, that he recited the Qur'an to Sulaym ibn Isa ten times, and that Sulaym narrated to him that he recited it to Hamzah, and that Hamzah mentioned that he recited the Qur'an to two men - al-A'mash and Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla. Whatever was of the recitation of Ibn Abi Layla is upon the letter of Ali, and whatever was of the recitation of al-A'mash is upon the letter of Ibn Mas'ud. Of what we took of the recitation of Hamzah from other than Ibn Abi Imran: Ibn Abi Layla recited the Qur'an to his brother Isa ibn Abd al-Rahman, his brother recited it to his father, his father recited it to Ali, al-A'mash recited it to Yahya ibn Waththab, Yahya recited it to Ubayd ibn Nudaylah, Ubayd recited it to Alqamah ibn Qays al-Nakha'i, and Alqamah recited it to Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him."
"377","55 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the cause for which the two verses at the beginning of Surah al-Hujurat were revealed: {O you who believe, do not advance} - the verse - {O you who believe, do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet} - the verse. 335 - Bakkar ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us, Nafi' ibn Umar al-Jumahi narrated to us, Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated to us, from Ibn al-Zubayr who said: {Al-Aqra' ibn Habis came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Bakr said: O Messenger of Allah, employ him over his people. Umar said: Do not employ him, O Messenger of Allah. They spoke concerning that until their voices rose. Abu Bakr said to Umar: Did you intend but to oppose me? He said: I did not intend to oppose you. He said: Then was revealed: {Do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet, nor speak loudly to him in speech.} He said: After that, when Umar spoke he would not make the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hear until he asked him to repeat. He said: He did not mention his father nor his grandfather - meaning Abu Bakr and al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them.}"
"378","336 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Abi Abbad al-Makki narrated to us, Nafi' ibn Umar narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah who said: The two good ones almost perished - Abu Bakr and Umar raised their voices with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a party of Banu Tamim came to him. One of them pointed to al-Aqra' ibn Habis, brother of Banu Mujashi', and the other pointed to another man whose name I do not retain. Abu Bakr said to Umar: Did you intend but to oppose me? He said: I did not intend to oppose you. Their voices rose in that, so Allah the Exalted revealed: {O you who believe, do not raise your voices} to the end of the verse."
"379","Yusuf narrated to us, Ya'qub narrated to us, Nafi' narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Mulaykah said, and Ibn al-Zubayr said: After this verse, Umar would not make the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) hear until he asked him to repeat. Ibn Abi Mulaykah said: He did not mention that of his father Abu Bakr. Abu Ja'far said: In this hadith is that the verse revealed concerning the matter that was from Abu Bakr and Umar mentioned in this hadith is: {Do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet, nor speak loudly to him in speech.} It has been narrated that the verse revealed in that is His saying: {O you who believe, do not advance before Allah and His Messenger} - the verse."
"380","337 - As Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Makhlad al-Asbahani Abu al-Husayn narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us, Hisham ibn Yusuf narrated to me in the tafsir of Ibn Jurayj: {Do not advance before Allah and His Messenger} - Ibn Abi Mulaykah informed me that Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr informed them that a party of Banu Tamim came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Bakr said: Appoint al-Qa'qa' ibn Ma'bad ibn Zurarah. Umar said: Rather appoint al-Aqra' ibn Habis. Abu Bakr said: Did you intend but to oppose me! Umar said: I did not intend to oppose you. They disputed until their voices rose, and concerning that was revealed: {O you who believe, do not advance before Allah and His Messenger.} What is in the first two hadiths is more resembling that the verse mentioned therein is the one revealed concerning what was from Abu Bakr and Umar in the matter mentioned therein - and Allah knows best. That has been strengthened by what has been narrated of what was at its revelation of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas al-Ansari."
"381","338 - Fahd narrated to us, Musa ibn Isma'il Abu Salamah al-Minqari narrated to us, Sulayman - meaning Ibn al-Mughirah - narrated to us, Thabit narrated to us from Anas who said: When this verse was revealed: {O you who believe, do not raise your voices above the voice of the Prophet} - he said: {Thabit ibn Qays was loud-voiced. When this verse was revealed, he sat in his house and said: I am the one who used to raise my voice above the voice of the Prophet and speak loudly to him in speech. My deed has been nullified, and I am of the people of the Fire. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) missed him, so a man of his Companions came to him and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has missed you. He said: Concerning me this verse was revealed. I am the one who used to raise my voice above the voice of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and speak loudly to him in speech. My deed has been nullified, and I am of the people of the Fire. The man brought him and said: He says such-and-such. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather he is of the people of Paradise.} Anas said: We used to see him walking among us, and we knew that he is of the people of Paradise. When the day of Yamamah came, there was in some of us some exposure, so he came forth, having shrouded himself and embalmed himself, and said: Evil is what you have accustomed your peers to. He fought them until he was killed, may Allah have mercy on him. As for the revelation of the other verse which we recited in this chapter, it was in what was narrated from A'ishah in a meaning other than that meaning in which the other verse was revealed."
"382","339 - As Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, my father narrated to me in the dictation of Abu Yusuf upon them, from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Taymi, from Hibal ibn Rufaydah, from Masruq ibn al-Ajda' who said: We were with A'ishah, Mother of the Believers, on the day of Arafah, while people were asking, thinking it is the day of Sacrifice. She said to a slave-girl of hers: Bring out sawiq for Masruq and sweeten it; had I not been fasting I would have tasted it. He said to her: Have you fasted this day, when it is doubted in? She said: This verse was revealed concerning the like of this day: {O you who believe, do not advance before Allah and His Messenger.} There were a people who would precede the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in fasting and its like, so they were forbidden from that."
"383","And as al-Hasan ibn Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman Abu Ali al-Marrudhi narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Mansur al-Saluli narrated to us, Ja'far al-Ahmar informed us, from Yahya al-Jabir, from Hibal ibn Rufaydah, from Masruq: That a man fasted the day of doubt, so A'ishah said to him: Do not do it, for they used to consider that this verse was revealed concerning it: {Do not advance before Allah and His Messenger.} What we mentioned indicated, upon validating what we narrated, that each of the two verses we recited was revealed in a meaning other than the meaning in which the other of the two was revealed. In the hadith of Ibn al-Zubayr is a meaning at which one must stop: what is in the hadith of Bakkar ibn Qutaybah which we narrated, of Abu Bakr's saying to Umar: Did you intend but to oppose me? And of Umar's saying at that: I did not intend to oppose you. And what is in the hadith of Yusuf ibn Yazid and Muhammad al-Asbahani in place of that: Abu Bakr said to Umar: Did you intend but to oppose me? And Umar's saying to him at that: I did not intend to oppose you. What is in the hadith of Bakkar is worthier in our view and more resembling them; for that is a question from Abu Bakr to Umar: What did he intend by opposing him? What is in the two hadiths of Yusuf and Muhammad - Did you intend but to oppose me? - is in the manner of dispute and reproach from Abu Bakr to Umar for what was from him in that. Allah the Exalted has cleared them both of the fabrication that casts disagreement between them in this and its like, purified their hearts, and made each of them a guardian to his companion in this world and the Hereafter; for its inward does not contradict its outward. There has been narrated from Mujahid in the interpretation of Allah's saying: {Nor speak loudly to him in speech.}"
"384","What Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Warqa' narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid concerning His saying: {Nor speak loudly to him in speech as the loud speech of some of you to others} - he said: Do not call out a call; do not say: O Muhammad. Rather say a gentle saying: O Messenger of Allah. It was also narrated from him in the interpretation of His saying: {Do not advance before Allah and His Messenger.}"
"385","What Ibn Abi Maryam also narrated to us, he said: Al-Firyabi narrated to us, Warqa' narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: {Do not advance before Allah and His Messenger} - he said: Do not decide ahead of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until Allah pours it forth upon his tongue. It was narrated from al-Hasan al-Basri in that:"
"386","What Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us, Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi, Musa ibn Isma'il, and Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Humayd, from al-Hasan {Do not advance before Allah and His Messenger} - he said: Do not slaughter until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) slaughters. He said: And al-Kalbi said: Do not advance before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in word or deed. What we narrated in this chapter from al-Hasan and from Mujahid confirms what we mentioned of what agrees with it. We ask Allah for success."
"387","56 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: {Satan ties upon the back of the head of one of you three knots when he sleeps, each knot of which striking its place: Upon you is a long night. When morning comes and he does not pray, he rises lazy, evil-souled.} 340 - Al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Ibn Abi al-Zinad and Malik informed me, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Satan ties upon the back of the head of one of you when he sleeps three knots, each knot striking its place: Upon you is a long night: sleep. When he wakes, if he remembers his Lord the Almighty, a knot is undone; if he performs ablution, a knot is undone; if he prays, a knot is undone. He rises energetic, good-souled. If he does not do it, he rises evil-souled, lazy.}"
"388","341 - Fahd narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' al-Kufi narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Satan has at the head of one of you a rope in which are three knots. When he wakes and declares Allah's oneness, a knot is undone; if he stands and performs ablution, another knot is undone. When he prays, all his knots are undone, and he rises light, good-souled. If he sleeps until morning, knots remain upon him, and he rises heavy, evil-souled.} Then an objector said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when you have narrated from him the forbidding of describing the soul with vileness, and his commanding whoever wishes to say: My soul is vile, to say laqisat nafsi in place of khabuthat nafsi? And he mentioned in that:"
"389","342 - What Muhammad ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Let none of you say: My soul is vile; let him say: My soul is surly.}"
"390","343 - And what Ibn Khuzaymah also narrated to us, Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it."
"391","344 - And what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from his father: That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Let none of you say: My soul is vile; let him say: My soul is surly.}"
"392","345 - And what Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Umamah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of it. He did not say: from his father. Our answer to him in that was that describing the soul with vileness is describing it with wickedness. Of it is Allah's saying: {Vile women for vile men, and vile men for vile women.} It was disliked for a man to charge himself with wickedness when there is nothing from it necessitating that upon it, and beloved for him to say in its place: My soul is surly - though their meaning is one meaning: coarseness and harshness of character. Such is their meaning with the people of Arabic. Among those who narrated that from them is Abu Ubayd; Ali ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated that to us from him. In what he narrated to us from him in that: Of it is Umar's saying in describing al-Zubayr: He is a vehement, surly one - meaning this meaning. Since the meaning of vile was one with the meaning of surly which we mentioned, the worthier of the two for whoever wishes to describe his soul with the meaning to which they return is the better of them: what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded him with in the two hadiths of A'ishah and Sahl - until there is from his soul what deserves its being described with vileness: its abandoning prayer, its arising and choosing sleep over that. That is wickedness from it, and it thereby deserves to be described with vileness whose meaning is this wickedness, upon what is in the hadith of Abu Hurayrah which we narrated. It has become clear, praise be to Allah, that each meaning of the two meanings we mentioned in these narrations does not oppose the other meaning mentioned therein nor contradict it, and that each of them has been directed to a meaning of the two meanings mentioned in these hadiths, other than the meaning to which the other hadith of them was directed - along with that there has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with a praiseworthy chain that he said: {When morning comes and he does not pray, he rises surly-souled.}"
"393","346 - And it is what al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb ibn Sa'id al-Azdi narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Fahmi known as al-Baytari narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned the like of the two hadiths of al-Rabi' and Fahd which we mentioned in this chapter, except that he said at its end: If he does not do it - meaning {he does not remember Allah, perform ablution, nor pray - he rises surly-souled, lazy.} This has mentioned what we mentioned, and indicated that the meaning of evil-souled is surly-souled. Yet the worthier for a man's describing his soul, when there is from it no choice of blameworthy matters, while with it is coarseness and harshness of character, is with what is in the two hadiths of A'ishah and Sahl. When with it is choice of blameworthy matters, it is permitted for him to describe it with what is in the two hadiths of al-A'raj and Abu Salih from Abu Hurayrah, and of what is in the hadith of Sa'id from Abu Hurayrah - describing it with whichever of them he wills. Success is from Allah."
"394","57 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning what was from him in his gift to al-Najashi, his promising it to Umm Salamah if it returned to him by al-Najashi's death before its reaching him, his giving Umm Salamah after its return to him some of it and the rest of his wives besides her the remainder. 347 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from his mother, from Umm Kulthum daughter of Abi Salamah who said: {When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Umm Salamah, he said to her: I have gifted to al-Najashi ounces of musk and a robe. I do not consider him but dead, and I do not consider the gift I gifted to him but that it shall be returned to me. When it is returned to me, it is yours. It was as he said: Al-Najashi perished. When the gift was returned, he gave each woman of his wives an ounce of that musk, gave the remainder to Umm Salamah, and gave her the robe.}"
"395","348 - Al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Asad narrated to us, Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to us, and he mentioned the like of it. An objector rejected this hadith, saying: What is in it of the Messenger's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying concerning al-Najashi: I do not consider him but dead - is refuted by what was of the Messenger's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing people of his death on the day in which his death was, and his praying for them over him. And he mentioned in that:"
"396","349 - What Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata who said: I heard Jabir say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {A righteous man of Abyssinia, Ashamah, has died today. Come, stand and pray over him. He said: So we lined up and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed over him.} Abu Ja'far said: Ashamah is an Abyssinian word; its interpretation is gift, and it is the name of this man."
"397","350 - And what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Malik narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) announced to people al-Najashi on the day in which he died. He went out with them to the prayer ground, lined them up, and uttered four takbirs over him.}"
"398","351 - And what Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, Ibn al-Musayyib, Abu Salamah, and Abu Umamah ibn Sahl informed me, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. He did not mention Abu Hurayrah nor anyone else."
"399","352 - And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib and Abu Salamah informed me, from Abu Hurayrah, {from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That he announced to them al-Najashi, the ruler of Abyssinia, on the day in which he died, saying: Seek forgiveness for your brother.}"
"400","353 - And what Ibrahim narrated to us, Abd Allah narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to me, Uqayl narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, Ibn al-Musayyib informed me: That Abu Hurayrah narrated to him: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lined them up at the prayer ground and uttered over him - meaning al-Najashi - four takbirs.} In that is his standing upon the death of al-Najashi on the day in which his death was. How is it permissible that he say of what he had stood upon the reality of: I do not consider him but that it was? He said: Also refuting it is what has been mentioned therein of the Messenger's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) promising Umm Salamah the gift if it were returned to him, and that when it was returned to him he gave her some of it and withheld from her the remainder - in that is his breaking some of what he promised her. Far be it from Allah that it be of his character; for his promises (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to run contrary to that, until Abu Bakr would fulfill them on his behalf after his passing (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Of what has been narrated in that:"
"401","354 - What Ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from {Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Had the wealth of Bahrayn come to us, I would give you thus and thus and thus. The wealth of Bahrayn did not come until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away.} When the wealth of Bahrayn came, Abu Bakr said: Whoever had with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a debt or a promise, let him come to us. Jabir said: So I came to him and said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) promised me to give me thus. Abu Bakr gave me, then I came to him after, asking him, and he did not give me. Then I came to him and asked him, and he did not give me. Then I came to him the third time and said: I asked you and you did not give me, then I asked you and you did not give me. Either give me or be miserly toward me. He said: What disease is more diseased than miserliness? I withheld nothing from you but intending to give you."
"402","355 - And what Ibn Abi Aqil narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn Ali, from Jabir, the like of it. He said: He scooped for me a scoop, then said: Count it. I counted it and found in it five hundred. He said: Take the like of it twice."
"403","356 - And what Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, Ibn al-Munkadir informed me, from Jabir. And Amr ibn Dinar, from Muhammad ibn Ali, from Jabir who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away - and Abu Bakr was - Amr said: The first wealth that came to him was from before al-Ala' ibn al-Hadrami - Abu Bakr said: Whoever had upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a debt, or had with him a promise, let him come to us. Jabir said: I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) promised me thus and thus and thus, three times. Jabir spread his two palms, and Abu Bakr counted out for me five hundred, five hundred, and five hundred. This objector said: Since the promises of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his life were obligatory upon his successor after his passing to carry out, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his life was worthier of that. Our answer to him in that was that what he mentioned of the Messenger's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informing people of the reality of al-Najashi's death on the day in which his death was, is as mentioned. Yet it is possible that before that, when the matter of his gift was delayed from him and news of al-Najashi concerning it was cut off from him, there fell into his heart at that what falls into the hearts of others of Adam's children in what had been of what custom runs contrary to it: what was mentioned in the first hadith which we mentioned at the beginning of this chapter. Then when Allah apprised him of the reality of al-Najashi's death on the day in which his passing was, there was from him what he informed people of, of what was mentioned in the second section of this chapter. As for what was from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in giving Umm Salamah some of the gift returned to him, and giving its remainder to others of his wives besides her after his prior promise to her of all of it: It is of what is possible that when the gift was returned to him, he offered it to Umm Salamah as he had promised her, then she did not accept it except by his including the rest of his wives with her therein, out of dislike of preferring herself over them - as was from the Ansar when he invited them to allot them of Bahrayn what he wished to allot them of that, and they said: We shall not do it until you allot our brothers of the Muhajirun the like of what you allotted us of that, out of dislike of preference over them, of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them. We shall mention that with its chain in what is worthier of it than this place of this book of ours, Allah willing. What the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did in the affair of Umm Salamah is possible to be upon this meaning. In that is what has necessitated for her loftiness of rank and excellence of companionship to her fellow-wives of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"404","58 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning Allah's saying: {A company of the former ones, and a few of the later ones} and concerning His saying: {A company of the former ones, and a company of the later ones.} 357 - Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, Sharik narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman, freedman of the family of Talhah, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: {When was revealed: {A company of the former ones, and a few of the later ones}, that was hard upon the Muslims. Then was revealed {A company of the former ones, and a company of the later ones}. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I indeed hope you are a third of the people of Paradise - half of the people of Paradise - and said another time: half of the people of Paradise, sharing with them the remaining half.} We reflected upon these two verses and found the first of them preceded by Allah's saying: {And you are three kinds: the companions of the right - what are the companions of the right? The companions of the left - what are the companions of the left? The foremost, the foremost - those are the brought near.} So He made the brought-near highest in rank, noblest in station, described them with precedence, then informed that they are a company of the former ones - as though He, glorified and mighty, means of the nations that preceded them - and a few of the later ones. We found the second of them preceded by His saying: {We created them a creation, made them virgins, loving, of equal age, for the companions of the right} {A company of the former ones} {And a company of the later ones.} What is in the first - His saying: {And a few of the later ones} - is upon the brought-near; what preceded in the second verse - His saying: {And a company of the later ones} - is upon the companions of the right, and they are other than the brought-near. We found Him clarify that at the end of the surah in which are these two verses, by His saying: {Then if he is of the brought-near: rest, sweet basil, and a garden of bliss. If he is of the companions of the right: peace to you from the companions of the right. If he is of the deniers, the erring: a lodging of scalding water and roasting in Hell.} By that we understood that the brought-near are other than the companions of the right, that they are highest of the three groups in rank and station, fewest in number of the companions of the right. They are the mentioned in the first verse of the first two verses; those mentioned in the second verse of them are the companions of the right. Both pairs - the brought-near and the companions of the right - are people of Paradise, except that the brought-near among them are higher therein in rank and nobler therein in station than the companions of the right. That indicated to us that the joy of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the second verse was for what they knew by it: that of the people of Paradise besides the brought-near among them are the companions of the right - and Allah knows best what He intended by that. Then we sought what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning his nation that shall enter Paradise: how much it is of those who shall enter Paradise besides it."
"405","358 - We found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith al-Tannuri narrated to us, Hisham al-Dastuwa'i narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Imran ibn Husayn, from {Ibn Mas'ud who said: We conversed with the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one night until we wearied the talk, then returned to our families. In the morning we came early to the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: The prophets were displayed to me with their nations and their followers of their nations. A prophet would pass with three of his nation with him, a prophet with a band of his nation with him, a prophet with a company of his nation with him, a prophet with none of his nation with him - until Musa ibn Imran passed me in a multitude of the Children of Isra'il. When I saw them they pleased me, so I said: O Lord, who are these? He said: This is your brother Musa ibn Imran and whoever followed him of the Children of Isra'il. I said: O Lord, where is my nation? He said: Look to your right. I looked, and there were the hills, the hills of Makkah, teeming, blocked with the faces of men. He said: Are you pleased? I said: My Lord, I am pleased. Who are these? He said: These are your nation. Are you pleased? I said: I am pleased, my Lord. Then He said: Look to your left. I looked, and the horizon was blocked with the faces of men. He said: Are you pleased? I said: My Lord, I am pleased. He said: With these are seventy thousand who shall enter Paradise with no reckoning upon them. Ukkashah ibn Mihsan, brother of Banu Asad ibn Khuzaymah, arose and said: O Prophet of Allah, invoke Allah to make me of them. He said: O Allah, make him of them. Then another man arose and said: O Prophet of Allah, invoke Allah to make me of them. He said: Ukkashah has preceded you to it. He said: It was mentioned to us that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you can - my father and mother be ransom for you - to be of the seventy, then do. If you are unable and fall short, then be of the people of the hills. If you are unable and fall short, then be of the people of the horizon. I have seen with him people teeming, many. It was mentioned to us that men of the believers conferred among them, saying: What do you think these seventy thousand did to become of their affair? They said: These were born in Islam and kept acting by it until they died. He said: Not so; rather they are those who do not cauterize, do not seek ruqyah, do not take omens, and upon their Lord they rely. He said: It was mentioned to us that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I indeed hope to have with me of my nation a quarter of the people of Paradise. We uttered takbir. Then he said: I indeed hope you are a third. We uttered takbir. Then he said: I indeed hope you are half. We uttered takbir. Then he recited this verse: {A company of the former ones, and a company of the later ones}.}"
"406","359 - We found Yazid narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Musa al-Ammi narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan and al-Ala' ibn Ziyad, from Imran: That Ibn Mas'ud said: We conversed one night with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned this hadith - except that he mentioned therein at his saying: {When the prophet with none with him - Allah the Exalted has informed you of the people of Lut - meaning in what He said to them: {Is there not among you a right-minded man?}}"
"407","360 - We found Abu Umayyah narrated to us, he said: Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us, Isra'il informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Abd Allah who said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaned his back upon a dome of tanned hide at Mina, then said to his Companions: Are you not pleased to be a quarter of the people of Paradise? They said: Yes. He said: Are you not pleased to be a third of the people of Paradise? They said: Yes. He said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I indeed hope you are half of the people of Paradise. I shall narrate to you of the fewness of Muslims among disbelievers on the Day of Resurrection: like a black hair in the hide of a white bull, or a white hair in the hide of a black bull. None shall enter Paradise but a believing soul.}"
"408","361 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq who said: I heard Amr ibn Maymun narrating from Ibn Mas'ud who said: {We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a dome, about forty. He said to us: Are you pleased to be a third of the people of Paradise? We said: Yes. He said: By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muhammad, I indeed hope you are half of the people of Paradise. That is because none enters Paradise but a Muslim soul. You among shirk are but like the white hair in the hide of the black bull, or like the black hair in the hide of the red bull.}"
"409","362 - We found Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr, from Abd Allah, then he mentioned the like of it - except that he added, saying: {Are you pleased to be a quarter of the people of Paradise? We said: Yes. He said: Are you pleased to be a third of the people of Paradise? We said: Yes. He said: Are you pleased to be half of the people of Paradise? We said: Yes} - then he mentioned the rest of the hadith."
"410","363 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Musa and Hakim ibn Sayf narrated to us, they said: Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun al-Awdi who said: I heard Ibn Mas'ud say: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us one night and leaned his back upon a dome of tanned hide. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: To proceed: Are you not pleased to be a quarter of the people of Paradise? We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Are you not pleased to be a third of the people of Paradise? We said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I indeed hope you are half of the people of Paradise. None enters Paradise but a Muslim soul. Muslims on the Day of Resurrection in fewness are like the white hair in the black bull, and the black hair in the white bull.}"
"411","364 - We found Salih ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to us, he said: Yusuf ibn Adi al-Kufi narrated to us, Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Amr ibn Maymun, from Ibn Mas'ud who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Are you not pleased to be a quarter of the people of Paradise? People uttered takbir. He said: Are you not pleased to be a third of the people of Paradise? People uttered takbir. He said: Are you not pleased to be half of the people of Paradise? I shall narrate to you of that: Muslims among disbelievers are but like the black hair in the white bull, or like the white hair in the black bull. Then we found Allah increased him beyond what he hoped of that, making his nation two-thirds of the people of Paradise.}"
"412","365 - As Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Affan ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, al-Harith ibn Haserah narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from his father, from {Ibn Mas'ud who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: How are you with a quarter of the people of Paradise - for you its quarter, for the rest of people its three-quarters? They said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: How are you with its third? They said: That is greater. He said: How are you with the half? They said: That is greater. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people of Paradise on the Day of Resurrection are one hundred twenty rows; you are of them eighty rows.}"
"413","366 - And as Ibrahim also narrated to us, Affan narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muslim al-Qasmali narrated to us, Abu Sinan narrated to us, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The people of Paradise are one hundred twenty rows; this nation is of them eighty rows.} To this ended what we stood upon of what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter, of what Allah honored His Prophet with in his nation, giving him what He gave none other of His prophets - blessings of Allah be upon him and them. We ask Allah for success."
"414","59 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the intent of His saying: {Drive not away those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His Face} and concerning His saying: {Keep yourself with those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His Face.} 367 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Ahmad ibn al-Mufaddal al-Hafari narrated to us, Asbat ibn Nasr narrated to us, from al-Suddi, from Abu al-Kanud, from Khabbab: {Drive not away those who call upon their Lord} - the verse - he said: {Al-Aqra' ibn Habis and Uyaynah ibn Hisn came and found the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Bilal, Ammar, Suhayb, and Khabbab among some weak believers. When they saw them around him, they scorned them. They came to him and secluded him, saying to him: We wish you make for us a sitting from you by which the Arabs recognize our merit. Delegations of Arabs come to you, and we are ashamed they see us sitting with these slaves. When we come to you, remove them from us; when we finish, sit with them if you wish. He said: Yes. They said: Write for us a document upon you. He called for the sheet to write for them, and called Ali to write. When he intended that, while we were sitting in a corner, Jibril (peace be upon him) descended and said: {Drive not away those who call upon their Lord} - the verse. Then he mentioned al-Aqra' and his companion, saying: {Thus We tried some of them with others, that they say: Are these} - the verse. Then he mentioned, saying: {When those who believe in Our verses come to you} to {mercy}. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) threw the sheet, called us, and we came to him while he was saying: Peace be upon you. We drew near him, placing our knees upon his knees. When he wished to rise he would rise and leave us, so Allah the Exalted revealed: {Keep yourself with those who call upon their Lord} - the verse - meaning: the sittings of nobles; {Obey not him whose heart We made heedless} - the verse. The one whose heart he made heedless is Uyaynah and al-Aqra'; furut is ruin. Then He struck for them the parable of two men, and the parable of worldly life. {After that we would sit with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). When we reached the hour in which he rises, we rose and left him until he rises; otherwise he would ever wait until we rise.}} We reflected upon what is in this hadith of the mention of the people concerning whom the asking of al-Aqra' and Uyaynah was what they asked, and in what was revealed on account of that of His saying: {Drive not away those} - the verse - and of His saying: {Keep yourself} - the verse: Are they two specific to the group mentioned in this hadith, or are they upon whoever is of the people of the description mentioned therein - among them this group mentioned in this hadith?"
"415","We found Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us, Ibn Ajlan informed me, from Nafi', Ibn Umar informed me concerning this verse: {Keep yourself with those who call upon their Lord morning and evening, seeking His Face} - that they are those who attend the prescribed prayers."
"416","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Yazid al-Muqri' narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ajlan, and he mentioned with its chain the like of it. We understood that those meant in the two verses we recited are those who attend the prescribed prayers, that they are not two specific to the group mentioned in the hadith of Khabbab to the exclusion of others of people, that they are upon the group described in the hadith of Ibn Umar, and that of them is the group mentioned in the hadith of Khabbab and their likes of whoever would attend what they attended of the five prayers."
"417","60 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) {concerning his forbidding his riding companion, at the stumbling of his camel or his donkey, to say: May Satan perish.} 368 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Abdah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Humran narrated to us, Khalid al-Hadhdha' narrated to us, from Abu Tamimah al-Hujaymi, from Abu al-Malih, from his father who said: I was riding behind the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); my camel stumbled and I said: May Satan perish. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Do not say: May Satan perish. He grows until he becomes like the house, saying: By my strength I threw him. Rather say: In the name of Allah. He shrinks until he becomes like the fly.}"
"418","369 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Qabisah narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu Tamimah, from the riding companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - he narrated it to us once thus, and narrated it to us another time, saying therein - or whoever narrated it to me, from the riding companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - he said: {A donkey stumbled and he said: May Satan perish. He said: Do not say: May Satan perish. Rather say: In the name of Allah. When you say: May Satan perish, he grows until he is like the mountain, saying: By my strength I threw him. When you say: In the name of Allah, he shrinks until he becomes like the fly.} In what we narrated was the Messenger's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbidding his riding companion, at the stumbling of his camel or donkey, from his saying: May Satan perish, and his informing him at that of what is from Satan by cause of this saying at this occurrence. Then an objector said: You have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of {his saying to Uthman ibn Abi al-As, when told that Satan confuses his recitation and prayer, to drive him away} - that affirms it from him for him. And he mentioned in that:"
"419","370 - What Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us, Abu Umar al-Hawdi narrated to us, Khalid ibn Abd Allah al-Wasiti narrated to us, from al-Jurayri, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah, from Mutarrif, from Uthman ibn Abi al-As who said: {I said: O Messenger of Allah, Satan comes to me and confuses my recitation. He said: That is a Satan called Khinzab. When he comes to you, drive him away. I did it and he went from me.}"
"420","371 - Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, al-Firyabi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Iyas al-Jurayri, from Yazid ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Shikhkhir, from Uthman - he did not mention Mutarrif - who said: {I said: O Messenger of Allah, Satan has come between me and my prayer and recitation. He said: That is a Satan called Khinzab. When you sense him, seek refuge with Allah and spit to your left three times.} This opponent said: Do you find an aspect giving each of the two hadiths a meaning other than the other's, so contradiction and disagreement fall from them? Our answer to him in that was that Satan's authority over Adam's children is his whispering to them, casting into their hearts what they dislike, and making them forget what they remember. Of that is Allah's saying, narrating from the companion of Musa (peace be upon him): {I forgot the fish; none made me forget it but Satan, that I remember it.} And His saying: {Satan made him forget the mention of his lord, so he remained in prison some years} in the story of His prophet Yusuf (peace be upon him), and things of this kind. He was not given authority in stumbling their beasts, nor in destroying their wealth. At that they were commanded to seek refuge with Allah the Exalted from him. Of that is His saying: {When you recite the Qur'an, seek refuge with Allah from the accursed Satan.} When there was from the Prophet's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) riding companion, at the stumbling of his camel or donkey, his saying: May Satan perish - and ta's is falling - upon his making that an act of Satan, by his asking with the saying: May Satan perish, that he do to him the like of that - the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade him; for by that he places Satan as though that act was from him, when it was not from him - it was only from Allah the Almighty. He commanded him to have in its place: In the name of Allah, so that Satan not have that there was from him with him in that an act. When there was from Uthman's complaining to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of Satan what he complained to him of it, of what is imagined from him that he does to him - for it is of his authority over Adam's children - he commanded him to drive him away, which is distancing. Of it is His saying: {He said: Remain despised therein, and speak not to Me.} The meaning of each of these two hadiths came out with no contradiction in it to what is in the other hadith of them. Success is from Allah."
"421","61 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what Abu Mas'ud Uqbah ibn Amr narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of his saying: {No soul alive shall remain upon the earth after one hundred years.} 372 - Fahd narrated to us, Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Nufayli narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, Mutarrif ibn Tarif narrated to us, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, {from Nu'aym ibn Dajajah who said: I was sitting with Ali when Abu Mas'ud came. Ali said to him, rising: O Furayj, you weary people. He said: I inform them that the latter, the latter is worse. He said: Narrate to us what you heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say concerning the hundred? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There shall not be one hundred years with an eye blinking upon the earth. He said: You erred, and erred in the beginning of your fatwa. He said that only of whoever was that day; is ease or relief but after the hundred?} We reflected upon what is in this hadith of what Abu Mas'ud narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): It is what was mentioned from him therein that there shall not be one hundred years with an eye blinking upon the earth. Its outward was that after one hundred years no blinking eye remains, upon the perishing of all people - and in their perishing, the going of the world. We found therein of Ali's speech that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only meant by that speech whoever was that day upon the earth of people, not others. His following that of his own saying: Is ease or relief but after the hundred? In that was his standing upon what Abu Mas'ud had not stood upon, of what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had said. In that was proof that what was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is the perishing of that generation, without a denial from him that generations succeed them, some after others, until the Day of Resurrection. Then we found from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), agreement with Ali in what he narrated of the intent of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what Abu Mas'ud narrated from him."
"422","373 - As Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, Nuh ibn Habib al-Qumasi informed us, Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, Salim and Abu Bakr ibn Sulayman narrated to me, from Ibn Umar who said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us one night in Isha prayer at the end of his life. When he gave salam he said: Have you seen this night of yours? Upon the head of one hundred years of it, none of whoever is upon the back of the earth shall remain.}"
"423","374 - And as al-Hasan ibn Ghulayb narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Kathir ibn Ufayr narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to me, Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid ibn Musafir narrated to me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim and Ibn Sulayman ibn Abi Hathmah: That Abd Allah ibn Umar said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in Isha prayer, then mentioned the like of it.} We found from Jabir also what indicates that:"
"424","375 - As Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Adi narrated to us, Hafs ibn Ghiyath informed us, from al-A'mash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Jabir who said: {A man said: O Messenger of Allah, when is the Hour? He said: What is your asking of the Hour? No soul alive upon which one hundred years come.}"
"425","376 - And as Fahd narrated to us, Umar ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Jabir who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {There is upon the earth no soul alive upon which one hundred years come. Sulayman said: I think they mentioned the Hour with him.} We found from Anas also this meaning."
"426","377 - As Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb al-Kaysani narrated to us, Ali ibn Ma'bad al-Abdi narrated to us, Abu Malih al-Hasan ibn Umar al-Fazari narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas who said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) led us in prayer, then leaned upon a youth and said: At the head of one hundred years, none of whoever is upon the back of the earth today shall remain alive.} The narrations we mentioned from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) have agreed and harmonized that his intent in what Abu Mas'ud narrated from him, of what we mentioned, is an imagined meaning, correct - not the meaning the ignorant thought, which observation has refuted, nor of what deludes one imagining of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) neglecting from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) some of what he said in that; for their transmission from him is the transmission of the multitude, and the transmission of the multitude is innocent of that. The like of this is only when in the transmission of single narrators. If an objector says: There remained among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mukhadramun of whoever was in the Jahiliyyah and remained in Islam until passing this period - of them Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, for there has been narrated concerning his age:"
"427","What Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Affan narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Humayd al-Tawil who said: I heard Abu Uthman say: One hundred thirty years have come upon me; nothing decreased but my hope. He has in that likes, like Zirr ibn Hubaysh and Suwayd ibn Ghafalah."
"428","62 - Chapter clarifying the problematic content of what was narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) {of his saying: Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire} upon what has been narrated from him in that, his saying: Whoever lies upon me deliberately. And of his saying: Whoever lies upon me, unrestricted. And concerning the cause from which that was. 378 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Zakariyya ibn Adi narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir informed us, from Salih ibn Hayyan, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father who said: {There was a clan of Banu Layth two miles from Madinah. A man had sought in marriage a woman of them in the Jahiliyyah, and they refused to marry him. He came to them wearing a robe and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clothed me with this robe, and commanded me to judge in your blood and wealth as I see. He went and alighted upon the woman. Word was sent to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: The enemy of Allah has lied. Then he sent a messenger, saying: If you find him alive, strike his neck - I do not think you shall find him alive. If you find him dead, burn him with fire. He came to him and found a viper had bitten him, so he died, and he burned him with fire.} That is the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.}"
"429","379 - Fahd narrated to us, al-Himmani narrated to us, Ali narrated to us, from Salih, from Abd Allah ibn Buraydah, from his father who said: {A man came to a people in the side of Madinah and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to judge by my opinion among you in such-and-such. He had sought in marriage a woman of them in the Jahiliyyah, and they refused to marry him. He went until he alighted upon the woman. The people sent to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: The enemy of Allah has lied. Then he sent a man, saying: If you find him alive, strike his neck - I do not think you shall find him alive. If you find him dead, burn him. The man went and found him bitten, dead, and burned him. At that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.} In what we narrated was mention of the path at which was his saying: {Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.} This saying has been narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) by more than one of his Companions. Of them: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him."
"430","380 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith al-Tannuri and Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us, they said: Abu al-Ghusn Dujayn ibn Thabit narrated to us, a shaykh of the people of Madinah at the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to me that he heard Umar say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever lies upon me is in the Fire. He said: I said: What is the shaykh's name? He said: Sulaym - or Aslam - freedman of Umar.} Of them: Uthman ibn Affan."
"431","381 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from his father, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Uthman who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take a house in the Fire.}"
"432","382 - Al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, Ibn Abi al-Zinad informed us, from his father, Amir ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas informed me that he heard {Uthman say: Nothing prevents me from narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that I be not the most retentive of his Companions from him. But bear witness: I heard him say: Whoever says upon me what I did not say, let him take his seat in the Fire.}} Of them: Ali ibn Abi Talib."
"433","383 - As Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us, Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan and Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Rib'i who said: I heard Ali preaching, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Do not lie upon me. Whoever lies upon me shall enter the Fire.}"
"434","384 - And as Yazid narrated to us, Abu Qatan Amr ibn al-Haytham al-Quta'i narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mansur, with its chain, the like of it."
"435","385 - And as Fahd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn al-Asbahani narrated to us, Sharik ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Rib'i, from Ali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the like of it. Of them: Talhah ibn Ubayd Allah:"
"436","As narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Tamam al-Kalbi Abu al-Karawwas; (he said:) Sulaiman ibn Ayyub ibn Isa ibn Musa ibn Talha ibn Ubayd Allah narrated to us; (he said:) my father narrated to me, from my grandfather, from Musa ibn Talha, from his father Talha ibn Ubayd Allah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Whoever narrates from me and lies deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.} And among them is al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam."
"437","As narrated to us by Yazid ibn Sinan; (he said:) Abu Dawud and Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, both saying: Shu'ba narrated to us; (he said:) Jami' ibn Shaddad al-Muharibi informed me, he said: {I heard Amir ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr narrating from his father, who said: I said to al-Zubayr: What prevents you from narrating from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as Ibn Mas'ud and so-and-so narrate from him? He said: By Allah, I have not parted from him since I accepted Islam, but I heard him say: Whoever lies about me, let him take his seat in the Fire. Wahb added in his narration: By Allah, he did not say: deliberately, while you say: deliberately.}"
"438","And as narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Marzuq; (he said:) Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us, then he mentioned the same as Yazid narrated to us from Wahb of this hadith."
"439","And as narrated to us by Yazid, Ibn Khuzaimah, and Fahd, who said: Abd Allah ibn Salih narrated to us; (he said:) al-Laith narrated to me; (he said:) Ibn al-Had narrated to me, from Amr ibn Abd Allah ibn Urwa, from Abd Allah ibn Urwa, from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Zubayr, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Whoever narrates a lie from me, let him take his seat in the Fire.} And among them is Sa'id ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl."
"440","As narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Abi Imran; (he said:) Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us; (he said:) Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad informed us, from Sadaqa ibn al-Muthanna, from his grandfather Riyah ibn al-Harith, from Sa'id ibn Zayd, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.} And among them is Ibn Mas'ud."
"441","As narrated to us by Ibn Marzuq; (he said:) Affan narrated to us; (he said:) Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdala, from Zirr, from Abd Allah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.} And among them is Ibn Abbas."
"442","As narrated to us by Ibn Marzuq; (he said:) Affan narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abd al-A'la al-Tha'labi, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire, and whoever speaks about the Qur'an without knowledge, let him take his seat in the Fire.}"
"443","And as narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Zakariyya ibn Yahya Abu Shurayh; (he said:) al-Firyabi narrated to us; (he said:) Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-A'la ... then he mentioned the like of it with his chain. And among them is A'isha."
"444","As narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam; (he said:) Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us; (he said:) al-Awza'i narrated to us; (he said:) Hisn narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Salama narrated to me; (he said:) A'isha narrated to me: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever says about me what I did not say, let him take a house in the Fire.} And among them is Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan."
"445","As narrated to us by Ali ibn Ma'bad; (he said:) Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us; (he said:) Shu'ba narrated to us, from Abu al-Fayd, from Mu'awiya, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.} And among them are Ammar and Abu Musa."
"446","As narrated to us by Fahd; (he said:) Abd ibn Ya'ish narrated to us; (he said:) Yunus ibn Bukayr al-Shaybani narrated to us; (he said:) Ali ibn Abi Fatima narrated to us, from Abu Maryam, who said: I heard Ammar ibn Yasir saying to Abu Musa: I adjure you by Allah, did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.} And among them is Ibn Umar."
"447","As narrated to us by Ja'far al-Firyabi; (he said:) Qutayba ibn Sa'id narrated to us; (he said:) al-Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us, from Ubayd Allah ibn Umar, from Abu Bakr ibn Salim, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Umar, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: {Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.} And among them is Abd Allah ibn Amr."
"448","As narrated to us by Yunus and al-Rabi' al-Muradi, who said: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us. And as narrated to us by Bakkar and Ibn Marzuq, who said: Abu Asim narrated to us — then they all agreed and said: from al-Awza'i, from Hassan ibn Atiyya, from Abu Kabsha, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Convey from me even a verse, and narrate from the Children of Israel and there is no harm, and whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.}"
"449","And as narrated to us by Abu Umayya; (he said:) Abu Asim narrated to us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Amr ibn al-Walid, from Abd Allah ibn Amr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever says about me what I did not say, let him take his seat in the Fire.} And among them is Abu Sa'id al-Khudri."
"450","As narrated to us by Ibn Marzuq; (he said:) Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us; (he said:) Shu'ba narrated to us, from Abu Salama, from Abu Nadra, from Abu Sa'id: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.}"
"451","Yazid narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Qatan narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Hanifa narrated to us, from Atiyya, from Abu Sa'id, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ... then he mentioned the like of it."
"452","And as narrated to us by Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus al-Baghdadi Abu Ya'qub; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Qudama al-Massisi narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Ubayda al-Haddad narrated to us, from Hammam, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from Abu Sa'id, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ... the like of it. And among them is Anas ibn Malik."
"453","As narrated to us by Yunus; (he said:) Shu'ayb ibn al-Laith narrated to us, from his father, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever lies about me — I think he said: deliberately — let him take his house in the Fire.}"
"454","Ubayd ibn Rijal narrated to us; (he said:) Bakr ibn Khalaf al-Basri narrated to us; (he said:) al-Mu'tamir and Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.}"
"455","And Muhammad ibn Khuzaimah narrated to us; (he said:) Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us; (he said:) al-Mu'tamir narrated to us ... then he mentioned the like of it with his chain."
"456","And as narrated to us by Ubayd; (he said:) Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us, and Musa ibn al-Hasan narrated to us; (he said:) Ali ibn al-Madini narrated to us, both saying: Harami ibn Umara narrated to us; (he said:) Shu'ba narrated to us, from Qatada, from Anas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it, except that from Ubayd's book in my copy the words falyatabawwa' fell out — so said Abu Ja'far."
"457","And as narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Marzuq; (he said:) Uthman ibn Amr narrated to us; (he said:) Shu'ba narrated to us, from Hammad — meaning Ibn Abi Sulayman — who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik say: Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.}"
"458","And as narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Mas'ud al-Maqdisi al-Khayyat; (he said:) al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us; (he said:) Sallam ibn Sulaym narrated to us, from Asim ibn Sulayman, from Anas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. And among them is Zayd ibn Arqam."
"459","As narrated to us by Yazid ibn Sinan; (he said:) Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us; (he said:) Yahya ibn Sa'id Abu Hayyan al-Taymi narrated to us; (he said:) Yazid ibn Hayyan al-Taymi narrated to me, he said: {I heard Zayd ibn Arqam say: Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad sent for me and said: What are these narrations that reach me which you narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), claiming that he has a Basin in Paradise? I said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated that to us and promised us it. He said: You have lied, but you are an old man who has become senile. So I said to him: My ears heard it from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he said: Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire — and I have not lied about the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him).} And among them is Abu Hurayra."
"460","As narrated to us by Yunus; (he said:) Ibn Wahb narrated to us; (he said:) Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to me, from Bakr ibn Amr, from Amr ibn Abi Nu'ayma, from Abu Uthman al-Tunbudhi, the foster-brother of Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan, who said: I heard Abu Hurayra say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever says about me what I did not say, let him take a house in Hell, and whoever gives a fatwa without knowledge, its sin is upon the one who gave him the fatwa, and whoever counsels his brother in a matter while knowing that right guidance lies elsewhere has betrayed him.}"
"461","And Yazid ibn Sinan and Mubashshir ibn al-Hasan ibn Mubashshir ibn Mukassir al-Basri Abu Bishr narrated to us, who said: Abu Abd al-Rahman Abd Allah ibn Yazid narrated to us; (he said:) Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us; (he said:) Bakr ibn Amr al-Ma'afiri narrated to me, from Abu Uthman Muslim ibn Yasar, from Abu Hurayra, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. And among them is Abu Musa al-Ghafiqi Malik ibn Ubada."
"462","As narrated to us by Yunus; (he said:) Ibn Wahb informed us; (he said:) Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me: that Yahya ibn Maymun narrated to him: {that Wada'a al-Hamdi narrated to him: that he was beside Malik ibn Ubada Abu Musa al-Ghafiqi, while Uqba ibn Amir was narrating, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said. So Malik said: This companion of yours is heedless — or ruined; the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) charged us in the Farewell Pilgrimage, saying: Hold to the Qur'an, and you will return to a people who desire narrations from me, so whoever understands something, let him narrate it, and whoever fabricates against me, let him take a house — or a seat — in Hell.} And Yunus narrated it to us more than once, saying in some of them: aqil, and in some: ghafil. And among them is Abu Qatada al-Ansari."
"463","As narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Uzayz ibn Abd Allah ibn Ziyad ibn Uqayl al-Ayli; (he said:) Salama ibn Rawh narrated to us, from Uqayl, from Ma'bad ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, that he heard Abu Qatada al-Ansari narrating that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {O people, beware of excessive narration, and whoever narrates from me, let him say nothing but truth — or he said: what is right — or he said: one of the two — and whoever fabricates against me, let him take a house in the Fire.}"
"464","And as narrated to us by Fahd; (he said:) Ubayd ibn Ya'ish narrated to us; (he said:) Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us, from Ma'bad ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, who said: I heard Abu Qatada say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever speaks about me, let him say nothing but what is right or true, and whoever says about me what I did not say, let him take his seat in Hell.} And among them is al-Mughira ibn Shu'ba al-Thaqafi."
"465","As narrated to us by Ali ibn Ma'bad and Muhammad ibn Bahr ibn Matar, who said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us; (he said:) Sa'id ibn Ubayd Abu al-Hudhayl al-Ta'i narrated to us, from Ali ibn Rabi'a, who said: Mourning was held over Qaraza ibn Ka'b, {then al-Mughira ibn Shu'ba addressed and said: What is with wailing in this umma? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: A lie about me is not like a lie about anyone; whoever lies about me, let him take his seat in the Fire, and whoever is wailed over is punished for what is wailed over him.} And among them is Uqba ibn Amir al-Juhani."
"466","As narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud and Fahd, who said: Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us; (he said:) Yahya ibn Ayyub informed us; (he said:) al-Hasan ibn Thawban and Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me, from Hisham ibn Abi Ruqayya al-Lakhmi, who said: I heard Maslama ibn Makhlad saying to Uqba ibn Amir: Stand and narrate to the people what you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So Uqba stood and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Whoever lies about me, let him take his house in Hell. And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Silk and gold are forbidden for the males of my umma, lawful for its females.} And among them is Khalid ibn Urfuta."
"467","As narrated to us by Fahd; (he said:) Ubayd ibn Ya'ish narrated to us; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Abdi narrated to us; (he said:) Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'ida narrated to us; (he said:) Khalid ibn Salama narrated to us: that Muslim, the freedman of Khalid ibn Urfuta, narrated to him that Khalid ibn Urfuta said to al-Mukhtar: This is a lying man, and I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in Hell.} Abu Ja'far said: And in this chapter there are narrations of this kind which I have omitted, since their paths are not like the paths of these reports. And in what we have narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter, his mention of deliberateness in lying about him occurs in some, and silence about it in some, and in our view — and Allah knows best — this does not entail contradiction; for whoever lies has deliberately lied, and the threat we mentioned attaches to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioning deliberateness in what he mentioned of that is only for emphasis, not for anything else — just as a man says: I did such-and-such with my hand, and I looked at such-and-such with my eye, and I heard such-and-such with my ear — for emphasis in speech, not meaning he does it without his hand, nor that he heard it without his ear, nor that he sees it without his eye. And the Book of Allah has come with the like of what we mentioned, entailing punishment in this world and threat in the Hereafter, without mention of deliberateness in it, since it can only be with deliberateness. Of that is His saying, exalted: {The thief, male and female} the verse, and His saying: {The recompense of those who wage war against Allah and His Messenger} the verse, and He followed that with mention of the threat for them in the Hereafter. And of that is His saying, exalted: {The fornicatress and the fornicator, flog} the verse, and deliberateness is not mentioned in any of that; for these things can only be with deliberateness — for no one is a liar, nor a fornicator, nor a warrior, nor a thief, except by intending it and deliberately doing it. Likewise what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who lies about him — his mention of deliberateness in part of it and his silence about it in part — his mention of deliberateness is only for emphasis in speech, not for anything else; for what the threat attaches to can only be for the deliberate, and none is a liar, nor a thief, nor a warrior, nor a fornicator, except one who deliberately does it. Deliberate and non-deliberate differ only in the like of killing, in which a man may be a killer non-deliberately and may be a killer deliberately, each distinguished from the other by intent and mistake. And there is narrated from Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter with an added meaning which he mentioned in it, whose mention we have deferred to this place in this chapter, differing from the hadith of the group whom we mentioned."
"468","And it is what Fahd narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Sa'id al-Ashajj narrated to us; (he said:) Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Talha ibn Musarrif, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever lies about me deliberately in order to mislead thereby, let him take his seat in the Fire.} And this is a rejected hadith; no one raises it with this wording except Yunus ibn Bukayr. And Talha ibn Musarrif is not of an age to have met Amr ibn Shurahbil, given the earliness of the latter's death. And it has been narrated to us from other than the hadith of Yunus ibn Bukayr, with Abu Ammar inserted between Talha and Amr ibn Shurahbil, and it is strange."
"469","As narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb; (he said:) Muhammad ibn al-Ala' informed us; (he said:) Abu Mu'awiya narrated to us; (he said:) al-A'mash narrated to us, from Talha, from Abu Ammar, from Amr ibn Shurahbil — and he did not mention Ibn Mas'ud after him — who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever lies about me deliberately in order to mislead thereby, let him take his seat in the Fire.} And we have also found it from the hadith of al-Thawri, from al-A'mash, likewise, except that he said: from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"470","As narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb; (he said:) Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Ahmad narrated to us; (he said:) Sufyan narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Talha, from Abu Ammar, from Amr ibn Shurahbil, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said ... then he mentioned the like of it exactly. And even if the hadith were sound, it would not contradict the other hadiths we narrated in this chapter; for that may be for emphasis, not for anything else. The like of that is the saying of Allah, exalted: {Who is more unjust than one who fabricates a lie against Allah to mislead people without knowledge} — He mentioned it thus in one place, and mentioned it in the other places He mentioned it in the Qur'an without mentioning with it the addition in this place. And that, in our view, is for His emphasizing where He wills to emphasize, and omitting it where He wills to omit, and the meaning in all of it is one. And Allah, exalted, we ask for success."
"471","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: {Whoever narrates from me a hadith seeing it to be a lie is one of the liars.} 421 - Ja'far al-Firyabi narrated to us; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from al-Hakam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ali, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever narrates from me a hadith while seeing it to be a lie is one of the liars.}"
"472","And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us; (he said:) al-Aqdi, Bishr al-Zahrani, and Affan narrated to us; (he said:) Shu'ba narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Samura ibn Jundub, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"473","And Bakkar narrated to us; (he said:) Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us; (he said:) Shu'ba narrated to us, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Maymun ibn Abi Shabib, from al-Mughira ibn Shu'ba, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"474","And Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi and Bishr ibn Umar narrated to us, both saying: Shu'ba narrated to us, from Habib, from Maymun, from al-Mughira, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"475","And Ibrahim narrated to us; (he said:) Wahb narrated to us; (he said:) Shu'ba narrated to us ... then he mentioned the like of it with his chain."
"476","And al-Hasan ibn Nasr narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Nu'aym and al-Firyabi narrated to us, both saying: Sufyan narrated to us. And Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan al-Raqqi narrated to us; (he said:) al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Habib, from Maymun, from al-Mughira, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So we examined this hadith to determine what is intended by it. We found that Allah, exalted, said in His Book: {Then there succeeded after them a succession who inherited the Book} up to His saying: {Was not the covenant of the Book taken from them that they should say of Allah nothing but the truth, and they studied what is in it?} So we found Him, exalted, informing that the People of the Book are bound not to say of Allah anything but the truth. And what they take from Allah, exalted, is what they take from His Messengers, Allah's blessings upon them, to them. So among what Allah, exalted, took from them was that they should say of Allah nothing but the truth, and included in that is His taking from them that they should say of His Messengers nothing but the truth. The truth here is like it is in His saying, exalted: {Except one who testifies to the truth while they know.} And whoever testifies by conjecture has testified to other than the truth, since conjecture is as Allah, exalted, described it in His saying: {And most of them follow nothing but conjecture; conjecture avails nothing against the truth.} And in that is His informing us that conjecture is other than the truth. And since one who testifies by conjecture testifies to other than the truth, the like of him is one who narrates from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a hadith by conjecture, narrating from him other than the truth. And one who narrates from him other than the truth narrates from him falsehood, and one who narrates from him falsehood lies about him, like one of the liars about him who are included in his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {Whoever lies about me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire} — and we seek refuge with Allah, exalted, from that."
"477","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning {his praying over the Juhani woman whom he stoned upon her confession to him of adultery, and his omitting prayer over Ma'iz whom he stoned upon his confession to him.} 427 - Malik ibn Yahya Abu Ghassan al-Hamdani narrated to us; (he said:) Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' narrated to us; (he said:) Hisham al-Dastuwai narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Imran ibn Husayn: {that a woman from Juhayna came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while she was pregnant from adultery and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have incurred a hadd, so establish it upon me. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) summoned her guardian and said to him: Treat her well, and when she delivers her pregnancy, bring her to me. So he did so. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered concerning her, so her garments were tied upon her, and he ordered concerning her and she was stoned. Then he prayed over her. So Ali said to him: Do you pray over her though she committed adultery? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: She has repented a repentance which, if divided among seventy of the people of Madina, would encompass them. And have you found anything better than that she was generous with herself for Allah, exalted?}"
"478","Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; (he said:) Isma'il ibn Mas'ud narrated to us; (he said:) Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated to us; (he said:) Hisham narrated to us, from Yahya, the like of it, except that he said in place of so Ali said to him: so Umar said to him, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"479","Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam narrated to us; (he said:) Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, from Yahya, from Abu Qilaba, from Abu al-Muhajir, from Imran ... then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said in place of what is in the first hadith so Ali said to him: so Umar said to him."
"480","Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us; (he said:) al-Firyabi narrated to us; (he said:) al-Awza'i narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of the hadith of Ibn Abd al-Hakam in its chain and text exactly. So in what we narrated is the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over this woman stoned for adultery."
"481","Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Naysaburi and Nuh ibn Habib al-Qumasi informed us, both saying: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us; (he said:) Ma'mar narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Salama ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir: {that a man from Aslam came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and confessed adultery, so he turned away from him; then he confessed and he turned away from him, until he testified against himself four times. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is there madness in you? He said: No. He said: Are you married? He said: Yes. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered concerning him and he was stoned. When the stones pained him, he fled, so he was caught and stoned until he died. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said good about him, but did not pray over him.} So in this is his omitting prayer over this man stoned for adultery, and he is Ma'iz ibn Malik. So we examined all that we narrated concerning each of these two persons stoned for adultery — concerning the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over the one he prayed over, and his omitting prayer over the one he omitted prayer over — for what reason that was from him. We found that the woman whom he stoned upon her confession to him of adultery had shown toward Allah, exalted, in her confession to him of that, generosity with herself for Him, and offering of herself for the establishment of the incumbent penalty for that adultery upon her, and patience upon that until it was taken from her. And that necessitated her praise, so he prayed over her — since it was of his practice (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to pray over the praised ones of his umma. And we found what was from the man who confessed to him adultery to be contrary to that; for he did not come to him offering himself for his stoning him which would bring his death, but came to him because he saw that he would not do that to him — and we shall bring what is narrated concerning that later in our book, if Allah wills — then there was from him after that, before his life was taken, his fleeing from the establishment of Allah's penalty upon him which what he confessed against himself had made incumbent upon him. And in that was ground for doubt in his affair; for it is possible that that flight was from him due to retraction of what he confessed, or flight from the establishment of the penalty that had become binding upon him. And he was blameworthy in each of these two states, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) omitted prayer over him for that; for of his practice is not to pray over the blameworthy of his umma — just as he did not pray over the killer of himself though he was a Muslim, and just as he did not pray over the pilferer from the raiders with him at Khaybar — and we have mentioned what is narrated concerning that with its chains in what preceded from us in this book of ours, in the chapter of what is narrated from him concerning the affair of Abd Allah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul, of his praying over him or omitting prayer over him. So among what is narrated concerning the affair of the stoned man whom we mentioned is his flight from completing the stoning, and what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of speech when that reached him."
"482","What Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us; (he said:) Isma'il ibn Salim al-Sa'igh narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Mu'awiya narrated to us; (he said:) al-Nu'man ibn Thabit informed me, from Alqama ibn Marthad, from Ibn Burayda, from his father, who said: {Ma'iz al-Aslami came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was sitting, and confessed adultery, so he turned him back four times. Then he ordered his stoning, so they set him up in a place of few stones. When the stones struck him, he panicked and ran headlong until he reached the Harra. There they held their ground against him and pelted him with its large stones until he fell silent. They said: O Messenger of Allah, Ma'iz, when the stones struck him, panicked and ran headlong. He said: Why did you not leave him alone?}"
"483","And what Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us; (he said:) Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us; (he said:) Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'ida narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, who said: {It was said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Ma'iz, when he felt death and the stones, fled. He said: Did you not leave him alone?}"
"484","And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us; (he said:) Ubayd Allah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us; (he said:) Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, from Abu al-Haytham ibn Nasr ibn Dahr al-Aslami, from his father, who said: I was among those who stoned Ma'iz. When he felt the touch of the stones, he panicked severely. So we mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Why did you not leave him alone? Ibn Ishaq said: So I mentioned that of his hadith when I heard him say: why did you not leave him alone, to Asim ibn Umar ibn Qatada. He said: Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali narrated to me, he said: That of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — {Why did you not leave him alone?} — was narrated to me concerning Ma'iz by six men of Aslam. I do not suspect the people, and I did not know the hadith. So I came to Jabir and said: Men of Aslam narrate that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them, when they mentioned Ma'iz's panic at the stones: Why did you not leave him alone? I do not suspect the people, nor do I know the hadith. He said: O son of my brother, I know this hadith best. I was among those who stoned the man. We stoned him, and he felt the touch of the stones and cried out to us: O people, return me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for my people killed me and deceived me about myself, and informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not kill me. So we did not desist from him until we killed him. When we returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of what he said, he said: Why did you not leave the man and bring him to me, so that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) could ascertain from him? As for abandoning a hadd, no. So I understood the import of the hadith."
"485","And what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Mubarak informed us; (he said:) Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us; (he said:) Sufyan narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Yazid ibn Nu'aym, from his father: {Ma'iz ibn Malik came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have committed adultery, so establish Allah's Book upon me — until he came four times. He said: Take him and stone him. When the stones touched him, he started and ran violently. Then Abd Allah came out from his desert camp and struck him with a donkey's foreleg, felling him. The people pelted him until they killed him. His flight was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Why did you not leave him alone? Perhaps he would repent and Allah would accept his repentance.} And in what we narrated in this section is the stoned man's saying to the people: My people killed me and deceived me about myself, and informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not kill me. That indicates that his coming to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his confession to him of what he confessed was not because he would stone him with the stoning in which his killing lies, but for other than that — of the descent of Qur'an concerning him with a meaning perhaps without punishment for him with it. So he was not in that like the Juhani woman, the confessor to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of adultery against herself, and her seeking from him the establishment of the penalty upon her, and her returning to him for that in her pregnancy, after delivering her pregnancy, and after weaning her child — in that is what indicates her knowledge of the penalty; for that is not hidden from the like of her in the like of that period, nor would one who sees her seeking the establishment of the hadd upon her conceal it from her, in what was from her — may Allah forgive her. And in that is what indicates the reason the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) omitted prayer over that stoned man. If one says: In the hadith of Jabir, from the narration of Abu Salama from him: {that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when what was from him reached him, said good about him} — in that is what indicates that he was in his view praised, not blameworthy. It is said to him: In the hadith of Jabir is what you mentioned, and there is narrated from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri concerning what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at that, in his affair, contrary to that."
"486","As narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb; (he said:) Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid — meaning al-Raqqi al-Qattan — narrated to us; (he said:) Mu'awiya ibn Hisham narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Abu Nadra, from Abu Sa'id, who said: {Ma'iz came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and confessed adultery four times. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked about him, then ordered concerning him and he was stoned. We stoned him with pottery shards, rocks, and bones. We did not dig for him, nor did we bind him. He outran us to the Harra, so we followed him. He stood his ground against us, and we pelted him until he fell silent. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) neither sought forgiveness for him nor reviled him.} So in this hadith is contrary to what is in the hadith of Jabir. Then we examined the hadith of Jabir and found in it, from Ibn Burayda, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what disclosed the meaning to us in it."
"487","As narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb; (he said:) Ibrahim ibn Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Juzajani informed me; (he said:) Yahya ibn Ya'la ibn al-Harith narrated to us; (he said:) my father narrated to us; (he said:) Ghaylan ibn Jami' narrated to us, from Alqama ibn Marthad, from Sulayman ibn Burayda, from his father: {that they remained after the stoning of Ma'iz two or three days. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came while they were sitting, greeted, then sat. He said: Seek forgiveness for Ma'iz ibn Malik. They said: May Allah forgive Ma'iz ibn Malik. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He has repented a repentance which, if divided among a nation, would encompass it.} So we learned thereby that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had omitted prayer over him — and of this statement, the duration mentioned in this hadith — and that indicates that praise attached to him from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) after the time of prayer over him had passed, even if another had prayed over him before that. And it is possible that that praise of him was for a reason the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) knew that had occurred in his affair, of Allah's mercy, exalted, overtaking him — either by revelation that came to him, or by a vision he saw concerning him? And we have found something of that in a hadith narrated from Abu Hurayra."
"488","And it is what al-Husayn ibn Nasr narrated to us, who said: I heard Yazid ibn Harun say: Hammad ibn Salama informed us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hadhdhad, from Abu Hurayra: {that Ma'iz ibn Malik committed adultery and came to Hazzal, confessing to him that he committed adultery. Hazzal said to him: Go to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and inform him before Qur'an descends concerning you. So he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, I have committed adultery. He turned away from him until he said that four times. Then he ordered him stoned. He took refuge by a tree and was killed. A man said to his companion: This one has been killed as a dog is killed. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by a bloated donkey and said to them both: Tear off from this. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we cannot — a putrid carcass. He said: What you have taken from your brother is more putrid; he plunges in the rivers of Paradise. Then he said: Woe to you, O Hazzal, did you not cover him? Woe to you, O Hazzal, did you not cover him?}"
"489","And as narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Hatim ibn Nu'aym narrated to us; (he said:) Hibban ibn Musa informed us; (he said:) Abd Allah — meaning Ibn al-Mubarak — informed us, from Hammad ibn Salama, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Hadhdhad, from Abu Hurayra, then he mentioned the like of it, except that he said: {in place of plunges in the rivers of Paradise: he immerses in the rivers of Paradise.} So what we mentioned in the hadith of Burayda indicates that this statement was from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) not immediately after his stoning Ma'iz, but between them was a period in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to know the reality of what he had come to with Allah, exalted — of which he had not been aware before that, nor knowing it, until Allah informed him of it. And what is in the hadith of Jabir — that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said good about him — was after other than prayer over him. As for what is in the hadith of Ibn Hadhdhad which we narrated, of what is related in it of the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the two men as connected with their turning from his stoning — that is impossible; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not attend his stoning, but his stoners came to him and informed him of what was from them and from him. Then there was from him after that this statement, after his becoming aware of the reality of what he had come to with his Lord, exalted, of His pardon of him."
"490","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of {his saying to the one who swore before him to his opponent with whom he had disputed to him over what he had claimed against him: You have indeed done it, so pay him his right, and La ilaha illa Allah will expiate from you what you did.} 440 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us; (he said:) Hibban ibn Hilal narrated to us; (he said:) Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us; (he said:) Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated to us, from Abu Yahya, from Ibn Abbas: {that two men disputed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) asked the claimant for proof, but he had no proof. So he had the defendant swear, and he swore by Allah besides whom there is no god. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have indeed done it, but Allah has forgiven you for your saying: La ilaha illa Allah.}"
"491","And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Samura al-Kufi informed us, from Waki', from Sufyan, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Yahya, from Ibn Abbas, who said: {Two men came disputing to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over something. He said to the claimant: Establish proof. He did not establish it. He said to the other: Swear. So he swore by Allah besides whom there is no god. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Pay him his right, and La ilaha illa Allah will expiate from you what you did.} So in this hadith is that La ilaha illa Allah has by it forgiven the swearer by it his oath upon what was in reality contrary to what he swore to by it. So one said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when you have narrated from him? Then he mentioned:"
"492","What al-Muzani narrated to us; (he said:) al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Jami' and Abd al-Malik, both hearing Abu Wa'il reporting from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Whoever swears an oath to cut off thereby the property of a Muslim man meets Allah, exalted, while He is angry with him. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited to us from Allah's Book: {Those who purchase with Allah's covenant and their oaths a small price}} the verse."
"493","And what Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Yahya ibn Jannad narrated to us; (he said:) Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us; (he said:) Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; (he said:) Humayd ibn Hilal narrated to us, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Mas'ud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: {Whoever swears an oath to cut off thereby the property of a Muslim man meets Allah, mighty and majestic, while He is angry with him.}"
"494","And what Ibn Marzuq narrated to us; (he said:) Umar ibn Yunus al-Yamami narrated to us; (he said:) Ikrima ibn Ammar narrated to us; (he said:) Tariq ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to me, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik — and his father Ka'b is one of the three who stayed behind — say: {Abu Umama narrated to me, while leaning his back against this pillar, a pillar of the mosque of the Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: I, your father Ka'b ibn Malik, and your brother Muhammad ibn Ka'b were sitting at this pillar, mentioning the man who swears over a man's property and cuts it off by his lying oath. At that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Any man who swears over a man's property falsely and cuts it off by his oath has been absolved from Paradise, and the Fire has become obligatory for him. Your brother Muhammad ibn Ka'b said: O Messenger of Allah, even if it is little? He said: He turned a siwak between his fingers and said: Even if it is a siwak of arak — or: even if it is a stick of arak.}"
"495","And what Abu Umayya narrated to us; (he said:) Umar ibn Yunus narrated to us; (he said:) Ikrima narrated to us; (he said:) Tariq narrated to me, he said: I heard Abd Allah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik say: Abu Umama narrated to me, he said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said concerning one who swears over another's property and cuts it off by his oath: The Fire has become obligatory for him, and Paradise has been absolved from him.}"
"496","And what Fahd narrated to us; (he said:) Umar ibn Abd al-Wahhab al-Riyahi narrated to us; (he said:) Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us; (he said:) Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayya, from Umar ibn Ata' ibn Abi al-Khuwar, from Ubayd ibn Jurayh, from al-Harith ibn al-Barsa', who said: {I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying, while walking between two of the jamras: Whoever takes anything of his brother's property by a wicked oath, let him take a house in the Fire.}"
"497","And what Ibn Khuzaimah narrated to us; (he said:) al-Ramadi Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Umayya, from Ibn Abi al-Khuwar, from Ubayd ibn Jurayj, from al-Harith ibn Malik al-Barsa': that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever cuts off the property of a Muslim man by a lying oath meets Allah while He is angry with him.}"
"498","Al-Muzani narrated to us; (he said:) al-Shafi'i narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Ala' ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Ma'bad ibn Ka'b, from his brother Abd Allah, from Abu Umama: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever cuts off a Muslim's right by his oath, Allah forbids Paradise to him and makes the Fire obligatory for him. They said: Even if it is something small, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Even if it is a stick of arak. He said it three times.}"
"499","And what Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Jannad narrated to us; (he said:) Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us; (he said:) Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Ma'bad ibn Ka'b, from his father — or from his uncle, Sufyan doubted — that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever cuts off the property of a Muslim man by a lying oath meets Allah while He is angry with him, and He detests him. They said: O Messenger of Allah, even if it is something small? He said: Even if it is a siwak of arak. Then this objector said: In these reports is of Allah's threat, exalted, against one who swears a lying oath to cut off thereby the property of a Muslim man, what is in them. And the swearer by it has declared Allah's oneness in swearing by it, and negated that there be any god besides Him, yet that threat did not lift, in the view of the one mentioned, that threat in them. And that threat of Allah against him had preceded in His Book by His saying: {Those who purchase with Allah's covenant} the verse. So how is it permissible for you to accept from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what is in the hadith of Ibn Abbas — meaning what we narrated at the head of this chapter? And these hadiths which this one mentioned — each category of that hadith and of these hadiths is contrary to the other category. So our answer to him — by Allah's success — was that the hadith of Ibn Abbas with which we began mention in this chapter does not contradict the hadiths he opposed us with. That is because the first hadith contains only that two men disputed over something, so he summoned the claimant for proof, but he did not bring it, so he had the defendant swear, and he swore. And it is possible that he swore upon what was in his view as he swore upon it; for what had preceded from him in it had left him, and what is in reality was other than what his oath was upon. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed him that there had been from him other than what he swore upon, and that what was in reality of what he swore upon was contrary to what he swore upon. And he ordered him to pay his opponent's right to his opponent, then informed him that it would expiate from him what was from him of swearing, by declaring Allah's oneness, exalted. This objector said: How can what you mentioned be as you described — of the possibility in this hadith of Ibn Abbas of this defendant's swearing upon what he swore upon of what is in reality contrary to that, and of his being forgetful of it? You have narrated in it that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed him that it would expiate from him what was from him of his oath which he swore in that. And expiation is only to expiate thereby, from the one it is expiated for, what was from him of disobedience to Allah, exalted, and departure from His obediences to their opposites — not by other than that. And the swearer upon forgetfulness is outside this meaning, no doubt; for he did not intend an oath upon what is not lawful for him to swear upon. So our answer to him in that was that expiations may become due in things in which there are no sins upon the one from whom they were — of that is Allah's saying in His Book: {And whoever kills a believer by mistake} up to His saying: {repentance from Allah} the verse — and that was not because he was sinful by his killing. And the like of that is what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning one who forgets a prayer or sleeps past it."
"500","As narrated to us by Ali ibn Ma'bad; (he said:) Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Abi Aruba informed us, from Qatada, from Anas: that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever forgets a prayer or sleeps past it, its expiation is to pray it when he remembers it.}"
"501","And as narrated to us by Fahd and Ahmad ibn Dawud, who said: Abu al-Walid narrated to us; (he said:) Hammam narrated to us, from Qatada, from Anas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Whoever forgets a prayer, let him pray it when he remembers it.} And in Ahmad's hadith specifically, Hammam said: Then I heard Qatada narrating it thereafter, saying: {And establish prayer for My remembrance.} And in Fahd's hadith: {There is no expiation for it except that.} So what is in this hadith is that what the forgetter of prayer and the sleeper past it are commanded of is an expiation for them for what we mentioned of them in it — though they were before not sinful. And before that is what is in the verse we recited concerning the mistaken killer, of what is placed upon him in it of expiation, and Allah's informing about it that it is a repentance from Allah — meaning concerning the killer. And in what we mentioned of this is what indicates that expiations may become due along with the lifting of sins. So the like of that is what we narrated from Ibn Abbas at the head of this chapter: that the swearer was not sinful in the swearing that was from him, and that what was from him of declaring Allah's oneness, exalted, and negating that there be any god besides Him, was an expiation for what he swore upon. And how would it be thought of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he would stand before a man upon one of the major sins over which Allah, exalted, has promised the Fire, then not command him to repent to Allah from it, and to act thereafter by what Allah might rescue him thereby from the Fire? And in what we mentioned of this is clear proof that the swearing which was from that swearer was upon what we described — of the departure from him of what he swore upon, that he did not do it, of what he had done — and that the other hadiths mentioned, in which is the threat agreeing with the threat mentioned in Allah's Book, are upon one who swears falsely, intending by his oath to cut off what he swore upon. So it has become clear, by Allah's praise, that each category of these two categories of reports which we mentioned in this chapter from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turns to a meaning other than the meaning to which the other category turns — not contradicting it. And there is narrated from Ibn Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), of what enters this meaning:"
"502","What Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us; (he said:) Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us; (he said:) Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Thabit, from Ibn Umar: {that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to a man: So-and-so, did you do such-and-such? He said: No, by the One besides whom there is no god, I did not do it. Then Gabriel (peace be upon him) came to him and said: He did do it, but Allah has forgiven him by sincerely saying La ilaha illa Allah.} So this is possible to be his swearing upon the matter being in his view as he swore upon it, with its having left him that he had done it — though he had done it in reality — so Allah, exalted, lifted from him the sin in that and did not punish him for it, and made his declaring His oneness and sincerity to Him an expiation for what is in reality prohibited for him. And Allah we ask for success."
"503","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning envy — whether it is open to anyone among people in any state or not. 453 - Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us; (he said:) Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us; (he said:) Shu'ba narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Khumayr, from Sulaym ibn Amir, {from Awsat al-Bajali, that he heard Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, addressing, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us in the first year, then Abu Bakr wept and said: Ask Allah for well-being, for people have not been given, after certainty, anything better than well-being} — and in it: {And hold to truthfulness, for it is with righteousness, and they are in Paradise. And beware of lying, for it is with wickedness, and they are in the Fire. Do not turn backs on one another, nor sever, nor hate one another, nor envy one another, and be servants of Allah as brothers, as Allah, mighty and majestic, commanded you.}"
"504","Yunus narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik informed him, from Ibn Shihab, from Anas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Do not hate one another, nor envy one another, nor turn backs on one another, and be servants of Allah as brothers. And it is not lawful for a Muslim to forsake his brother for more than three nights.}"
"505","Abu Umayya narrated to us; (he said:) Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Jurayj and Zakariyya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Ibn Shihab; (he said:) Anas informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Do not sever, nor turn backs, nor hate one another. And it is not lawful for a Muslim to forsake his brother for more than three nights} — and he did not mention in it: nor envy one another."
"506","And Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us; (he said:) Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us; (he said:) Shu'ba narrated to us, from Qatada, from Anas: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Do not sever, nor hate one another, nor envy one another, and be servants of Allah as brothers.}"
"507","Yunus narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Wahb informed us: that Malik narrated to him, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayra: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Beware of suspicion, for suspicion is the most lying speech. And do not envy one another, nor hate one another, nor compete, and be servants of Allah as brothers.} So in what we narrated is the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from envy, an absolute prohibition — and that agrees with Allah's Book, exalted; Allah, exalted, said: {Or do they envy people for what Allah has given them of His bounty?} So one said: Whence is it open to you, with this, to accept from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what you narrated from him: There is no envy except in two? And he mentioned:"
"508","What Yazid ibn Sinan and Bakkar narrated to us, who said: Abu Amir al-Aqdi narrated to us; (he said:) Sufyan narrated to us, from Isma'il, from Qays, from Ibn Mas'ud, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: {There is no envying except in two: a man to whom Allah gave wisdom, so he judges by it and teaches it; and a man to whom Allah gave wealth, so he wields it to its destruction in what is right.}"
"509","And what Yunus narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Wahb informed us; (he said:) Yunus informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {There is no envy except for two: a man to whom Allah gave this Book, so he stands with it in the hours of the night and the hours of the day; and a man to whom Allah gave wealth, so he gives charity in the hours of the night and the hours of the day.}"
"510","And what Abu Umayya narrated to us; (he said:) Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us. And what Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us; (he said:) Uthman ibn Umar ibn Faris narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — then he mentioned the like of it."
"511","And what Ibrahim narrated to us; (he said:) Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us; (he said:) my father narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Nu'man ibn Rashid narrating from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — then he mentioned the like of it."
"512","And what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Nasr al-Marwazi informed us; (he said:) Ayyub ibn Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Bakr — meaning Ibn Abi Uways — narrated to us, from Sulayman — and he is Ibn Bilal — from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Isma'il ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'd: that Salim ibn Abd Allah and Nafi' narrated to him that Abd Allah ibn Umar said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said — then he mentioned the like of it."
"513","And what Abu Umayya narrated to us; (he said:) Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us; (he said:) Shayban narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {There is no envy except in two: a man to whom Allah gave the Qur'an, so he recites it in the hours of the night and the hours of the day; and a man to whom Allah gave wealth, so he spends it in its right.}"
"514","And what Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Kurayb narrated to us; (he said:) Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us; (he said:) Yazid ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {There is no envy except in two: a man to whom Allah gave the Qur'an, so he recites it in the hours of the night and the hours of the day, and a man says: If Allah had given me the like of what He gave so-and-so, I would have done in it the like of what he did; and a man to whom Allah gave wealth, so he spends it in its right, and a man says: If Allah had given me the like of what He gave so-and-so, I would have done in it the like of what he did.} So our answer to him was: Envy divides into two kinds. One of them is envying one given something for what he was given of it, with the envier's wish that that thing be his to the exclusion of the one Allah gave it to — that is what is blameworthy from whomever it is. And one of them is envying one Allah gave something, with the envier's wish to be given the like of that thing — not that that very thing be transferred from the envied one until he is bereft of it and it belongs to the envier to his exclusion. And Allah has clarified these two meanings in His Book, saying: {And do not wish for what Allah has favored some of you with over others} up to His saying: {And ask Allah of His bounty} — i.e., until He gives you the like of it, while the one you envied remains with what Allah gave him, undiminished. So the envy in which is the wish to transfer the envied thing from the one Allah gave it to, to his envier, is blameworthy. And the envy in which there is not that wish, but in it is the envier's envying the envied one for what Allah gave him, until Allah gives him of His bounty the like of it, is not blameworthy. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clarified that in the hadith of Abu Kabsha al-Anmari which we narrated earlier in this book of ours — which he related from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: {The likeness of this world is the likeness of four: a man to whom Allah gave knowledge and gave wealth, so he acts in his wealth by his knowledge; and a man to whom Allah gave knowledge but did not give wealth, so he says: If I had of wealth the like of what so-and-so has, I would have done in it what he does — i.e., in his wealth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They are equal in reward.} And it is also established in the hadith of Yazid ibn Abd al-Aziz, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Sa'id, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) which we narrated in this chapter. So it has become clear, by Allah's praise and blessing, that there is no contradiction in anything we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and that each of the two envies — of what we mentioned in this chapter, of which he censured one and did not censure the other — are distinct: in one is what people ought to be upon, and in the other what people ought not to be upon. And with Allah is success."
"515","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the Ruwaybida whom he mentioned in describing the years before the Dajjal — who he is among people? 464 - Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Kurayb narrated to us; (he said:) Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Ibrahim ibn Abi Abla, from his father, from Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Before the Dajjal are deceptive years, in which rain is abundant and vegetation is scant; in them the liar is believed and the truthful is disbelieved; in them the treacherous is trusted and the trustworthy is deemed treacherous; and in them the Ruwaybida speaks. It was said: What is the Ruwaybida, O Messenger of Allah? He said: One to whom no attention is paid.}"
"516","And with it, from Ibn Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Anas, the like of it, except that he said: {It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what is the Ruwaybida? He said: The despicable sinner who speaks in public affairs.}"
"517","Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us; (he said:) Amr ibn Muhammad al-Naqid narrated to us; (he said:) Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Before the Hour are treacherous years, in which the liar is believed and the truthful is disbelieved; in them the treacherous is trusted and the trustworthy is deemed treacherous; and in them the Ruwaybida speaks. It was said: What is the Ruwaybida? He said: The despicable sinner who speaks in public affairs.} So there was not, in what we narrated of these reports, of the mention of the Ruwaybida, anything entailing disagreement over who he is among people upon the tongue of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); for it is possible that his describing him with the sinfulness that prevents the like of him from speaking in public affairs — that speech in public affairs is let loose for him in the blameworthy age, just as in it is the believing of the liar, the disbelieving of the truthful, and the trusting of the treacherous — and his describing him as one to whom no attention is paid is due to his open sinfulness, and because he is of those people have no need of — so he is thereby obscure, paid no attention. Thus, by Allah's praise, the two meanings we narrated in the interpretation of the Ruwaybida from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter agree and do not differ. And Allah we ask for success."
"518","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the interpretation of Allah's saying, exalted: {Then you will surely be asked that Day about bliss.} 467 - Ahmad ibn Dawud narrated to us; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Abi Samina narrated to us; (he said:) Sufyan informed us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Zubayr, who said: {When: {Then you will surely be asked that Day about bliss} was revealed, we said: O Messenger of Allah, what bliss — when they are only the two black ones? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It will come to be.} So we examined this hadith and found in it the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), upon the descent of this verse upon him: What bliss — i.e., what they are in — when they are only the two black ones? And the answer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to them at that: It will come to be — i.e., there will come to be for you a living beyond the two black ones, about which you will be asked. Thereby we understood that what they will be asked about is the surplus beyond the two black ones, of what exceeds what their selves subsist on; and that they are not asked about what their selves subsist on only. And we found what indicates that narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in other than this hadith."
"519","As narrated to us by Ibn Marzuq and Ibn Abi Dawud, who said: Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us; (he said:) Hashraj ibn Nubata narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Nusayra narrated to us, from Abu Asib, who said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out at night, passed by Abu Bakr and called him, so he came out to him. Then he passed by Umar and called him, so he came out to him. Then he walked on, we with him, until he entered one of the Ansar's gardens and said: Feed us unripe dates. They brought them a cluster, so they ate from it, and brought them water, so they drank. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is of the bliss you will be asked about. Umar said: Will we be asked about this on the Day of Resurrection? He said: Yes — except for three: a morsel with which a man staves off his hunger, a rag with which he covers his nakedness, and a stone he enters for heat and cold.}"
"520","And as narrated to us by Abu Umayya; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us; (he said:) Hashraj ibn Nubata narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it with his chain. And he added: {Umar took the cluster and struck the ground with it until the unripe dates scattered. Then he said: O Messenger of Allah, will we be asked about this?} So in this hadith was the clarification of what we mentioned; for in it is that they are asked about the unripe dates they ate and the water they drank — for they are surplus beyond the morsel with which they stave off hunger, the rag with which they cover their nakedness, and the stone that shields them from heat and cold."
"521","As narrated to us by Ahmad ibn Dawud; (he said:) Ubayd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Taymi narrated to us; (he said:) Hammad ibn Salama informed us, from Ammar ibn Abi Ammar, from Jabir, who said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, so we fed him fresh dates and gave him water to drink. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is of the bliss you will be asked about.}"
"522","As narrated to us by Fahd; (he said:) Abu al-Walid narrated to us; (he said:) Hammad narrated to us, from Ammar: I heard Jabir say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to our house — then he mentioned the like of it, except that Abu al-Walid doubted, saying: We fed him fresh dates or unripe dates."
"523","And as narrated to us by Abu Umayya; (he said:) Ubayd Allah narrated to us; (he said:) Shayban narrated to us. And Abu Umayya narrated to us; (he said:) al-Hasan al-Ashyab narrated to us; (he said:) Shayban narrated to us — both from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu Salama, from Abu Hurayra, who said: {The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out at an hour in which he does not go out and no one meets him in it. Abu Bakr came to him, so he said: What brought you out, O Abu Bakr? He said: I went out to meet the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), to look upon his face and greet him. It was not long before Umar came. He said: What brought you out, O Umar? He said: Hunger. He said: I too feel some of what you feel. Set out with us to Abu al-Haytham ibn al-Tayyihan.} And he mentioned the hadith at length, saying in it: {For the one consulted is trusted.} Thus Abu Umayya narrated it to us, and he is for its transmission."
"524","And as narrated to us by Yusuf ibn Yazid; (he said:) Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us; (he said:) Hushaym narrated to us; (he said:) Umar ibn Abi Salama narrated to us, from his father: {that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one day and sat. Then Abu Bakr came and sat beside the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: What brought you out at this hour? He said: Hunger. He said: O Abu Bakr, nothing brought me out but hunger. Then Umar came and he said the like of that. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Set out with us to the house of Abu al-Haytham. They did not find him, and his wife gave them permission. They had not waited but a little before Abu al-Haytham came. He cut for them a cluster from a palm and placed it before them. They ate of fresh and unripe dates, then drank of the water. And he ordered a sheep slaughtered for them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not slaughter one with milk. So he slaughtered for them. Then meat was brought and they ate of the fresh dates and meat until they were full. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will surely be asked about this — and this is of the bliss you will be asked about. When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left, he said to Abu al-Haytham: When captives come to us, come to us so we may order a servant for you. He waited as Allah willed. Then captives were brought, and Abu al-Haytham came to him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Choose whichever of them you will. He said: O Messenger of Allah, choose for me. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one consulted is trusted — two or three times. He said: Take this one and treat him well, for I saw him praying, and I am forbidden from those who pray. So Abu al-Haytham took him. When he came to his family, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined me to treat you well — so you are free for the sake of Allah, exalted.}"
"525","And as narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Sinan; (he said:) Isa ibn Sulayman narrated to us; (he said:) Khalaf ibn Khalifa narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Kaysan, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayra: {that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one day and there were Abu Bakr and Umar. He said: What brought you out at this hour? They said: Hunger, O Messenger of Allah. He said: By the One who sent me with the truth, nothing brought me out but what brought you out. So stand. They stood with him. He came to a man of the Ansar, but the man was not there — only his wife. When she saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with Abu Bakr and Umar, she said: Welcome. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Where is so-and-so? She said: He went to fetch us sweet water. While they were thus, the Ansari came, bearing a waterskin of water. When he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his two companions, he exclaimed takbir, then said: Allah is greatest — no one among people, male or female, has more noble guests than I today. He hung the skin on a stump, then went and brought a cluster with dates, fresh dates, and unripe dates, placing it before them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Why did you not pick select ones? He said: Choose before your eyes, O Messenger of Allah. Then he took the knife. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Beware the milch one. So he slaughtered for them a sheep, and they ate. When they were full, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By the One in whose hand is my soul, you will surely be asked about this bliss on the Day of Resurrection: hunger drove you from your houses, then you did not return until you attained this bliss.} So these reports we narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this chapter have agreed, by Allah's praise and blessing, their meanings harmonized, and disagreement and contradiction removed from them. And Allah we ask for success."
"526","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in answer to one who asked him about the Hour. 475 - Yunus narrated to us; (he said:) Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Anas: {that a man asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the Hour. He said: What have you prepared for it? He said: Love of Allah and His Messenger. He said: You are with whom you loved.}"
"527","Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Yunus al-Tha'labi al-Susi narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Mu'awiya narrated to us, from Hisham, from his father, {from A'isha, who said: The Bedouins used to come asking the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): When is the Hour? When is the Hour? He looked at one of them and said: If this one remains and old age does not kill him, his hour will come upon him.} And in this chapter are many reports of which we sufficed with these two. For the reports narrated in it besides them are mixed with other than this meaning, so we deferred them to place each hadith of them in a place more fitting for it than this place, if Allah wills. And what was from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of answer when asked about what he was asked about in these two is the answer Allah commanded him with, when asked about what he was asked about, of what we mentioned in them — His saying, exalted: {They ask you about the Hour, when is its anchorage} up to His saying: {Say: Its knowledge is only with my Lord; none will reveal it for its time but He} up to His saying: {except suddenly} — and by His saying: {They ask you about the Hour, when is its anchorage? What are you of its mention? To your Lord is its end} — i.e., when they asked him about that, they asked him about what Allah has hidden from him its reality. So his answer to them about that — the answer mentioned from him in these two reports — ended at what Allah, exalted, commanded him to end at in that meaning."
"528","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: {The distinction between our fasting and the fasting of the People of the Book is the predawn meal.} 477 - Yunus and al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us, who said: Ibn Wahb informed us; (he said:) Musa ibn Ulayy informed me, from his father, from Abu Qays, freedman of Amr ibn al-As, from Amr: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The distinction between our fasting and the fasting of the People of the Book is the predawn meal.} So we examined this to determine the meaning intended by it. We found that of the law of the People of the Book is that when they sleep in their night, there becomes forbidden to them thereby, for its remainder, what is forbidden to the fasting person — of approaching women, eating, and drinking — until their exit from the fast of the morrow of that night. And likewise were the people of Islam at the beginning of Islam, until Allah abrogated that with what He abrogated of His Book. And there is narrated concerning that:"
"529","What Bakkar narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us; (he said:) al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Amr ibn Murra, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, {from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, who said: Prayer passed through three states, and fasting through three states. He mentioned the three states of prayer, then said: As for the states of fasting: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madina and fasted three days of each month, and fasted the day of Ashura. He fasted them thus sixteen or seventeen months. Then Allah revealed: {Prescribed upon you is fasting as it was prescribed upon those before you} up to His saying: {And whoever volunteers good, it is better for him.} Whoever willed fasted, and whoever willed fed a poor person, and that sufficed him — until Allah, exalted, revealed: {The month of Ramadan in which the Qur'an was revealed.} Up to His saying: {So whoever witnesses the month, let him fast it.} And up to His saying: {Allah intends ease for you and does not intend hardship for you.} So Allah made it obligatory and established its fasting upon the healthy resident, and permitted it for the sick and traveler. And feeding remained for the old man who cannot fast it. They would eat, drink, and approach women; but when they slept, they abstained from that. A man called Sirma came, having toiled his day working. He came, prayed Isha, laid his head and slept before eating, and woke fasting. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him at the end of the day, exhausted, and said: I see you exhausted. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I toiled my day working, came to Isha prayer, and slept before eating. And Umar came, having had intercourse with women. Then this verse was revealed: {Permitted to you on the night of fasting is intercourse with your wives} up to His saying: {of the dawn.}}"
"530","And what Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud narrated to us; (he said:) Sa'id ibn Ya'qub al-Talqani narrated to us; (he said:) Hushaym narrated to us; (he said:) Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman informed us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, {from a man of the Ansar called Sirma ibn Malik — an old man — who came to his family at night while fasting. When one of them slept before eating, he would eat nothing until the like of it; and when a woman slept, her husband would not approach her until the like of it came. When Sirma came to his family and called for his dinner, they said: Wait until we make you hot food to break fast on. The old man laid his head and slept. They brought his food, and he said: I was asleep — so he did not taste it, spending his night with back cleaving to belly. In the morning he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him. Then this verse was revealed: {And eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread of dawn.} So they were permitted to eat from the beginning of the night to its end. And Umar came to his wife, and she said she had slept. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, thought she was making an excuse to him, so he had intercourse with her. He was informed she had slept. He mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then there was revealed concerning him: {Allah knew that you were betraying yourselves, so He turned to you and pardoned you} the verse.} So we learned thereby that the meaning of what we narrated in the hadith of Amr ibn al-As is that in our fast it is permitted for us to eat in its nights, even if we slept in them — unlike the fast of the People of the Book, who, when they sleep in the nights of their fast, do not eat therein until the morrow of that night passes."
"531","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what Ibn Abbas narrated concerning sighting the Ramadan crescent. 480 - Yusuf ibn Yazid narrated to us; (he said:) Hajjaj ibn Ibrahim al-Azraq narrated to us; (he said:) Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abi Harmala, who said: {Kurayb informed me: that Umm al-Fadl bint al-Harith sent him to Mu'awiya in Syria. He said: I came to Syria and fulfilled her need. The month of Ramadan came upon me while I was in Syria, and we saw the crescent on Friday night. Then I came to Madina at the end of the month. Ibn Abbas asked me about things, then mentioned the crescent. He said: When did you see the crescent? I said: We saw it on Friday night. He said: Did you see it? I said: Yes — and the people saw it, so they fasted, and Mu'awiya fasted. He said: But we saw it on Saturday night, so we shall fast until we complete thirty or see it. I said: Will you not suffice with Mu'awiya's sighting and his fasting? He said: No — thus the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us.}"
"532","And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; (he said:) Ali ibn Hujr informed us; (he said:) Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us with his chain the like of it, except that he said: I said: Will you not suffice with the sighting of Mu'awiya and his companions, in place of: and his fasting. So in this hadith from Ibn Abbas is that he did not suffice with the sighting of the people of a land other than his land in which he was, and his informing that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded them with that. Then one asked: Does this contradict what is narrated from Ibn Abbas besides it in this meaning? And he mentioned what:"
"533","Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; (he said:) Musa ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Masruqi informed us; (he said:) Husayn — meaning al-Ju'fi — narrated to us, from Za'ida, from Simak, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, who said: {A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I have sighted the crescent. He said: Do you testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger? He said: Yes. He said: O Bilal, call among the people that they fast tomorrow.}"
"534","And what Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn Yunus narrated to us; (he said:) Harun ibn Abd Allah — meaning al-Hammal — narrated to us; (he said:) Husayn ibn Ali narrated to us, from Za'ida, from Simak, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, who said: {A Bedouin testified before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to sighting the crescent, so he ordered Bilal to call among the people that they fast tomorrow.}"
"535","And what Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; (he said:) Muhammad ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rizma informed us; (he said:) al-Fadl ibn Musa — and he is al-Sinani — narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Simak, from Ikrima, from Ibn Abbas, who said: {A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: I saw the crescent. He said: Do you testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and Messenger? He said: Yes. He said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called out: Fast.}"
"536","And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us; (he said:) Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us, from Shu'ba, from Sufyan, from Simak, from Ikrima: {that a Bedouin testified before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he saw the crescent. He said: Do you testify that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah? He said: Yes. So he accepted his testimony} — and he did not mention Ibn Abbas. So our answer in that was that each of these two hadiths does not contradict the other; and that the hadith of Ikrima is upon employing the testimony of one Muslim for sighting the Ramadan crescent. And the hadith of Kurayb contains his informing Ibn Abbas of sighting the Ramadan crescent at a time when employing fasting by that sighting had passed — and there is not in it from Ibn Abbas that had that reached him while able to employ that report in fasting he would employ it. When that passed him, he returned to awaiting what would be at the end of the month of the crescent, indicating when its beginning was. It was possible that thirty days would pass upon what Kurayb had related to him, so he would thereby know the falsity of what Kurayb related to him, fasting thirty days upon his own sighting. And it was possible that he would see it after twenty-nine days upon what Kurayb related, so he would make up a day — employing what is in the hadith of Ikrima. And this meaning upon which we validated these two hadiths agrees with what Abu Hanifa and his companions held: accepting the testimony of one for the Ramadan crescent, and not accepting for the Fitr crescent but what they accept in other rights of the proofs they accept therein. They say: If people fast by one person's testimony of sighting the Ramadan crescent, and thirty days pass without seeing the crescent, they fast another day. And that is contrary to the ruling in that had an accepted testimony before the imam — by which he may judge in other than that — testified to sighting the crescent, so he ordered them to fast, and they fasted thirty days without seeing the crescent: he orders them to break fast and exit fasting. They make fasting by one person's testimony precautionary fasting, and fasting by the accepted, judged testimony in other things decisive fasting — and people's ruling is as though they all saw it. So it has become clear by what we mentioned that there is no contradiction in anything we described in this chapter from Ibn Abbas, from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"537","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the amount of the state by which begging becomes forbidden. 486 - al-Rabi' al-Muradi narrated to us; (he said:) Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir; (he said:) Rabi'a ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Abu Kabsha al-Saluli; (he said:) Sahl ibn al-Hanzaliyya narrated to me, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {Whoever begs people while having self-sufficiency only increases coals of Hell. I said: O Messenger of Allah, what is self-sufficiency? He said: That he knows that with his family is what gives them lunch or what gives them dinner.}"
"538","Yunus narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik narrated to him, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Ata ibn Yasar, from a man of Banu Asad, who said: {I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard him saying to a man asking him: Whoever of you begs while having an uqiyya or its equivalent has begged importunately. And the uqiyya then was forty dirhams.}"
"539","And Ibn Marzuq narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Asim al-Nabil narrated to us. And al-Hasan ibn Nasr narrated to us; (he said:) al-Firyabi narrated to us — both saying: al-Thawri narrated to us, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from his father, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {No servant begs a begging while having what suffices him, but it comes as a disgrace, or scratch-marks, or scratches on his face on the Day of Resurrection. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what is his sufficiency? He said: Fifty dirhams, or its reckoning in gold.}"
"540","And Ahmad ibn Khalid ibn Yazid al-Baghdadi narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Hisham al-Rifa'i narrated to us; (he said:) Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us; (he said:) al-Thawri narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it with his chain, except that he said: {scratch-marks on his face} — without doubt — and added: It was said to Sufyan: If only it were from other than Hakim. He said: Zubayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid."
"541","And Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Bakr al-Hanafi narrated to us; (he said:) Abd al-Hamid ibn Ja'far narrated to us; (he said:) my father narrated to me, {from a man of Muzayna, that he came to his mother, who said: O my son, if you went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and asked him. He said: So I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was standing addressing the people, saying: Whoever seeks sufficiency, Allah suffices him; and whoever seeks chastity, Allah makes him chaste; and whoever begs people while having the equivalent of five uqiyyas begs importunately.} So we examined these amounts narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning the forbidding of begging by their existence — whether we can validate them so none of them is contrary to the others. We found it possible that the first of these amounts by which begging was forbidden is the amount in the hadith of Ibn al-Hanzaliyya; then followed it its forbidding by the existence of what is in the hadith of the Asadi; then followed it its forbidding by the existence of what is in the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud; then followed it its forbidding by the existence of what is in the hadith of the Muzani. So the amount in the hadith of the Muzani is the amount at whose existence the forbidding of begging terminates — so the worthiest of these amounts we narrated for employment in this chapter. If one says: How did you employ in this the severest amount first, then employ after it what is lighter than it, when you employed them all thus — and not employ the lightest first, then after it what is severer, until you come upon them all? Our answer to him was: The abrogation of things is by one of two meanings. One is for punishment — abrogating the light with the severe — and it is Allah's saying, exalted: {For wrongdoing among those who Judaized} the verse. And one is contrary to punishment — abrogating the severe with the light — and that is mercy from Allah and lightening upon His servants. Of it is His saying, exalted: {O Prophet, urge the believers to fight} up to His saying: {of those who disbelieved.} Allah's imposition upon them in this verse was not to flee from ten times their number — and it was understood in that that they may flee from what is more than that — then Allah abrogated it, mercy from Him to them and lightening for their weakness, saying: {Now Allah has lightened for you} the verse. So Allah returned His imposition upon them to not flee from twice their number — and it was understood in that that they may flee from more than twice their number. Of it is His saying, exalted: {O enwrapped one, stand the night except a little} up to His saying: {measured recitation.} That was imposed upon him and upon his umma in night standing; then Allah abrogated that, mercy from Him to him and to them, by His saying: {Your Lord knows that you stand less than two-thirds of the night} up to His saying: {So recite what is easy of it.} So abrogation in what we mentioned, and its likes, in what has no displeasure or anger in it, is from severe to light. And the Muslims for whom the amounts we mentioned — each amount of which forbade begging to them — were not guilty of a sin deserving punishment, to be returned from light to severe. So it became incumbent thereby, in the abrogation we mentioned, that their being returned from part of it to another is a return from severe to light. So thereby became incumbent the employment of what we mentioned in it in this chapter. Thus we learned that the amount by which begging is forbidden is the amount in the hadith of the Muzani, to the exclusion of the other amounts mentioned in other than it in this chapter. And Allah we ask for success."
"542","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated {from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying to Qabisa ibn al-Mukhariq al-Hilali: Begging was forbidden except in three} — then he mentioned them, then followed that with his saying: {And what is besides that of begging is unlawful gain.} 491 - Yunus narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinana ibn Nu'aym, {from Qabisa ibn al-Mukhariq, that he undertook a suretyship and came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: We shall pay it for you from the camels of charity — or the livestock of charity. O Qabisa, begging was forbidden except in three: a man who undertakes a suretyship — begging is lawful for him until he pays it, then he desists; and a man struck by a calamity that sweeps away his wealth — begging is lawful for him until he attains sufficiency of livelihood or support of livelihood, then he desists; and a man struck by a calamity until three discerning men of his people testify that begging has become lawful for him — until he attains sufficiency of livelihood or support of livelihood, then he desists.}"
"543","Bakkar narrated to us; (he said:) al-Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us; (he said:) Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Harun, from Kinana, from Qabisa, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. And he added: {a man who bears a suretyship for his people intending reconciliation.}"
"544","Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us; (he said:) Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us; (he said:) Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinana — meaning al-Adawi — from Qabisa ibn al-Mukhariq al-Hilali, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he mentioned the like of it, except that he did not mention the addition Bakkar added in his hadith."
"545","And Yunus narrated to us; (he said:) Bashshar ibn Bakr narrated to us, he said: al-Awza'i said: And Harun ibn Ri'ab narrated to me; (he said:) Abu Bakr narrated to me — Abu Ja'far said: and he is Kinana ibn Nu'aym — he said: I was sitting with Qabisa — then he mentioned from him that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say ... then he mentioned the like of it."
"546","And Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; (he said:) Ali ibn Hujr narrated to us; (he said:) Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Harun ibn Ri'ab, from Kinana ibn Nu'aym, from Qabisa ibn al-Mukhariq, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — then he mentioned the like of it. So we examined this hadith and found the three things at which the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the begging forbidden before that. Of them is the suretyship by which the surety intends reconciliation — so he begs at that until he pays it. And in that is proof of the binding of the suretyship upon one who undertook it, its obligatoriness upon him as a debt, and the obligatoriness of taking him by it — even if the one undertaken for is suable — as those among the people of knowledge who say it say, among them Abu Hanifa, Abu Yusuf, Muhammad, and al-Shafi'i. And Malik had said it in what Ibn al-Qasim related from him, then retracted to saying: The undertaken-for need not demand the surety for what he bore until unable to demand the undertaken-for. And of them is begging at the need at which three discerning men of the beggar's people speak: that begging has become lawful for him — so he begs at that until he meets his need. So one said: How did he intend in this three of his people to the exclusion of two, when Allah made two a proof in testimony, in judgment of game recompense, and in judgment between the spouses in discord? So our answer to him in that — by Allah's success, mighty and majestic, and His aid — was that creatures are Allah's servants; He binds them by what He wills. So He bound them by making two a proof in what He made them so in it; then He made the proof in other than that — and it is adultery — more than their number. And the like of that was in the begging at which He permitted begging: He bound them in it, upon the tongue of His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), by three — differing between that and what besides it in which He made two a proof. And the need we mentioned was less than the need mentioned with it in this hadith. So need was of what people's states differ at — the one stricken being unlike the one struck by the sweeping calamity with which nothing remains to him. So he needed to meet his need, but He did not grant him that by his saying: begging has become lawful for him, until returned to the statements of the stated number in this hadith. And people's needs differed by the difference of their provisions in its little and its much. So that was referred to the amount of the need in itself. And asking was free for its people thereby until Allah meets it by what He wills to meet it by of amounts of things. He did not mention, for that, the amount that prevents begging specifically. And that was not contrary to the amounts we mentioned in this chapter before this chapter. And what is in that was for the need after which there is no need; and what is in this hadith was for the need that may be, with remaining with the one seeking begging for it something of his wealth with which he cannot meet his need — so begging was permitted to him until he meets it. And people's amounts differed in that in their needs, so He did not mention the amount of the remainder with the one permitted begging with it for that. And with Allah is success."
"547","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: {Two festival months do not decrease: Ramadan and Dhu al-Hijja.} 496 - Ibn Marzuq narrated to us; (he said:) Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us; (he said:) Shu'ba narrated to us, from Khalid al-Hadhdha', from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakra, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Two festival months do not decrease: Ramadan and Dhu al-Hijja.}"
"548","Ibn Marzuq and Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us together, both saying: Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us; (he said:) Hammad — and he is Ibn Salama — informed us, from Salim ibn Ubayd Allah ibn Salim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakra, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — then he mentioned the like of it. So we examined this hadith to determine the meaning intended by what is in it: whether it is upon decrease of number, as one who said that said; or whether it is upon the existence of decrease of number in one of them and its absence from the other, so they are not both deficient together; or contrary to these two mentioned meanings. We found what we have known in times: that decrease of the two numbers occurs in one to the exclusion of the other, and may occur in both together — no dispute in that — and what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of what he ordered employed for Ramadan and its beginning and end has confirmed it."
"549","As narrated to us by Ali ibn Ma'bad and Ibn Marzuq, who said: Rawh ibn Ubada narrated to us; (he said:) Zakariyya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar: that Muhammad ibn Jubayr informed him that he heard {Ibn Abbas saying: I marvel at those who fast before Ramadan. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only said: When you see the crescent, fast; and when you see it, break fast. If it is obscured to you, count thirty.}"
"550","And as narrated to us by Bakkar; (he said:) Ibrahim ibn Bashshar narrated to us; (he said:) Sufyan narrated to us; (he said:) Amr ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Muhammad, from Ibn Abbas, who said: I heard him say ... then he mentioned the like of it."
"551","And as narrated to us by Ibn Khuzaimah; (he said:) Ali ibn al-Ja'd narrated to us; (he said:) Shu'ba informed us, from Muhammad ibn Ziyad, who said: I heard Abu Hurayra say: Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Fast upon its sighting, and break fast upon its sighting. If it is obscured to you, count thirty.}"
"552","And as narrated to us by Fahd; (he said:) al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us; (he said:) Ibrahim ibn Humayd al-Ru'asi narrated to us, from Mujalid ibn Sa'id, from al-Sha'bi, from Adi ibn Hatim, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: {When Ramadan comes, fast thirty, unless you see the crescent before that. So we thereby understood that the month of Ramadan may be thirty and may be twenty-nine} — so we needed the meaning of his saying: {Two festival months do not decrease} — what it is? We found these two months — Ramadan and Dhu al-Hijja — built upon other than them of the months; for in one of them is fasting, and in no other of the months. And in one of them is Hajj, and in no other of the months. So it was imagined that it would fall in the hearts of people that when they are twenty-nine, twenty-nine, the fast in one of them and the Hajj in the other decrease thereby from what they are upon when they are thirty, thirty. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed them that they do not decrease — even if they are twenty-nine, twenty-nine — from what is in them of these two worships; and that these two worships are complete in them, even if they are in number thus, like their completeness in them when they are thirty, thirty. And Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq al-Basri narrated this hadith, from Abu Bakra, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), contrary to this meaning."
"553","As narrated to us by Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud; (he said:) Farwa ibn Abi al-Maghra' narrated to us; (he said:) al-Qasim ibn Malik al-Muzani narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakra, from his father: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Every sacred month is thirty days and thirty nights.} So this, in our view, is nothing — since Abd al-Rahman ibn Ishaq does not rival Khalid al-Hadhdha' in his leadership in narration, nor in his precision in it, nor in his mastery of it — and since plain sight has refuted that. And with Allah is success."
"554","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: {Whoever does well in Islam is not taken for what he did in Jahiliyya; and whoever does ill in Islam is taken for the first and the last.} 503 - Yazid ibn Sinan and Bakkar narrated to us, who said: Abu Amir al-Aqdi narrated to us; (he said:) Sufyan narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Abd Allah, who said: {A man said: O Messenger of Allah, is one of us taken for what he did in Jahiliyya? He said: Whoever does well in Islam is not taken for what he did in Jahiliyya; and whoever does ill in Islam is taken for the first and the last.}"
"555","Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Asim narrated to us; (he said:) al-Thawri narrated to us, from Mansur and al-A'mash, from Abu Wa'il, from Abd Allah, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it."
"556","Bakkar narrated to us; (he said:) Mu'ammal ibn Isma'il narrated to us; (he said:) Sufyan narrated to us; (he said:) al-A'mash and Mansur narrated to us — then he mentioned the like of it with his chain."
"557","Al-Hasan ibn Abd Allah ibn Mansur al-Balisi Abu Ali narrated to us; (he said:) al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us; (he said:) Za'ida ibn Qudama and Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: The people said: O Messenger of Allah — then he mentioned the like of it exactly. Then one asked: Does this hadith cohere with the hadith you narrated from Amr ibn al-As from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Then he mentioned:"
"558","What Fahd narrated to us, who said: Yusuf ibn Buhlul narrated to us; (he said:) Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Ishaq narrated to us; (he said:) Yazid ibn Abi Habib narrated to me, from Rashid, freedman of Habib ibn Abi Aws, from Habib ibn Abi Aws, who said: Amr ibn al-As narrated to me his hadith from his mouth, mentioning the story of his Islam. He said: {I said: O Messenger of Allah, I pledge you upon being forgiven what preceded, and I do not mention what I begin anew? He said: O Amr, pledge — for Islam cuts off what was before it, and Hijra cuts off what was before it.} So our answer to him about that, by Allah's success, was that these two hadiths cohere, neither differing nor contradicting. That is because the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the hadith of Ibn Mas'ud, in our view — and Allah knows best — whoever does well in Islam is upon the meaning of whoever enters Islam in Islam. Of that is His saying, exalted: {Whoever comes with the good deed, for him is better than it} — so the intended good deed in that is Islam. So whoever comes with Islam has cut off from him what was from him in Jahiliyya — agreeing with what is in the hadith of Amr that {Islam cuts off what was before it.} And whoever adheres to disbelief in Islam has come with the evil deed in Islam. Of it is Allah's saying, exalted: {And whoever comes with the evil deed is recompensed only the like of it} — so the punishment of that evil deed upon him is added to the punishments of what preceded it of his evil deeds in Jahiliyya. Thus, by Allah's praise, the two hadiths of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) we mentioned agree and do not differ."
"559","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of his saying: {When Kisra perishes, there is no Kisra after him; and when Caesar perishes, there is no Caesar after him.} 508 - Yunus narrated to us; (he said:) Anas ibn Iyad informed us, from al-Harith ibn Abi Dhubab, from his uncle, from Abu Hurayra: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {When Kisra perishes, there is no Kisra after him; and when Caesar perishes, there is no Caesar after him. By the One in whose hand is my soul, their treasures will surely be spent in Allah's cause.}"
"560","Ahmad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; (he said:) Qutayba informed us; (he said:) Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {Kisra perishes and there is no Kisra after him; and when Caesar perishes, there is no Caesar after him. By the One in whose hand is my soul, their treasures will surely be spent in Allah's cause.}"
"561","Ibn Marzuq narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us, from Shu'ba, from Ya'la ibn Ata', who said: I heard Abu Alqama narrating from Abu Hurayra, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {When Kisra perishes, there is no Kisra after him; and when Caesar perishes, there is no Caesar after him.}"
"562","Yunus narrated to us; (he said:) Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us; (he said:) Ubayd Allah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samura, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {When Kisra goes, there is no Kisra after him; and when Caesar goes, there is no Caesar after him. By the One in whose hand is my soul, their treasures will surely be spent in Allah's cause.}"
"563","Ibn Khuzaimah narrated to us; (he said:) Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Awana narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samura, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the like of it. So we examined this hadith to determine the intended meaning. We found al-Muzani related to us from al-Shafi'i, in its interpretation, saying: Quraysh used to frequent Syria very frequently — much of their livelihood was from it — and would come to Iraq. When they entered Islam, they mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), fearing the severance of their livelihood by trade from Syria and Iraq, having parted from the disbelievers and entered Islam with the opposition of the kings of Syria and Iraq to the people of Islam. So he said: {When Kisra perishes, there is no Kisra after him} — so there was in the land of Iraq no Kisra established in authority after him. And he said: {When Caesar perishes, there is no Caesar after him} — so there was in the land of Syria no Caesar after him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered them upon what they said, and it was as it was until today: Allah cut the Kisras from Iraq and Persia, and Caesar and whoever stood after him in Syria. And he said concerning Caesar: his kingdom is established in the lands of Rome, and his kingdom is removed from Syria. And all this agrees, each confirming the other. Abu Ja'far said: And I asked Ahmad ibn Abi Imran about the interpretation of this hadith, and he answered me contrary to this saying. He mentioned that the meaning of his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {When Kisra perishes, there is no Kisra after him} — he said: Kisra perished as he informed us he would perish, so there was no Kisra after him, nor will there be a Kisra after him until the Day of Resurrection. And the meaning of his saying: {When Caesar perishes, there is no Caesar after him} is informing them that he will perish — but he has not perished until now; rather he will perish before the Day of Resurrection. And Kisra and he were differed between in hastening Kisra's perishing and delaying Caesar's perishing, due to the difference of what was from them upon the arrival of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) letter to each of them."
"564","Ibn Abi Imran said to us: And there is narrated concerning that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): 513 - What Ibrahim ibn Hamza al-Zubayri narrated to us; (he said:) Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah informed me: that Ibn Abbas informed him: {that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Caesar calling him to Islam, sending his letter — meaning with Dihya ibn Khalifa al-Kalbi — ordering him to hand it to the chief of Busra, to hand it to Caesar. The chief of Busra handed it to Caesar. When the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) letter came to him and he read it, he said: Seek for me here someone of his people whom I may question about him.} Ibn Abbas said: Abu Sufyan informed me that they were admitted to him; and when he read the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) letter and questioned Abu Sufyan about what he questioned him, and Abu Sufyan answered him with what he answered in that, he said: If what you say is true, he is about to possess the place of these two feet of mine. By Allah, if I hoped to reach him, I would undertake meeting him; and if I were with him, I would wash his feet.}"
"565","And Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us; (he said:) Abd al-Aziz al-Uwaysi narrated to us; (he said:) Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, then he mentioned this hadith with its chain, as Ibn Abi Imran narrated it to us, from Ibrahim ibn Hamza, from Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, exactly. So this was what was from Caesar upon the arrival of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) letter calling him to Islam. And what was from Kisra upon the arrival of the Messenger of Allah's (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) letter with the like of that was:"
"566","What Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Dawud al-Baghdadi narrated to us; (he said:) Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Hashimi narrated to us; (he said:) Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us; (he said:) Salih ibn Kaysan and Ibn Akhi Ibn Shihab narrated to us, both from Ibn Shihab, from Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah, from Ibn Abbas: {that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent his letter to Kisra with Abd Allah ibn Hudhafa al-Sahmi, ordering him to hand it to the chief of Bahrayn. The chief of Bahrayn handed it to Kisra. When he read it, he tore it.} Ibn Shihab said: I think Ibn al-Musayyib said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated against them {that they be torn every tearing.}"
"567","And what Ibrahim ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us; (he said:) al-Uwaysi narrated to us; (he said:) Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah ibn Utba informed me, from Ibn Abbas — then he mentioned the like of it exactly. Ibn Abi Imran said: So their perishings were differed between in hastening one and delaying the other. And this interpretation, in our view, is likelier than the first; for in the first interpretation is the mention of Caesar's perishing — yet he has not perished; rather what was from him was his shifting with his kingdom from Syria to the place in which he now resides. And of what confirms also the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): {By the One in whose hand is my soul, their treasures will surely be spent in Allah's cause} — the treasure of Kisra has been spent in that, while the treasure of Caesar has not been spent in the like of it until now; but it will be spent in the future in the like of that — for the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is only from Allah, exalted, and Allah does not break the promise. And that is also confirmed by what is narrated from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning Caesar's perishing."
"568","As narrated to us by Ali ibn Ma'bad; (he said:) Mu'awiya ibn Amr al-Azdi narrated to us; (he said:) Za'ida ibn Qudama narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samura, from Nafi' ibn Utba ibn Abi Waqqas, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: {You will fight the Arabian Peninsula and Allah, exalted, conquers it; then you will fight Persia and Allah conquers it; then you will fight Rome and Allah conquers it; then you will fight the Dajjal and Allah conquers him.} Jabir said: And the Dajjal does not emerge until Rome emerges."
"569","And what Abu Umayya narrated to us; (he said:) Khalaf ibn al-Walid al-Lu'lu'i narrated to us; (he said:) Abu Ja'far al-Razi narrated to us — al-Tahawi said: his name is Isa ibn Mahan — from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samura, who said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: {You will raid the Arabian Peninsula and it is conquered for you; you will raid Persia and it is conquered for you; you will raid Rome and it is conquered for you; then the Dajjal.} He said: And he did not mention Nafi' ibn Utba. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed that the conquest of Rome, conjoined with the conquest of Kisra, had not been — and that it will be; and that its being, when it is, is like the being of Kisra's conquest which had been. And there is narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning its sign:"
"570","What Ahmad ibn Yahya ibn Yazid al-Suri Abu Abd Allah narrated to us; (he said:) al-Haytham ibn Jamil narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Thawban narrated to us, from his father, from Makhul, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Malik ibn Yukhamir, from Mu'adh, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: {The flourishing of Bayt al-Maqdis is the ruin of Yathrib; the ruin of Yathrib is the coming of the Malhama; the coming of the Malhama is the conquest of Constantinople; and the conquest of Constantinople is the emergence of the Dajjal. Then he struck my thigh — or the thigh of the one beside him, or his shoulder — then said: It is indeed true, as you are here.}"
"571","And what Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim al-Harawi narrated to us; (he said:) Ali ibn al-Ja'd al-Jawhari narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Thawban narrated to us, then he mentioned the like of it with his chain, except that he said: the presence of the Malhama, in place of: the coming of the Malhama. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed us of the meaning at which Caesar's perishing will be — until his perishing is like the perishing of Kisra which had been — so there is no Caesar after him until the Day of Resurrection, just as there is no Kisra after Kisra until the Day of Resurrection; and all lands are empty of each of them; and their treasures have been turned to what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said they would be spent in."
"572","Chapter explaining the difficulty in what is narrated from A'isha and Umm Salama, the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die until women were made lawful to him. 521 - Abd al-Ghani ibn Abi Aqil al-Lakhmi narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Uyayna narrated to us, from Amr, from Ata', from A'isha, who said: {The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die until women were made lawful to him.}"
"573","Ahmad ibn Dawud ibn Musa narrated to us; (he said:) Isma'il ibn Bakkar narrated to us; (he said:) Wuhayb ibn Khalid narrated to us; (he said:) Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from Ata', from Ubayd ibn Umayr, from A'isha, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not pass away until there was made lawful to him of women what he willed."
"574","And Harun ibn Muhammad al-Asqalani Abu Yazid licensed to me what was mentioned to me that he heard from al-Ala' — saying: Abu Asim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ata', from A'isha, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die until it was made lawful to him to marry of women what he willed. He said: I said: Who informed you this? He said: I think I heard it from Ubayd ibn Umayr. He said: And Abu al-Zubayr said: I heard a man informing Ata' of it."
"575","Ja'far ibn Sulayman ibn Muhammad al-Nawfali al-Hashimi Abu al-Qasim narrated to us; (he said:) Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us; (he said:) Umar ibn Abi Bakr al-Mawsili narrated to us; (he said:) al-Mughira ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Hizami narrated to me, from Abu al-Nadr, freedman of Umar ibn Ubayd Allah, from Abd Allah ibn Wahb ibn Zam'a, from Umm Salama, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die until it was made lawful to him to marry of women what he willed, except one of unmarriageable kin. And that is Allah's saying, exalted: {You may defer whom you will of them and take to you whom you will.} So in what we narrated from A'isha and Umm Salama is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die until women were made lawful to him. So we examined which women they were who had been forbidden to him until Allah made them lawful to him, upon what is in these two hadiths — and what is narrated from the predecessors concerning that."
"576","So we found Muhammad ibn Khuzaimah narrated to us, who said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us; (he said:) Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Dawud — and he is Ibn Abi Hind — from Muhammad ibn Abi Musa, from Ziyad ibn Abd Allah, who said: I asked Ubayy ibn Ka'b about these verses: {Not lawful to you are women after, nor to exchange them for wives, though their beauty pleases you.} He said: I said to him: Was it lawful for him to marry others than them? He said: Yes — and what harm in that? Allah, exalted, says: {O Prophet, We have made lawful to you your wives to whom you gave their dowries} — up to: {Not lawful to you are women after, nor to exchange them for wives.} He said: Not lawful to you is what is besides that of women — the mothers, sisters, and daughters. {We have known what We imposed upon them concerning their wives} — he said: the four women. He said: So this was impossible; for in it is that the women forbidden to him were the mothers, sisters, and daughters. And in the hadith of A'isha and Umm Salama which we narrated is that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die until women were made lawful to him — so we thereby understood that they are other than these."
"577","And Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us; (he said:) al-Firyabi narrated to us; (he said:) Warqa' narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning Allah's saying, exalted: {Not lawful to you are women after} — he said: No Christian woman, no Jewish woman, no disbelieving woman; nor to exchange the Muslim women for others of the Christians, Jews, and polytheists, {though their beauty pleases you, except what your right hand possesses.}"
"578","And al-Firyabi narrated to us; (he said:) Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid: {Not lawful to you are women after} — he said: the women of the People of the Book. And this too, in our view, is impossible; for had that been of what was made lawful to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whoever he married of the Jewish and Christian women would thereby return as mothers of the Muslims — by Allah's saying, exalted: {The Prophet is closer to the believers than themselves, and his wives are their mothers.}"
"579","And we found Ibn Khuzaimah narrated to us, who said: Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us; (he said:) Hammad ibn Salama narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Hasan, concerning His saying: {Not lawful to you are women after, nor to exchange them for wives} the verse — he said: Allah confined him to his nine wives whom he left at his death. Ali said: So I informed Ali ibn al-Husayn of that. He said: Indeed, it had been lawful for him to marry others than them."
"580","And we found Ja'far ibn Sulayman al-Hashimi al-Nawfali narrated to us, who said: Ibrahim ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us; (he said:) Umar ibn Abi Bakr al-Mawsili narrated to me; (he said:) Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Awn — and he is Abd al-Wahid — from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, concerning His saying: {Not lawful to you are women after.} He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was confined to his wives, not marrying after them; and they were confined to him."
"581","Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, al-Khasib ibn Nasih narrated to us, Sulayman ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to us, from Matar al-Warraq, from al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin, they both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only gave his wives a choice between this world and the Hereafter, so they chose Allah and the abode of the Hereafter, so Allah thanked them for that and confined him to them, saying: {No women are lawful to you after this, nor to exchange them for other wives}. This was plausible, except that it is subject to what we shall mention, God willing, in the remainder of this chapter."
"582","And we found that Ibn Marzuq narrated to us, he said: Habban ibn Hilal Abu Habib al-Muqri narrated to us, Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Abu Razin, regarding His saying, exalted is He: {And the daughters of your paternal uncles and the daughters of your paternal aunts and the daughters of your maternal uncles and the daughters of your maternal aunts}, he said: No women are lawful to you after this description, meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This was impossible in our view, because if it were so, there would not have been among his wives anyone outside this description, yet among them were those outside it: Zaynab bint Jahsh ibn Ri'ab, Juwayriyah daughter of al-Harith ibn Abi Dirar, Maymunah daughter of al-Harith, and Safiyyah daughter of Huyayy ibn Akhtab, and none of these fall under that description, for Zaynab, Juwayriyah and Maymunah were non-Qurashi Arabs with no maternal kinship to him, and Safiyyah was neither of Quraysh nor of the Arabs but of the People of the Book. Since these views we mentioned are impossible, nothing remains of what was said in interpreting this verse except what we narrated from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, and from al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin, that it means he would not marry other than his nine wives. Then an objector said: How can that be, when Allah confined him to them only as thanks for their choosing Allah and His Messenger and the Hereafter, so how may that be taken away from them? Our answer was that it is possible Allah granted them that as thanks for their choosing Allah and His Messenger and the Hereafter over this world, then permitted His Prophet after that to marry others, but he did not wish to do so and confined himself to them."
"583","Chapter 78: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the sneezing person whom he ordered to be answered with tashmit: which of the two sneezers is he? 525 - Muhammad ibn Amr al-Susi narrated to us, Asbat ibn Muhammad narrated to me, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Anas, he said: Two men sneezed before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he said tashmit for one of them and not for the other. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, two men sneezed and you said tashmit for one and not for the other. He said: This one praised Allah, and this one did not praise Allah, mighty and majestic is He."
"584","526 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sabiq narrated to us, Malik ibn Mighwal narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Anas, the same as it."
"585","527 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Salt narrated to us, Mansur ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from Abu Burdah, from Abu Musa, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded us that when a man sneezes and praises Allah we say tashmit for him, and when he does not praise Allah we do not say tashmit for him."
"586","528 - Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Ya'la ibn Ubayd narrated to us, Abu Munayn - who is Yazid ibn Kaysan - narrated to us, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man sneezed and praised Allah, so the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah have mercy on you. Then another sneezed and he was silent and said nothing to him. He said: O Messenger of Allah, this one sneezed and you said to him: May Allah have mercy on you, and I sneezed and you said nothing to me. He said: This one praised Allah and you were silent. Then an objector said: How do you accept this from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when you narrated from him... then he mentioned what we have narrated."
"587","529 - Yunus narrated to us, Bishr ibn Bakr informed me, al-Awza'i informed me, from Ibn Shihab, from Ibn al-Musayyib, that Abu Hurayrah said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The right of a Muslim upon a Muslim is five: returning the greeting of peace, visiting the sick, following funeral processions, accepting the invitation, and saying tashmit for the sneezer."
"588","530 - And Sulayman al-Kaysani narrated to us, Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us, al-Awza'i narrated to us, al-Zuhri narrated to me, Ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me, Abu Hurayrah narrated to me, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The right of a Muslim upon his Muslim brother is five: he greets him when he meets him, he says tashmit for him when he sneezes, he answers him when he invites him, he visits him when he is ill, and he attends his funeral when he dies."
"589","531 - And Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn An'um al-Ma'afiri informed me, from his father, that a mooring at sea brought them together with Abu Ayyub al-Ansari. When our lunch came we sent to Abu Ayyub and the people of his ship, and he said: You invited me while I am fasting, so it was my duty to answer you. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: A Muslim has six duties toward his brother: he answers him when he invites him, he greets him when he meets him, he says tashmit for him when he sneezes - or gives him to drink when he is thirsty, Yunus doubted - he visits him when he is ill, he attends him when he dies, and he advises him when he seeks his advice. He said: These two are contradictory, for one mentions saying tashmit when he sneezes, and the other mentions saying tashmit when he sneezes and praises Allah. Our answer, by Allah's guidance and help, is that they are not contradictory, for the meaning of his saying: and his saying tashmit when he sneezes, is upon saying tashmit when he sneezes and praises Allah, as we narrated at the beginning of this chapter. Similar is Allah's saying about expiations of oaths: {That is the expiation of your oaths when you swear, and guard your oaths}, where the intent is not merely when you swear but when you swear and break the oath, for there is no disagreement among the scholars that one who swears and does not break it owes no expiation. So just as that means when you swear and break it, it is not strange that his saying: he says tashmit when he sneezes, means when he sneezes and praises Allah. And in what we mentioned is what removes the contradiction imagined by this ignorant one in the hadith of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Allah grants success."
"590","Chapter 79: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about the truthfulness of Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him. 532 - Ibrahim ibn Ahmad ibn Marwan Abu Ishaq al-Wasiti narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Azraq al-Wasiti narrated to us, my grandfather Ishaq ibn Yusuf narrated to me, Sharik al-Nakha'i narrated to me, from al-A'mash, he said: I heard Abu Wa'il narrating, from Hallam ibn Jazl, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Neither the green sky has shaded nor the dusty earth has carried one with a tongue more truthful than Abu Dharr."
"591","533 - Fahd narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, al-A'mash narrated to us, from Uthman Abu al-Yaqzan, from Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Amr say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, then he mentioned the same as it."
"592","534 - Abu Umayyah narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Musa al-Ashyab narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Bilal ibn Abi al-Darda, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the same as it. We examined this hadith to determine the intended meaning: it reports that the green sky never shaded and the dusty earth never carried one with a tongue more truthful than Abu Dharr. In our view, Allah knows best, this means he, may Allah be pleased with him, was at the highest rank of truthfulness, without negating that there were among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) those equal to him in truthfulness. What is affirmed is the highest rank of truthfulness for Abu Dharr, without negating others from that rank; it only negates anyone being at a rank of truthfulness higher than it. And Allah we ask for success."
"593","Chapter 80: Explanation of the difficulty in what was narrated from him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about one who wakes up in a state of major impurity on a day of Ramadan: does he fast that day or not? 535 - Yunus narrated to us, Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Sumayy, freedman of Abu Bakr, that he heard Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman say: I and my father were with Marwan ibn al-Hakam, the governor of Madinah, when it was mentioned that Abu Hurayrah says: Whoever wakes up in a state of major impurity breaks the fast that day. Marwan said: I adjure you to go to the Mother of the Believers Aishah and Umm Salamah and ask them about that. Abd al-Rahman went and I went with him until we entered upon Aishah. Abd al-Rahman greeted her, then said: O Mother of the Believers, we were with Marwan and it was mentioned to him that Abu Hurayrah says: Whoever wakes up in a state of major impurity breaks the fast that day. Aishah said: Evil is what Abu Hurayrah said. O Abd al-Rahman, do you turn away from what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do? He said: No, by Allah. She said: I bear witness that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wake up in a state of major impurity from intercourse, not from a wet dream, then fast that day. Then we went out and entered upon Umm Salamah and asked her about that, and she said the same as Aishah said. We went out to Marwan and Abd al-Rahman told him what they said. Marwan said: I adjure you, O Abu Muhammad, to ride my mount, for it is at the door, and go to Abu Hurayrah, for he is at his land at al-Aqiq, and inform him of that. Abd al-Rahman rode and I rode with him until we came to Abu Hurayrah. Abd al-Rahman spoke with him a while, then mentioned that to him, and Abu Hurayrah said: I have no knowledge of that; an informant only told it to me."
"594","536 - al-Hasan ibn Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Marwazi narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, Abdullah ibn Abi Salamah, freedman of Banu Taym, narrated to us, from Irak ibn Malik al-Ghifari - and al-Nu'man ibn Abi Ayyash al-Ansari, then al-Zuraqi, each of them said: Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham al-Makhzumi narrated to me, he said: I sat with Abu Hurayrah and a man asked him about a fasting person who wakes up in a state of major impurity, and Abu Hurayrah said to him: He has no fast. Abu Bakr said: I mentioned that to my father Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith, and my father mentioned it to Marwan ibn al-Hakam, the governor of Madinah. Marwan said to him: You must go to Aishah and Umm Salamah, the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and ask them about this matter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for none knows this matter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) better than his wives. My father went out and I went out with him until we entered upon Umm Salamah and asked her about that, and she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wake up in a state of major impurity from intercourse, not from a wet dream, then fast. Then we went out from her and sat at Aishah's door, and my father sent her freedman Dhakwan to her to ask her about that. Dhakwan came to him and said: She says to you: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wake up in a state of major impurity from intercourse, not from a wet dream, then fast. My father returned to Marwan and told him that, and he said: I insist that you go to Abu Hurayrah and inform him of this. My father said to him: May Allah forgive you, O Amir, I conveyed to you a hadith from a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then when you found otherwise you ordered me to inform him of it. Marwan said to him: I insist that you do it. Then Marwan went out for Hajj or Umrah, and we went out with him until we were at Dhu al-Hulayfah - and Abu Hurayrah had land there where he was - so we stood before him, I with my father, and my father said to him: O Abu Hurayrah, I told the Amir that you said: Whoever reaches dawn in a state of major impurity has no fast, so he ordered me to ask the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about that, and I did. Umm Salamah and Aishah told me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wake up in a state of major impurity from intercourse, not from a wet dream, then fast. Abu Hurayrah said: I do not know; al-Fadl ibn Abbas told me that."
"595","537 - And al-Hasan ibn Bakr narrated to us, Ya'qub narrated to us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, al-Harith ibn Abi Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham narrated to me, from his father, the same as the hadith of Abdullah ibn Abi Salamah, from Irak and al-Nu'man."
"596","538 - Ali ibn Shaybah narrated to us, Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Awn informed us, from Raja ibn Haywah, from Ya'la ibn Uqbah, he said: I woke up in a state of major impurity while intending to fast, so I came to Abu Hurayrah and asked him, and he said to me: Break the fast. I came to Marwan and asked him and told him what Abu Hurayrah said, so he sent Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith to Aishah to ask her, and she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to come out for the dawn prayer with his head dripping from intercourse, then fast that day. He returned to Marwan and told him, and he said: Go to Abu Hurayrah and inform him. He came to him and informed him, and he said: I did not hear it from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); al-Fadl only narrated it to me, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"597","539 - And Ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us, Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Awn informed us, then he mentioned with its chain the same as it. In these reports we narrated is what Abu Hurayrah mentioned from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), prohibiting one who wakes up in a state of major impurity from fasting, and in them is the report of Aishah and Umm Salamah contradicting that prohibition. An objector said: How can you incline to what Aishah and Umm Salamah narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and abandon what Abu Hurayrah narrated from al-Fadl, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), without declaring both sound, making the hadith of Aishah and Umm Salamah a report about his own ruling, and the hadith of al-Fadl about the ruling of others of his community, so neither meaning contradicts the other? Our answer was that we found evidence that his own ruling in that was like the ruling of the rest of his community, namely:"
"598","540 - Yunus narrated to us, he said: Ibn Wahb informed us that Malik informed him, from Abdullah ibn Mu'mar al-Ansari, from Abu Yunus, freedman of Aishah, from Aishah: A man said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was standing at the door, and I was listening: O Messenger of Allah, I wake up in a state of major impurity while intending to fast. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I too wake up in a state of major impurity while intending to fast, then I bathe and fast. The man said: You are not like us; Allah has forgiven you your past and future sins. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) became angry and said: By Allah, I hope I am the most God-fearing of you toward Allah, exalted is He, and the most knowledgeable of you of what I guard against. When we established thereby the equality of his ruling and the ruling of the rest of his community in that, we understood that those two meanings were two rulings of Allah, exalted is He, one abrogating the other. What is in the hadith of al-Fadl was the strict ruling, and what is in the hadith of Aishah and Umm Salamah was the lenient ruling. We mentioned earlier in this book that abrogation without disobedience to Allah is a mercy from Allah, returning strictness to leniency, and there was, praise be to Allah, in nothing of what caused this abrogation any disobedience entailing strictness. So we held that the abrogation in this ruling was from strictness to leniency, entailing the obligation to act upon what is in the hadith of Aishah and Umm Salamah rather than what is in the hadith of al-Fadl. Moreover, we found the Book of Allah requires that, in His saying: {Intercourse with your wives is permitted to you on the night of fasting}, up to His saying: {until the night}. This shows the permissibility of approaching women at night until dawn, while the bath it necessitates can only be in the daytime, which permits fasting with major impurity, agreeing with the hadith of Aishah and Umm Salamah from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). And among what was narrated from him also, from the hadith of Aishah and Umm Salamah agreeing with this meaning:"
"599","541 - Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Dawud and Rawh ibn Ubadah narrated to us, they said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from al-Hakam, he said: I heard Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham narrating, from his father, he said: I entered upon Aishah, wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and she told me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to wake up in a state of major impurity, then bathe, then go out to the mosque with his head dripping, then fast that day. I told Marwan of it and he said: Go to Abu Hurayrah and inform him of that. I said: He is a friend of mine, so excuse me. He said: I insist that you go to him. I set out, I and my son, to Abu Hurayrah and told him of that, and Abu Hurayrah said: Aishah knows better than me. Shu'bah said: And in the written copy: Aishah knows the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) better."
"600","542 - And Ali ibn Ma'bad narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata narrated to us, Dawud ibn Abi Hind informed us, from al-Sha'bi, from Umar ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his brother Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman, that he used to fast and not break it. One day he entered upon his father while breaking the fast, and he said to him: What is the matter with you breaking the fast today? He said: Major impurity befell me and I did not bathe until morning, so Abu Hurayrah ruled that I break the fast. They sent to Aishah asking her, and she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would be struck by major impurity, then bathe after morning, then come out with his head dripping water and lead his Companions in prayer, then fast that day."
